stname': 'Tracy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b0676cc-642d-510c-ab65-33a3633b74e8', 'word_count': 1434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b0676cc-642d-510c-ab65-33a3633b74e8'}, {'abstract': '“A Shared Legacy: Folk Art in America,” at the American Folk Art Museum, includes more than 60 choice objects made between 1800 and 1920.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/02/arts/a-shared-legacy-at-the-american-folk-art-museum.html', 'snippet': '“A Shared Legacy: Folk Art in America,” at the American Folk Art Museum, includes more than 60 choice objects made between 1800 and 1920.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a letter he wrote in 1825, the American painter John Vanderlyn said that paintings by the itinerant portraitist Ammi Phillips were “... cheap and slight, for the mass of folks can’t judge of the merits of a well-finished picture.” Vanderlyn had gone to France for academic training. His masterpiece, “Ariadne Asleep on the Island of Naxos” (1809-14), a suavely erotic, neo-Classical vision of a nude woman dozing in a pastoral landscape, is one of the gems of the collection of the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts. But a discerning viewer today might well prefer Phillips. His “Girl in Red Dress With Cat and Dog” (1830-35) is one of the loveliest paintings by any American artist. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Simple, but Oh, What Depths', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Simple, but Oh, What Depths', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN FOLK ART MUSEUM', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hicks, Edward', 'rank': 5, 'maj