was 94.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/us/sub-goodlad-obit/sub-goodlad-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 577, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/02/us/sub-goodlad-obit/sub-goodlad-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 577}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/us/sub-goodlad-obit/sub-goodlad-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 985, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/us/sub-goodlad-obit/sub-goodlad-obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1970, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/us/sub-goodlad-obit/sub-goodlad-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/02/us/sub-goodlad-obit/sub-goodlad-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/us/sub-goodlad-obit/sub-goodlad-obit-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'John I. Goodlad, Progressive Educator, Dies at 94', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'John I. Goodlad, Progressive Educator, Dies at 94', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Place Called School (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Washington', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of California, Los Angeles', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodlad, John I (1920-2014)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-01T21:44:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamar Lewin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b5ad03a-2cdb-5af7-bd82-a79589e02ebe', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b5ad03a-2cdb-5af7-bd82-a79589e02ebe'}, {'abstract': 'Veterans of the conflict saw the gridiron as a perfect training ground for soldier-scholars.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/01/how-the-civil-war-created-college-football/', 'snippet': 'Veterans of the conflict saw the gridiron as a perfect training ground for soldier-scholars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At a ceremony in Cambridge, Mass., on June 10, 1890, the philanthropist Henry Lee Higginson declared, “I ask to make [Soldiers Field] a memorial to some dear friends who gave their lives … to their country and to their fellow men in the hour of great need — the War of the Rebellion.” The 31 acres of marshlands and pasture that Higginson donated to Harvard College, his alma mater, would serve as the site of the country’s oldest concrete football arena, Harvard Stadium, built over a decade later in 1903. As he memorialized the Civil War dead, the Union veteran addressed a group of 400 male students and alumni, most of whom were too young to have experienced and learned from the horrors of battle during the nation’s bloodiest war. Like Higginson, however, many late-19th-century Americans saw a deep connection between the battlefield and the athletic field, believing that collegiate athletics, including football, could teach the next generation their “own duties as men and citizens of the Republic” and train them to manage “the burden of carrying on this country in the best way.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Civil War Created College Football', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-01T21:51:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Brickell Bellows', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': 'Brickell', 'lastname': 'Bellows', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03292763-93e2-5e4f-9829-9f8090c44fa8', 'word_count': 1136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03292763-93e2-5e4f-9829-9f8090c44fa8'}, {'abstract': 'The second season of the Scandinavian series “The Bridge,” online at Hulu on Friday, begins with a cargo ship mysteriously veering off course.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/02/arts/television/after-a-remake-for-american-viewers-back-to-understatement-.html', 'snippet': 'The second season of the Scandinavian series “The Bridge,” online at Hulu on Friday, begins with a cargo ship mysteriously veering off course.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The police drama “The Bridge,” in both its original (Scandinavian) and American versions, took its title from its evocative opening gimmick: A body is found on a bridge that crosses an international border, lying half in one country and half in the other. The result is an unlikely partnership between detectives from two countries, Sweden and Denmark in the original, the United States and Mexico in the remake.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/SUBBRIDGE3/SUBBRIDGE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 521, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/SUBBRIDGE3/SUBBRIDGE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 521, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/SUBBRIDGE3/SUBBRIDGE3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/SUBBRIDGE3/SUBBRIDGE3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/SUBBRIDGE3/SUBBRIDGE3-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After a Remake for American Viewers, Back to Understatement', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After a Remake for American Viewers, Back to Understatement', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hulu.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scandinavia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-01T21:56:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/844a63d6-6c18-5c86-95a5-071ccfd3135b', 'word_count': 472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/844a63d6-6c18-5c86-95a5-071ccfd3135b'}, {'abstract': 'A collection featuring items from the pianist Blind Tom will be up for auction, as will a stock photo agency’s inventory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/02/arts/recalling-blind-tom-an-autistic-pianist-born-to-slavery.html', 'snippet': 'A collection featuring items from the pianist Blind Tom will be up for auction, as will a stock photo agency’s inventory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The pianist Blind Tom began drawing crowds to his performances as a child in the 1850s. His black family and various white guardians fought for rights to earn money from his career until his death in 1908. Blind Tom himself, who was born into slavery and given a series of last names over the years, was autistic and unable to fathom the legal battles.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/02ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/02ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 356}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/02ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 608, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/02ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1068, 'width': 1799, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/02ANTIQUES1/02ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/02ANTIQUES1/02ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/02ANTIQUES1/02ANTIQUES1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Recalling Blind Tom, an Autistic Pianist Born to Slavery ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Recalling Blind Tom, an Autistic Pianist Born to Slaver