'pub_date': '2015-01-02T13:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Penny Prince', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Penny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prince', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10ab94d5-c32b-569c-8414-1470eeb56fbd', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10ab94d5-c32b-569c-8414-1470eeb56fbd'}, {'abstract': 'Officials said that they had identified three of the bodies, but that stormy seas had prevented them from deploying sophisticated sonar equipment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/03/world/asia/bodies-search-airasia-flight-8501-plane.html', 'snippet': 'Officials said that they had identified three of the bodies, but that stormy seas had prevented them from deploying sophisticated sonar equipment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SURABAYA, Indonesia — An Indonesian official said four major pieces of the Airbus A320-200 jetliner that plunged into the Java Sea last Sunday had been found. The wreckage was located by sonar equipment, but divers had not yet been able to examine it because of high waves and perilous currents, according to the National Search and Rescue Agency.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Four Big Pieces of AirAsia Jet Are Found; Search Crews Have Recovered 46 Bodies', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Four Big Pieces of AirAsia Jet Are Found; Search Crews Have Recovered 46 Bodies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AIRASIA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aviation Accidents, Safety and Disasters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indonesia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AirAsia Flight 8501', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T13:33:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom McCawley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCawley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13e6ab38-802d-5a1f-b4fb-f067c8d317bd', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13e6ab38-802d-5a1f-b4fb-f067c8d317bd'}, {'abstract': 'The Animal Collective musician shares some influences with T in anticipation of the Jan. 13 release of his new album, “Panda Bear Meets the Grim Reaper.”', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/02/panda-bear-noah-lennox-interview/', 'snippet': 'The Animal Collective musician shares some influences with T in anticipation of the Jan. 13 release of his new album, “Panda Bear Meets the Grim Reaper.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A name is just a name, even the not-so-vaguely menacing title “Panda Bear Meets the Grim Reaper.”\xa0In fact, the fifth album from Panda Bear – the singer-songwriter also known as Noah Lennox – shows the Animal Collective member’s purported encounter with the afterlife to have been quite productive. As was the case with Lennox’s 2011 release “Tomboy,” the new album transforms experimental techniques into moody yet uplifting pop music that somehow evokes both a dank basement rave and the sunny sounds of the Beach Boys.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/t-magazine/02panda-miller/02panda-miller-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/201