', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T15:16:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christoph Niemann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christoph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Niemann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27499a2f-fc9a-537c-9b18-5a328663e9a2', 'word_count': 25, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27499a2f-fc9a-537c-9b18-5a328663e9a2'}, {'abstract': 'In a time when couples overshare, we could use some cautionary tales about all that they miss.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/magazine/the-split-screen-marriage.html', 'snippet': 'In a time when couples overshare, we could use some cautionary tales about all that they miss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The other day, just off the train, I pulled out my phone and texted my wife: “just off the train.” A few minutes later, standing in line at the grocery store, I decided that this moment, too, was not without import, and so out came my phone again: “in line at store.” My wife, throughout the day, contributed reporting of her own: “heading into gym”; “forgot my umbrella”; “wait, have umbrella.” When we reunited that evening, we were — like so many couples who spend their days smiling faintly at their phones — as well informed of each other’s doings as if we had each hired a private investigator to trail the other.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/magazine/04riff/04mag-04riff.t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 121, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/magazine/04riff/04mag-04riff.t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 121}}, {'