': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T15:21:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4e9f930-e966-50cd-89ce-941eb5ed316e', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4e9f930-e966-50cd-89ce-941eb5ed316e'}, {'abstract': 'Sign? Dine? Fine?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/magazine/d-list-doppelgnger.html', 'snippet': 'Sign? Dine? Fine?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I share a highly uncommon name with a very minor celebrity who hasn’t worked much in decades. A fan, mistaking me for the celebrity, sent me a note offering praise for his fine work several decades ago and asked for an autograph on an enclosed publicity photo. I’ve had a few such misunderstandings over the years and have come to learn that the celebrity is impossible to locate. The fan’s note was quite endearing, and I am tempted to simply sign the photo and thank the fan for the support. I’d be claiming to be someone I’m not, which is deceitful, but the fan would probably be happier with that than with the truth. Does the charity of such an act outweigh the deception? NAME WITHHELD', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 745, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 745}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 825, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '