world; Sven Beckert talks about “Empire of Cotton”; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Parul Sehgal is the host, filling in for Pamela Paul.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside The New York Times Book Review: Charles D’Ambrosio’s ‘Loitering’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T15:57:42+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/ef9753ad-4348-59ff-80af-ef43f0e0427d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/ef9753ad-4348-59ff-80af-ef43f0e0427d'}, {'abstract': 'Jessica Chastain says she modeled her ambitious wife on the vice president, while Oscar Isaac says his businessman husband is based on a particular performance by the singer.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/02/how-dick-cheney-and-marvin-gaye-inspired-a-most-violent-year/', 'snippet': 'Jessica Chastain says she modeled her ambitious wife on the vice president, while Oscar Isaac says his businessman husband is based on a particular performance by the singer.', 'lead_paragraph': ' While the title “A Most Violent Year” suggests, as Vanity Fair’s Richard Lawson put it, “a picturesque orgy of gangster violence,” the film instead tells a quieter story about a heating oil (!) salesman with bourgeois aspirations and industry enemies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/violentyear/violentyear-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/02/arts/violentyear/violentyear-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'ju