jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmitt, Eric-Emmanuel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bronsky, Alina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Curtis, Howard (1949- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McLean, Anne', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mohr, Tim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison McCulloch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCulloch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1443acf-a247-58b1-ba55-681cf3fd346e', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1443acf-a247-58b1-ba55-681cf3fd346e'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Todd’s latest historical mystery opens on the day the Austrian archduke and his wife are assassinated in Sarajevo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/a-fine-summers-day-by-charles-todd-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Charles Todd’s latest historical mystery opens on the day the Austrian archduke and his wife are assassinated in Sarajevo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Picking up one of Charles Todd’s post-World War I historical mysteries is like starting off on an uncertain journey. In each book, Inspector Ian Rutledge of Scotland Yard, a shellshocked veteran of the Great War, makes his solitary way to some provincial English town, ostensibly to assist the local constabulary with a baffling crime but also to bear witness to the incalculable devastation brought about by the fighting. In 16 previous novels, the authors (a mother and son who write under a pen name) have sent their haunted hero all over the country, taking stock of the terrible desolation he sees everywhere and knowing that true justice is beyond his powers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/010414/010414-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 761, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/010414/010414-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 761}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/010414/010414-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 808, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/010414/010414-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1616, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/010414/010414-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/010414/010414-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/010414/010414-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Eve of Destruction', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eve of Destruction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Todd, Charles (Author)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Thomas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hill, Susan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hogan, Phil (1955- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/65fae21a-dd4a-5d20-b9c8-c416004c6d61', 'word_count': 909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65fae21a-dd4a-5d20-b9c8-c416004c6d61'}, {'abstract': 'In a logging town on the Pacific Coast, a family grapples with violence and tragedy at the turn of the 20th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/the-bully-of-order-by-brian-hart.html', 'snippet': 'In a logging town on the Pacific Coast, a family grapples with violence and tragedy at the turn of the 20th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the opening pages of his second novel, “The Bully of Order,” Brian Hart warns the reader of the harshness to come. “This is war but bloodier,” one voice tells another. “Nah,” the other replies, “I seen war and this is darling compared.” Those brave enough to forge ahead are soon faced with a fond recollection of a sexual act with a steer’s heart and a story that culminates in a dog’s hanging itself, among other atrocities. On Hart’s late-19th-century Pacific Coast, nothing stays clean for long, literally or spiritually: “White here is brave and temporary, steam in the windows, ice in the pan.” Violence is a way to pass the time, and time is just counting down to death. Hopes, when a character dares to have any, are shattered into pieces, those pieces burned and the ashes tossed into the ocean. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/sub-04SCOTT/sub-04SCOTT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/sub-04SCOTT/sub-04SCOTT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/sub-04SCOTT/sub-04SCOTT-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/sub-04SCOTT/sub-04SCOTT-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1389, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/sub-04SCOTT/sub-04scott-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/sub-04SCOTT/sub-04scott-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/sub-04SCOTT/sub-04scott-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Bully of Order,’ by Brian Hart', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wild, Wild Northwest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hart, Brian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c51ecd3-b99b-5eac-8b9f-d0be9dffdee0', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c51ecd3-b99b-5eac-8b9f-d0be9dffdee0'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DYING EVERY DAY: Seneca at the Court of Nero,  by James Romm. (Vintage, $17.95.) Romm adeptly unravels the enigma of the great Stoic philosopher Seneca, who watched his student Nero spiral into madness as Roman emperor — a descent that saw family murders, the apocalyptic fire of A.D. 64 and a savage purging of the supreme minds of the Imperial Senate’s golden age.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/2243c16f-241c-5951-9ad4-768f8886fb10', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2243c16f-241c-5951-9ad4-768f8886fb10'}, {'abstract': 'A look ahead to new novels by Toni Morrison, Jona