b92-a04e-8c8661bcaa7e'}, {'abstract': 'What is the proper role of government? A history of the Republican Party traces its responses to that question.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/to-make-men-free-by-heather-cox-richardson.html', 'snippet': 'What is the proper role of government? A history of the Republican Party traces its responses to that question.', 'lead_paragraph': 'America does not have a broken political system. It has a broken political party: the Republicans.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/0RAUCH/04rauch-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 839, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/0RAUCH/04rauch-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 839}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/0RAUCH/04rauch-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 732, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/0RAUCH/04rauch-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1464, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/0RAUCH/04rauch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/0RAUCH/04rauch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/0RAUCH/04rauch-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘To Make Men Free,’ by Heather Cox Richardson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Abe Lincoln to Ted Cruz', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richardson, Heather Cox', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Rauch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rauch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0302c1d-74ce-5530-a203-a8246a52c58d', 'word_count': 1297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0302c1d-74ce-5530-a203-a8246a52c58d'}, {'abstract': 'Spies, terrorism, adultery, guns — Brock Clarke’s novel sets this combustible mix in remote upstate New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/the-happiest-people-in-the-world-by-brock-clarke.html', 'snippet': 'Spies, terrorism, adultery, guns — Brock Clarke’s novel sets this combustible mix in remote upstate New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the center of this new novel from the author of “An Arsonist’s Guide to Writers’ Homes in New England” and “Exley” is a high school guidance counselor named Henry. Well, not really. His name isn’t Henry, it’s Jens, and he isn’t a high school guidance counselor, he’s a Danish cartoonist on the run from terrorists. But he’s not one of the Danish cartoonists you’re thinking of, the ones who satirized the Muslim world with their representations of Muhammad. Rather, he’s a different Danish cartoonist, one who drew a cartoon about the other cartoonists, and whose cartoonist cartoon proves to be just as controversial as the original cartoonists’ cartoons. Anyway, for the purposes of this paragraph, let’s just say he’s a high school guidance counselor in Broomeville, N.Y., a cold and isolated upstate town. Although, really, he’s one of two guidance counselors there. The first one has been fired for being a drunk, but actually she’s been fired so that the principal of the school, Matty, who is sort of the protagonist — well, wait, he’s not really the protagonist, per se, he’s one of several people who function, at various times, as protagonists — but as I was saying, Matty has fired the original guidance counselor in order to do a favor for his ex-lover, the wildlife researcher, which is to hire Henry, who is actually Jens. Because the ex-lover, whose name is Locs but really it’s Lorraine, isn’t a wildlife researcher anymore, she’s a C.I.A. agent. And she is trying to protect the cartoonist-of-cartoonists-cum-guidance-counselor by hiding him in Broomeville from the terrorists, who are actually, it turns out, just this one guy, who, it also turns out, is not really a terrorist. But, it further turns out, she’s just trying to find a way to get Matty (whom she calls Matthew) back, though she never really had him in the first place.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04LENNON/04lennon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04LENNON/04lennon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04LENNON/04lennon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04LENNON/04lennon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04LENNON/04lennon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04LENNON/04lennon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04LENNON/04lennon-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Happiest People in the World,’ by Brock Clarke', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Our Man in Broomeville', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lennon, J Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarke, Brock', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Happiest People in the World (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. Robert Lennon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'Robert', 'lastname': 'Lennon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/465cdb92-70e4-54cc-8a37-f89e45c7b74a', 'word_count': 1200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/465cdb92-70e4-54cc-8a37-f89e45c7b74a'}, {'abstract': 'The lives and experiences of three generations of slaves on a Caribbean sugar estate and a Southern plantation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/a-tale-of-two-plantations-by-richard-s-dunn.html', 'snippet': 'The lives and experiences of three generations of slaves on a Caribbean sugar estate and a Southern plantation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For enslaved peoples in the New World, it was always the worst of times. Whether captured in Africa or born into bondage in the Americas, slaves suffered unimaginable torments and indignities. Yet the specific form their miseries took, as the historian Richard S. Dunn shows in his painstakingly researched “A Tale of Two Plantations: Slave Life and Labor in Jamaica and Virginia,” depended on whether one was a slave in the British Caribbean or in the United States. The contrasts between the two slave societies were many, covering family life, religious beliefs and labor practices. But one difference overrode all others. In the Caribbean, white masters treated the slaves like “disposable cogs in a machine,” working them to death on sugar plantations and then replacing them with fresh stock from Africa. In the United States, white masters treated their slaves like the machine itself — a breeding machine.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04GRANDIN/04GRANDIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04GRANDIN/04GRANDIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04GRANDIN/04GRANDIN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04GRANDIN/04GRANDIN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1435, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04GRANDIN/04GRANDIN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04GRANDIN/04GRANDIN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04GRANDIN/04GRANDIN-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Tale of Two Plantations,’ by Richard S. Dunn', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Born, Bought, Sold', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grandin, Greg', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunn, Richard S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery (Historical)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Grandin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grandin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/11a96958-34e3-5b5d-949e-8b0cac17e480', 'word_count': 1196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11a96958-34e3-5b5d-949e-8b0cac17e480'}, {'abstract': 'Two hardened men — a Montana deputy and a killer awaiting trial — forge an uneasy friendship in this debut novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/the-ploughmen-by-kim-zupan.html', 'snippet': 'Two hardened men — a Montana deputy and a killer awaiting trial — forge an uneasy friendship in this debut novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some protagonists crave a perch in history. Others hope merely to survive with dignity. The lead characters of Kim Zupan’s first novel, “The Ploughmen,” occupy opposite ends of this spectrum, but share two critical traits: They cannot sleep, and the source of their insomnia is their terrible familiarity with death.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HAGY/0104-bks-HAGY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HAGY/0104-bks-HAGY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HAGY/0104-bks-HAGY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HAGY/0104-bks-HAGY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HAGY/0104-bks-HAGY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HAGY/0104-bks-HAGY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HAGY/0104-bks-HAGY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Ploughmen,’ by Kim Zupan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where the Bodies Are Buried', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hagy, Alyson', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zupan, Kim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alyson Hagy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alyson', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hagy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b91ef73-d821-5949-81b6-97527d088594', 'word_count': 577, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b91ef73-d821-5949-81b6-97527d088594'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SUSPENDED SENTENCES: Three Novellas,  by Patrick Modiano. Translated by Mark Polizzotti. (Yale University, paper, $16.) These novellas offer as good a place as any to enter the long, slow-moving river of the French Nobelist’s fiction.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/17771df0-1158-5c8f-850c-7fedae31e935', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17771df0-1158-5c8f-850c-7fedae31e935'}, {'abstract': 'A historian portrays Coca-Cola as an international public health burden, profiting from other nations’ infrastructures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/citizen-coke-by-bartow-j-elmore.html', 'snippet': 'A historian portrays Coca-Cola as an international public health burden, profiting from other nations’ infrastructures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Halfway into “Citizen Coke,” Bartow J. Elmore describes taking a treacherous journey through an Indian jungle in 2010. He was searching for a Coke bottling plant that had been shut down six years earlier after villagers claimed it had parched their wells and polluted their streams, causing skin and stomach diseases. Thirsty and tired, he stumbled upon a restaurant in a secluded village where the only safe thing to drink was . . . Coca-Cola. “It seemed I had become trapped in the history I had come to investigate,” he writes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04MACY/04macy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04MACY/04macy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04MACY/04macy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04MACY/04macy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1249, 'width': 1936, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04MACY/04macy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04MACY/04macy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04MACY/04macy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Citizen Coke,’ by Bartow J. Elmore', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The World Buys a Coke', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Macy, Beth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elmore, Bartow J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coca-Cola Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Beth Macy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/586df0e6-f189-549d-a7eb-ee3d0d5f538b', 'word_count': 1181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/586df0e6-f189-549d-a7eb-ee3d0d5f538b'}, {'abstract': 'A powerful friendship between two lads illuminates family and community ties in Jane Gardam’s linked stories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/jane-gardams-hollow-land.html', 'snippet': 'A powerful friendship between two lads illuminates family and community ties in Jane Gardam’s linked stories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just as devotees of certain television shows dream of finding lost episodes and Beatles fans imagine opening a box and fishing out recordings of forgotten studio sessions, so readers long for ways to extend the pleasure they receive from beloved writers. In this regard Jane Gardam has been more than accommodating. After American readers began to embrace the British writer’s novel “Old Filth,” released here in 2006, they learned that Gardam had been publishing fine fiction regularly since the 1970s. Her backlist is extraordinary and deep, populated by titles published only in Britain as well as ones that also appeared in America but had essentially been hiding in plain sight, the way books sometimes do when they don’t find their audience.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04WOLITZERSUB/0104-bks-WOLITZER-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04WOLITZERSUB/0104-bks-WOLITZER-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 638}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04WOLITZERSUB/0104-bks-WOLITZER-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 963, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04WOLITZERSUB/0104-bks-WOLITZER-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1926, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04WOLITZERSUB/04WOLITZERSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04WOLITZERSUB/04WOLITZERSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04WOLITZERSUB/04WOLITZERSUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jane Gardam’s ‘Hollow Land’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Boys’ Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolitzer, Meg', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardam, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T16:38:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meg Wolitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/240c3dce-017d-5367-8406-4fe8c4afdb59', 'word_count': 1243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/240c3dce-017d-5367-8406-4fe8c4afdb59'}, {'abstract': 'Foreign visitors in Sri Lanka unhesitantly spill their secrets in front of a minivan driver.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/books/review/noontide-toll-by-romesh-gunesekera.html', 'snippet': 'Foreign visitors in Sri Lanka unhesitantly spill their secrets in front of a minivan driver.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This collection of linked stories, set in post-civil-war, post-tsunami Sri Lanka, is narrated by a retired clerk who has sunk his savings into a minibus and now hires himself out as a driver. Each story traces a different journey, with passengers that include three Russians on their way to a new luxury spa; a Roman Catholic priest and his acolyte pursuing a kind of detective mission; a Czech couple who share a walk on a beach with a night watchman whose entire family perished, along with more than 30,000 other Sri Lankans, in the 2004 tsunami; a delegation of Chinese businessmen appraising vast “junk fields” of military scrap; and a Sri Lankan general (also a renowned ballroom dancer) en route to a meeting with a woman whose family he has all but destroyed. The 14 stories in “Noontide Toll” function like the stages of a tour through a place that’s trying to emerge from the ruins, yet is still “trapped by the past despite the prospects ahead.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HEIGHTON/04heighton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 904, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HEIGHTON/04heighton-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 904}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/books/review/04HEIGHTON/04heighton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 680, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'