'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T20:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c09cc2fd-19a8-5983-ae53-f7a631206121', 'word_count': 1032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c09cc2fd-19a8-5983-ae53-f7a631206121'}, {'abstract': 'The Royal Danish Ballet will perform the work of the 19th-century Romantic August Bournonville in a program of excerpts at the Joyce Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/arts/dance/royal-danish-ballet-brings-bournonville-back-to-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'The Royal Danish Ballet will perform the work of the 19th-century Romantic August Bournonville in a program of excerpts at the Joyce Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than half a century, Americans have felt a kind of fascination for Danish dancers. The attraction dates at least to the 1950s, when a small group from the Royal Danish Ballet traveled to the Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival in Becket, Mass., for the first time. At those performances, audiences and critics were charmed by the company’s buoyant dancing, amiable stage manner and seemingly natural acting abilities. The most celebrated ballets were by August Bournonville, a 19th-century choreographer in the Romantic vein who has come to define the Danish style. An ensemble of 13 dancers from the company, led by the principal dancer Ulrik Birkkjaer, will perform a program of Bournonville excerpts at the Joyce Theater in Manhattan from Jan. 13 to 18.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/arts/04ROYAL1/04ROYAL1