argis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/395a9a38-4f8d-5331-8635-785a2dec209c', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/395a9a38-4f8d-5331-8635-785a2dec209c'}, {'abstract': 'A small group helped the football organization with the “No More” public-service announcements against domestic abuse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/style/the-team-behind-the-nfls-no-more-campaign.html', 'snippet': 'A small group helped the football organization with the “No More” public-service announcements against domestic abuse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This National Football League season got off to a disastrous off-the-field start. In early September, TMZ published a video of Baltimore Ravens running back Ray Rice punching his fiancée (and now wife), Janay Palmer, in an elevator. Days later, Minnesota Vikings running back Adrian Peterson was charged with child abuse for striking his son with a tree branch. The Associated Press named the N.F.L.’s domestic violence problem the top sports story of 2014, ahead of Donald Sterling’s racist comments and LeBron James’s return to Cleveland.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04NOMORESUB01/04NOMORESUB01-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04NOMORESUB01/04NOMORESUB01-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 448}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04NOMORESUB01/04NOMORESUB01-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 765, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04NOMORESUB01/04NOMORESUB01-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1042, 'width': 1395, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04NOMORESUB01/04NOMORESUB01-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04NOMORESUB01/04NOMORESUB01-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04NOMORESUB01/04NOMORESUB01-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Team Behind the N.F.L.’s ‘No More’ Campaign', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Small Team Behind ‘No More’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'No More', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koblin, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Violence', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-02T22:44:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Koblin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koblin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c87fb98d-0c41-58ed-86cc-f23894e06ab5', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c87fb98d-0c41-58ed-86cc-f23894e06ab5'}, {'abstract': 'The “in case you missed it” sentiment has a way of haunting us. In the Internet age, there is simply too much readable, viewable and listenable data for anyone to stay abreast of.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/04/style/the-unending-anxiety-of-an-icymi-world.html', 'snippet': 'The “in case you missed it” sentiment has a way of haunting us. In the Internet age, there is simply too much readable, viewable and listenable data for anyone to stay abreast of.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In case you missed it, the initials ICYMI stand for the first five words of this sentence. In the event you have, indeed, missed it (ITEYH, I, MI has not taken off yet for some reason), it’s most likely because you’re not on the Internet much, particularly Twitter, where individuals and outlets deploy it every few seconds to bring links to the attention of others who may not have seen them. The New York Times now even has a section at the bottom of its app called “In Case You Missed It” with articles from previous days.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04FUTURETENSE/4FUTURETENSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 194, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04FUTURETENSE/4FUTURETENSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 194}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/04/fashion/04FUTURETENSE/4FUTURETENSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_na