n_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73ec11d0-666f-529f-8681-a18ef07c5262', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73ec11d0-666f-529f-8681-a18ef07c5262'}, {'abstract': 'The Peter Bernstein Quartet, who came together on the mid-1990s album “Signs of Life,” played Dizzy’s Club Coca-Cola over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/05/arts/music/peter-bernstein-quartet-at-dizzys-club-coca-cola.html', 'snippet': 'The Peter Bernstein Quartet, who came together on the mid-1990s album “Signs of Life,” played Dizzy’s Club Coca-Cola over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1994, the guitarist Peter Bernstein made the record “Signs of Life” for the Dutch label Criss Cross Jazz, with the pianist Brad Mehldau, the bassist Christian McBride and the drummer Greg Hutchinson. Almost exactly 20 years later, starting on Friday, Mr. Bernstein assembled the same band — all of them among the most respected players in jazz — for a three-night run at Dizzy’s Club Coca-Cola. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/BERNSTEIN/BERNSTEIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/BERNSTEIN/BERNSTEIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/BERNSTEIN/BERNSTEIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 513, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/BERNSTEIN/BERNSTEIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1025, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/BERNSTEIN/BERNSTEIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/BERNSTEIN/BERNSTEIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/BERNSTEIN/BERNSTEIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Still Studying, Inventing and Respecting After All These Years', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Studying, Inventing and Respecting After All These Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bernstein, Peter, Quartet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernstein, Peter (1967- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mehldau, Brad', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McBride, Christian', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hutchinson, Gregory (1970- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-04T22:26:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1afe201a-368a-512b-a7e0-5088abb1d01c', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1afe201a-368a-512b-a7e0-5088abb1d01c'}, {'abstract': '“Music for a Prince: The Esterhazy Orchestra,” a concert by the ensemble Artek, focused on pieces that Haydn composed for an aristocratic family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/05/arts/music/music-for-a-prince-an-artek-concert-focusing-on-haydn.html', 'snippet': '“Music for a Prince: The Esterhazy Orchestra,” a concert by the ensemble Artek, focused on pieces that Haydn composed for an aristocratic family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s telling that Haydn’s name does not feature in the title “Music for a Prince: The Esterhazy Orchestra,” an all-Haydn concert presented by the period-instrument ensemble Artek at Immanuel Lutheran Church on the Upper East Side on Saturday. The fruitful relationship between Haydn and the aristocratic Esterhazy family, in whose employ Haydn created the bulk of his vast output, is well known. But Artek’s delightful performance served as a reminder that within the much-cited isolation of the Esterhazy country estate — where Haydn said he was “forced to become original” — the most direct influence on his music was the small band of highly talented court musicians.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/ARTEK/ARTEK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 447, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/ARTEK/ARTEK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 447}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/ARTEK/ARTEK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/05/arts/ARTEK/ARTEK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1471, 'width'