lifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/066eac3e-dbb8-5c24-9138-67ba3ccb73f9', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/066eac3e-dbb8-5c24-9138-67ba3ccb73f9'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: 1940: Coffee is to the French what tea is to the English.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/05/1940-paris-welcomes-hitlers-coffee/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: 1940: Coffee is to the French what tea is to the English.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Following an announcement that 4,000 tons of coffee seized on a German freighter are to be distributed shortly in France, Parisians are hoping that some of this amount, which is enough to supply national consumption for ten days, will come to their particular groceries. As French newspapers announced it yesterday morning, ‘‘Our coffee for the next ten days will be on Adolf Hitler.’’ For some time Frenchmen, who remember that during the last war there was always an adequate supply of coffee, have been asking themselves why is there a shortage. Coffee is to the French what tea is to the English. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Jan. 6, 1940', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/06/blogs/06-iht-retrospective-cafe/06-iht-retrospective-cafe-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/06/blogs/06-iht-retrospective-cafe/06-iht-retrospective-cafe-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 376}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/06/blogs/06-iht-retrospective-cafe/06-iht-retrospective-cafe-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 501, 'width': 7