me': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2e3caef-52c9-5d02-a7c2-06445731ca85', 'word_count': 239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2e3caef-52c9-5d02-a7c2-06445731ca85'}, {'abstract': 'Two new books and a Bobbi-Brown-endorsed greens supplement make for a sound mind in a sound body for 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/05/new-health-rules-frank-lipman/', 'snippet': 'Two new books and a Bobbi-Brown-endorsed greens supplement make for a sound mind in a sound body for 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his new book “The New Health Rules” (out Jan. 6 from Artisan, $20), Dr. Frank Lipman, the founder and director of the Eleven Eleven Wellness Center in New York City and a regular contributor to Gwyneth Paltrow’s lifestyle website Goop, offers his secrets to feeling our best. He prescribes a new lifestyle, not a diet, which he breaks down into five categories: eating (“eat till you’re only 80 percent full”), moving (“stop if it hurts”), boosting (“spend lots of time with people you love”), healing (“wander barefoot”) and living (“lose the mainstream deodorant”). It’s a handy little collection of healthy habits, endorsed with book-jacket blurbs by prominent multitaskers including Paltrow, Arianna Huffington and the makeup artist Bobbi Brown.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/05/t-magazine/05book-beddie/05book-beddie-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 431, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/05/t-magazine/05book-beddie/05book-beddie-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 431}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'ca