 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Smale and Melissa Eddy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eddy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e869bb10-a4d6-5703-a798-5c6978b472f4', 'word_count': 1067, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e869bb10-a4d6-5703-a798-5c6978b472f4'}, {'abstract': 'With the police arresting half as many people and giving more than 90 percent fewer tickets, some residents were relieved while others feared for their safety.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/07/nyregion/life-in-new-york-city-where-arrests-are-down-and-tickets-are-rarities.html', 'snippet': 'With the police arresting half as many people and giving more than 90 percent fewer tickets, some residents were relieved while others feared for their safety.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a city of more than eight million people and 33 million subway rides a week, police officers arrested or ticketed just 22 people for jumping the turnstile last week. In the same period a year ago, nearly 1,400 fare-beaters were caught.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/07/nyregion/jpSLOWDOWN2/jpSLOWDOWN2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/07/nyregion/jpSLOWDOWN2/jpSLOWDOWN2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/07/nyregion/jpSLOWDOWN2/jpSLOWDOWN2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subT