ion': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d691cd4-36a6-57f8-8da5-2f3e8c8936bb', 'word_count': 1272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d691cd4-36a6-57f8-8da5-2f3e8c8936bb'}, {'abstract': 'Now that more than 70 percent of Americans live in places that have legalized marriage equality, the Supreme Court needs to decide whether to make it legal nationally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/07/opinion/florida-joins-the-wave-on-same-sex-marriage.html', 'snippet': 'Now that more than 70 percent of Americans live in places that have legalized marriage equality, the Supreme Court needs to decide whether to make it legal nationally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now that Florida has become the 36th state to allow same-sex couples to marry, more than 70 percent of Americans live in jurisdictions that permit such unions. What’s made this possible is a string of supportive federal court rulings following the Supreme Court’s pivotal 2013 decision striking down provisions of the Defense of Marriage Act — the 1996 federal law that denied federal benefits to lawfully married same-sex couples. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/07/opinion/07wed3/07wed3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/07/opinion/07wed3/07wed3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/07/opinion/07wed3/07wed3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/07/opinion/07wed3/07wed3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'su