yline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a88941cf-6923-58cd-a5e9-b8e8dc2c8ed0', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a88941cf-6923-58cd-a5e9-b8e8dc2c8ed0'}, {'abstract': '“The Woodsman” returns to 59E59 Theaters for a six-week run.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/theater/the-woodsman-returns-to-59e59-theaters.html', 'snippet': '“The Woodsman” returns to 59E59 Theaters for a six-week run.', 'lead_paragraph': 'L. Frank Baum’s novel “The Wonderful Wizard of Oz” (1900) certainly has dark elements, like those creepy flying monkeys, but the story is just one part of Baum’s Oz series. In 1918, he wrote the even darker back story of one character in “The Tin Woodsman of Oz.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sorrowful Quest of an Empty Kettle', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theater: Sorrowful Quest of an Empty Kettle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baum, L Frank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '59E59 THEATERS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Woodsman (Play)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Strangemen & Co.', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ortiz, James', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T14:55:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven McElroy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McElroy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32ea2df5-2115-566b-bb94-a8172f09f7f7', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32ea2df5-2115-566b-bb94-a8172f09f7f7'}, {'abstract': 'The British memoirist and novelist Rachel Cusk “skews the boundary between autobiography and fiction.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/rachel-cusks-outline.html', 'snippet': 'The British memoirist and novelist Rachel Cusk “skews the boundary between autobiography and fiction.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was out to dinner recently with a man who observed of a mutual friend: “He’s a cipher. He just stands there and doesn’t speak.” He felt our friend did not pull his weight in social situations. I countered that there were participatory ways of saying nothing; that I experienced our friend’s silence as encouragement to display, for his appreciation and scrutiny, the more typically hidden parts of myself. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11julavitz/11julavitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 916, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11julavitz/11julavitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 916}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11julavitz/11julavitz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 671, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11julavitz/11julavitz-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1341, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11julavitz/11julavitz-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11julavitz/11julavitz-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11julavitz/11julavitz-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rachel Cusk’s ‘Outline’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Stories We Tell', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Julavits, Heidi', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cusk, Rachel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Outline (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T15:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heidi Julavits', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heidi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Julavits', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/162fcd62-97e7-59af-925d-493250025893', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/162fcd62-97e7-59af-925d-493250025893'}, {'abstract': 'Picture-book biographies of the painter Benny Andrews and the soprano Leontyne Price trace their paths from African-American childhoods in the segregated South to international acclaim.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/07/books/review/draw-what-you-see-and-leontyne-price.html', 'snippet': 'Picture-book biographies of the painter Benny Andrews and the soprano Leontyne Price trace their paths from African-American childhoods in the segregated South to international acclaim.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world has no shortage of interesting people who make a big impact without ever quite becoming household names, and lately we’ve seen a plentiful crop of picture-book biographies about some of them. Among several good recent examples are Jen Bryant and Melissa Sweet’s “The Right Word: Roget and His Thesaurus” and Jeanette Winter’s “Mr. Cornell’s Dream Boxes.” Both found a whimsical, child-friendly charm in the eccentricities of subjects few would have called ideal picture-book material: the neurotic Victorian Peter Mark Roget, who came up with the idea for the thesaurus, and the reclusive avant-garde artist Joseph Cornell, who innovated with assemblage. Two new ones are particularly welcome additions to the genre, for both their beautiful illustrations and their stirring stories: Each takes up the life of a 20th-century artist whose accomplishments are no doubt too rarefied to be already familiar to the average elementary schooler; but the subjects’ experiences growing up African-American in the segregated South, then making their ways through civil-rights-era America, give a natural storybook feel to their life trajectories.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/07/books/07childrens/07childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/07/books/07childrens/07childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/07/books/07childrens/07childrens-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}