ue': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duke, David E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scalise, Steve', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T16:14:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Rappeport', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rappeport', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25062911-565e-56b9-aa27-18aa3dabc768', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25062911-565e-56b9-aa27-18aa3dabc768'}, {'abstract': 'Blake Leyh, a supervising sound editor for “Into the Woods,” discusses the falling trees and cow burps he recorded for the movie.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/07/below-the-line-the-sounds-of-into-the-woods/', 'snippet': 'Blake Leyh, a supervising sound editor for “Into the Woods,” discusses the falling trees and cow burps he recorded for the movie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If a tree falls in the forest during “Into the Woods,” does it make a sound? For one of the film’s supervising sound editors, Blake Leyh, the answer is a definitive yes. That noise, along with others like\xa0 the plodding footsteps of a giant, were meticulously crafted and layered.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/into-woods/into-woods-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/into-woods/into-woods-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/into-woods/into-woods-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 625, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/into-woods/into-woods-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1251, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/into-woods/into-woods-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/into-woods/into-woods-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/into-woods/into-woods-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Below the Line: The Sounds of ‘Into the Woods’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leyh, Blake', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T16:18:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ad7d6a4-da0f-5b54-93a7-91fd4a46483a', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ad7d6a4-da0f-5b54-93a7-91fd4a46483a'}, {'abstract': 'The new Senate majority leader said “the uptick appears to coincide” with “the expectation of a new Republican Congress.”', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/07/did-mitch-mcconnell-just-take-credit-for-the-economic-recovery/', 'snippet': 'The new Senate majority leader said “the uptick appears to coincide” with “the expectation of a new Republican Congress.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new Senate majority leader, Mitch McConnell, suggested earlier today that the Republican Party deserves credit for recent data showing that the economic recovery has picked up speed. As ridiculous as that statement is, at least it is intellectually consistent with the party’s past statements. After the financial crisis, Republicans said President Obama had hurt the economy by (occasionally) criticizing Wall Street bankers for their misdeeds. Now, Mr. McConnell is claiming credit for a recovery based solely on the fact that Republicans have just taken control of both houses of Congress, even though the year has barely begun and they have not yet passed any bills.Here’s what Mr. McConnell said on the floor this morning:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Did Mitch McConnell Just Take Credit for the Economic Recovery?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T16:24:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vikas Bajaj', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vikas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bajaj', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f32d3d1-71a6-552f-a96f-297a53238e51', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f32d3d1-71a6-552f-a96f-297a53238e51'}, {'abstract': 'Phylicia Rashad, who played Bill Cosby’s wife on two sitcoms, defends him against accusations of sexual assault.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/07/phylicia-rashad-defends-cosby-saying-forget-his-accusers/', 'snippet': 'Phylicia Rashad, who played Bill Cosby’s wife on two sitcoms, defends him against accusations of sexual assault.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Phylicia Rashad, who played Bill Cosby’s wife on two popular sitcoms, broke her silence on Tuesday to defend the comedian, who has been accused of sexually assaulting many women.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Phylicia Rashad Defends Cosby, Saying ‘Forget’ His Accusers', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rashad Defends Cosby, Saying ‘Forget’ His Accusers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cosby, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rashad, Phylicia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T16:26:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lorne Manly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lorne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Manly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/980356e0-30c3-5315-a51c-a6e0d437f1e4', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/980356e0-30c3-5315-a51c-a6e0d437f1e4'}, {'abstract': 'Leon Wieseltier on the state of culture in the digital age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/books/review/among-the-disrupted.html', 'snippet': 'Leon Wieseltier on the state of culture in the digital age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid the bacchanal of disruption, let us pause to honor the disrupted. The streets of American cities are haunted by the ghosts of bookstores and record stores, which have been destroyed by the greatest thugs in the history of the culture industry. Writers hover between a decent poverty and an indecent one; they are expected to render the fruits of their labors for little and even for nothing, and all the miracles of electronic dissemination somehow do not suffice for compensation, either of the fiscal or the spiritual kind. Everybody talks frantically about media, a second-order subject if ever there was one, as content disappears into “content.” What does the understanding of media contribute to the understanding of life? Journalistic institutions slowly transform themselves into silent sweatshops in which words cannot wait for thoughts, and first responses are promoted into best responses, and patience is a professional liability. As the frequency of expression grows, the force of expression diminishes: Digital expectations of alacrity and terseness confer the highest prestige upon the twittering cacophony of one-liners and promotional announcements. It was always the case that all things must pass, but this is ridiculous.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18wieseltier/18wieseltier-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18wieseltier/18wieseltier-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18wieseltier/18wieseltier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18wieseltier/18wieseltier-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1264, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18wieseltier/18wieseltier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18wieseltier/18wieseltier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18wieseltier/18wieseltier-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Among the Disrupted', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Among the Disrupted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wieseltier, Leon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T16:27:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leon Wieseltier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wieseltier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4829854b-a7e6-583a-881a-472651bef093', 'word_count': 2534, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48298