material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/a810f8f2-07d7-5fa8-aeda-813f979cba3d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/a810f8f2-07d7-5fa8-aeda-813f979cba3d'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/07/classical-playlist-beethoven-brahms-john-luther-adams-and-more/', 'snippet': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEETHOVEN, BRAHMS, WEBER Jon Manasse, clarinetist; Jon Nakamatsu, pianist; Clive Greensmith, cellist (Harmonia Mundi) The clarinetist Jon Manasse, the pianist Jon Nakamatsu and the cellist Clive Greensmith open this disc with Beethoven’s Trio in B flat (Op. 11), Mr. Nakamatsu’s fleet-fingered clarity enhancing the vivacious outer movements and all three playing with deep expression in the Adagio. Mr. Manasse demonstrates a lovely, limpid tone throughout, such as in the second movement of Weber’s Grand Duo Concertant in E flat (Op. 48). Also included on the disc is a passionate rendition of Brahms’s autumnal Trio in A minor (Op. 114). (Vivien Schweitzer)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/classical/classical-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/classical/classical-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/classical/classical-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/classical/classical-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1320, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/classical/classical-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/classical/classical-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/arts/classical/classical-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Classical Playlist: Beethoven, Brahms, John Luther Adams and More', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Do Plants Sound Like? One Composeru2019s Answer, and More', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adams, John Luther', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manasse, Jon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mazzoli, Missy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nakamatsu, Jon', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T17:47:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a78b51fd-754d-50f3-8678-6c1bdc19847c', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a78b51fd-754d-50f3-8678-6c1bdc19847c'}, {'abstract': 'The group of experts endorsed a close version of the cancer drug Neupogen, which could open the door to more such biosimilar medicines.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/08/science/fda-panel-vote-biologics.html', 'snippet': 'The group of experts endorsed a close version of the cancer drug Neupogen, which could open the door to more such biosimilar medicines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SILVER SPRING, Md. — An expert panel unanimously recommended on Wednesday that the Food and Drug Administration approve a cheaper copy of a special drug used in cancer therapy, paving the way for alternatives to an entire class of complex and costly drugs to enter the United States market.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For First Time, F.D.A. Panel Approves Generic Copy of Costly Biologic Drug ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For First Time, F.D.A. Panel Approves Generic Copy of Costly Biologic Drug', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tavernise, Sabrina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Generic Brands and Products', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T17:52:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sabrina Tavernise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sabrina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tavernise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fff22c09-4223-5721-888e-8a9911ada99b', 'word_count': 818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fff22c09-4223-5721-888e-8a9911ada99b'}, {'abstract': 'The new energy coursing through the city can be enjoyed even before this year’s World Expo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/travel/what-to-do-in-36-hours-in-milan.html', 'snippet': 'The new energy coursing through the city can be enjoyed even before this year’s World Expo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If it’s been a few years since you last visited Milan (our top choice for 52 Places to Go in 2015), you’re in for a pleasant shock. Yes, the preparations for the World Expo being held here this year (May through October) turned large swaths of the city into construction zones, many of which are still in progress. But alongside these urban development projects, a more organic wave of rejuvenation has swept through the city. Old structures of various stripes — among them a sawmill, a foundry, a bank and a farmhouse — have recently been repurposed as bars, shops, restaurants and cultural centers worthy of this most international of Italian cities. Put simply, there’s new energy coursing through cosmopolitan Milan, and it’s likely to last long after the Expo.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Milan', 'kicker': '36 Hours', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Ingrid K', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Milan (Italy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-07T17:54:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ingrid K. Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ingrid', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa209dd0-66ac-555e-a16e-88820a5b82ea', 'word_count': 1601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa209dd0-66ac-555e-a16e-88820a5b82ea'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers reported that a new drug, extracted from a soil sample, easily cured infectio