 {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard University Press', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Murty Classical Library of India', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T13:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26925343-9fba-5190-bb58-0dbcf894c853', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26925343-9fba-5190-bb58-0dbcf894c853'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/08/arts/television/whats-on-tv-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (Showtime) FINDING VIVIAN MAIER (2014) In 2007, John Maloof bought a box of negatives — nearly 40,000, taken from the 1950s to the ’70s — at a Chicago auction for about $400. He was told they were by Vivian Maier, but a Google search revealed no information about her. Eventually he mounted some of the works on Flickr, which led to a story in the British newspaper The Independent and an exhibition at the Chicago Cultural Center about Ms. Maier, a nanny and amateur street photographer who died in 2009. This film, which Mr. Maloof directed with Charlie Siskel, documents his search for the woman behind the images while serving as a sort of feature-length advertisement for the work he owns, Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times. “It’s no surprise that Maier is now the subject of a documentary, given the quality of her work, the nominal exoticism of her life and the secrets that still drift around her,” Ms. Dargis said. “She’s a terrific story — part Mary Poppins, part Weegee — who was at once emancipated and in service.” Still, she added, “time and professional pronouncements will decide if she was an outsider artist or just an unorthodox one who, as a single 20th-century woman, found freedom, her voice and her own private bohemia while under cover of other people’s bourgeois domesticity.” The documentary has been shortlisted as an Oscar contender.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T13:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/84f533a3-87fc-583f-a4a9-f2d4e20d5071', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84f533a3-87fc-583f-a4a9-f2d4e20d5071'}, {'abstract': 'The singer-songwriter Meghan Trainor made a splash with the bouncy “All About That Bass,” but she has no intention of being a one-hit wonder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/arts/music/meghan-trainor-enters-spotlight-with-title.html', 'snippet': 'The singer-songwriter Meghan Trainor made a splash with the bouncy “All About That Bass,” but she has no intention of being a one-hit wonder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Meghan Trainor doesn’t really dance.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11TRAINOR1/11TRAINOR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width':