iced even by some staff members. Roberto C. Ferrari, an art historian and librarian who became Columbia’s curator of art properties in 2013, has set out to raise the profile of the 10,000 objects on campus.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 723}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 850, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 995, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Raising the Profile of Columbia’s Art ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out of the Storage Room and Into the Limelight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARKANSAS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T21:37:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve M. Kahn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e376c992-2d36-5354-8b4d-93b2a829f944', 'word_count': 1129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e376c992-2d36-5354-8b4d-93b2a829f944'}, {'abstract': 'Many wondered why The Times had not printed the cartoons.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/08/times-readers-on-the-charlie-hebdo-massacre/', 'snippet': 'Many wondered why The Times had not printed the cartoons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readers responding to a Times editorial on the Charlie Hebdo killings seemed most keen to discuss what wasn’t in the editorial: Reproductions of Charlie Hebdo cartoons.\xa0 A Times spokesperson has explained that the paper does “not normally publish images or other material deliberately intended to offend religious sensibilities.” But many readers said that was a cop out.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Times Readers on the Charlie Hebdo Massacre', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charlie Hebdo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T21:37:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/098b8a1a-7580-52f0-a6e5-9761778ad312', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/098b8a1a-7580-52f0-a6e5-9761778ad312'}, {'abstract': 'The writer and director discusses his film.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/08/j-c-chandor-narrates-a-scene-from-a-most-violent-year/', 'snippet': 'The writer and director discusses his film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this video, the writer and director J. C. Chandor narrates a sequence from his film, featuring Oscar Isaac as the intimidating owner of a heating oil company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'J. C. Chandor Narrates a Scene From ‘A Most Violent Year’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chandor, J C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T21:38:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c0c1781-f45e-5916-8fc9-1701d0d8e463', 'word_count': 28, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c0c1781-f45e-5916-8fc9-1701d0d8e463'}, {'abstract': 'Yahoo isn’t a stranger to the complex deal structure known as a “cash-rich split-off” that could be used to sell off its stakes in its Japanese affiliate and Alibaba Group.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2015/01/08/more-on-the-complex-tax-minded-deal-that-starboard-doesnt-want-yahoo-to-do/', 'snippet': 'Yahoo isn’t a stranger to the complex deal structure known as a “cash-rich split-off” that could be used to sell off its stakes in its Japanese affiliate and Alibaba Group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The activist hedge fund Starboard Value fretted on Thursday that Yahoo might employ a type of complicated transaction to dispose its minority stakes in the Chinese Internet giant Alibaba Group and in the publicly traded Yahoo Japan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More on the Complex Tax-Minded Deal That Starboard Doesn’t Want Yahoo to Do', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Jeff', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alibaba Group Holding Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Berkshire Hathaway Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Duracell International Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Starboard Value LP', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yahoo! Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T21:39:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16990a44-4dc7-5dc0-85d0-e55303ca4375', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16990a44-4dc7-5dc0-85d0-e55303ca4375'}, {'abstract': '“Stalin: Paradoxes of Power” is the first of three volumes that, in Stephen Kotkin’s explanation, tell “the story of Russia’s power in the world and Stalin’s power in Russia.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/books/stalin-paradoxes-of-power-by-stephen-kotkin.html', 'snippet': '“Stalin: Paradoxes of Power” is the first of three volumes that, in Stephen Kotkin’s explanation, tell “the story of Russia’s power in the world and Stalin’s power in Russia.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readers plunging into Stephen Kotkin’s “Stalin: Paradoxes of Power” expecting a detailed dissection of the cobbler’s son and seminarian from Georgia who evolved into the monster called Stalin may be disconcerted to find that, as a boy called Iosif Dzhugashvili (Jughashvili in this book), he plays a relatively minor role in early chapters.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09JPBOOKKOTKIN2/JPBOOKKOTKIN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 471, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09JPBOOKKOTKIN2/JPBOOKKOTKIN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 471}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09JPBOOKKOTKIN2/JPBOOKKOTKIN2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 804, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09JPBOOKKOTKIN2/JPBOOKKOTKIN2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1609, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09JPBOOKKOTKIN2/JPBOOKKOTKIN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09JPBOOKKOTKIN2/JPBOOKKOTKIN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09JPBOOKKOTKIN2/JPBOOKKOTKIN2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Czarist Rubble, a Russian Autocrat Rises ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Czarist Rubble, a Russian Autocrat Rises', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kotkin, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stalin, Joseph', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Stalin: Volume I: Paradoxes of Power, 1878-1928 (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T21:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Serge Schmemann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Serge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmemann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a73b3c7-012d-5f7c-9838-faa27b4f7d74', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a73b3c7-012d-5f7c-9838-faa27b4f7d74'}, {'abstract': '“Subterranean Monuments,” a retrospective at Tibor de Nagy, celebrates the work of Rudy Burckhardt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/arts/design/rudy-burckhardt-subterranean-monuments-a-centenary-celebration.html', 'snippet': '“Subterranean Monuments,” a retrospective at Tibor de Nagy, celebrates the work of Rudy Burckhardt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rarer than the artist’s artist is the poet’s artist, and Rudy Burckhardt was one. Born in Switzerland in 1914 into a family of scholars and businessmen, he picked up photography early and, after meeting the American poet and critic Edwin Denby, came to New York in 1935. Creating art and living in an aesthetic milieu seemed, initially at least, to have about equal weight for him. Mr. Burckhardt tended to speak of himself as a committed amateur in the sense of being someone who does what he does for love, not glory or pay. In his case, that love embraced several mediums, all represented in a small museum-ready survey called “Rudy Burckhardt: Subterranean Monuments: A Centenary Celebration” at Tibor de Nagy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09BURCKHARDTGALLERY1/BURCKHARDT1GALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09BURCKHARDTGALLERY1/BURCKHARDT1GALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09BURCKHARDTGALLERY1/BURCKHARDT1GALLERY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09BURCKHARDTGALLERY1/BURCKHARDT1GALLERY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1667, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09BURCKHARDTGALLERY1/BURCKHARDT1GALLERY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnai