r': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T21:46:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By James B. Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3f44e83-fa08-5e6e-a255-bd575291f3d2', 'word_count': 1397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3f44e83-fa08-5e6e-a255-bd575291f3d2'}, {'abstract': 'This show features art created in response to the 2011 earthquake in the Tohoku region of Japan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/arts/design/takashi-murakami-at-gagosian-in-chelsea.html', 'snippet': 'This show features art created in response to the 2011 earthquake in the Tohoku region of Japan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his exhibition at Gagosian, Takashi Murakami appears unintimidated by big historical and religious subjects but strangely cowed by his fellow artists. Working in response to the devastating earthquake and tsunami of 2011 in the Tohoku region of Japan, he has come up with a quasi-spiritual installation of paintings and sculptures surrounding a copy of the gates of a Buddhist temple. It invites us to meditate and heal from the trauma of natural disasters and to savor expensively fabricated art objects while worshiping Koons, Hirst and Warhol (as if we needed another place to do that).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09MURAKAMIGALLERY4/MURAKAMI-GALLERY4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09MURAKAMIGALLERY4/MURAKAMI-GALLERY4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption'