k of the photographer Horace Poolaw.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new show at the National Museum of the American Indian features the work of the photographer Horace Poolaw.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/design/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/design/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 768}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/design/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/design/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/design/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/design/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/design/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7/20150109-POOLAWWALTHER-slide-A4L7-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘For a Love of His People’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Native Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Museum of the American Indian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poolaw, Horace (1906-84)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T22:01:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/296c8ebb-250b-54af-b871-a9fc55a8e555', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/296c8ebb-250b-54af-b871-a9fc55a8e555'}, {'abstract': 'The Museum of Modern Art’s new exhibition of 20th-century photography from the Walther Collection is enhanced by information tools that give it scope and depth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/arts/design/moma-gives-walther-photo-collection-multiple-platforms.html', 'snippet': 'The Museum of Modern Art’s new exhibition of 20th-century photography from the Walther Collection is enhanced by information tools that give it scope and depth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Overflowing with prints by Berenice Abbott, Andre Kertesz, Edward Weston and other luminaries from the first half of the 20th century, “Modern Photographs: The Thomas Walther Collection, 1909-1949,” at the Museum of Modern Art, would seem to be a straightforward look at photography’s past. But the show, packaged with a book, a symposium and an engrossing interactive website, is really a bold attempt to visualize the future of photography inside the museum as it reckons with the unwieldy, image-saturated culture outside the galleries.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Black and White and Accessible All Over ', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Black and White and Accessible All Over', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenberg, Karen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walther, Thomas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T22:01:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/681b52bd-b05f-52e6-b4b2-a66e81b189a2', 'word_count': 766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/681b52bd-b05f-52e6-b4b2-a66e81b189a2'}, {'abstract': 'Horace Poolaw’s photographs at the National Museum of the American Indian in Lower Manhattan show Native Americans inventively fusing past and present.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/arts/design/photographs-by-horace-poolaw-at-national-museum-of-the-american-indian.html', 'snippet': 'Horace Poolaw’s photographs at the National Museum of the American Indian in Lower Manhattan show Native Americans inventively fusing past and present.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 2001 exhibition called “Spirit Capture: Native Americans and the Photographic Image,” at the National Museum of the American Indian in Lower Manhattan, remains one of the most moving photography shows I’ve seen in New York in two decades. Culled from the museum’s permanent collection, it wasn’t about formal perfection or a singular sensibility. It was an archival look at a group of people as they were historically portrayed through a visual medium they didn’t invent and, until recently, rarely used. Indeed, it was a show about how that medium invented and used them, or rather stereotyped images of them — the recalcitrant savage, the timeless primitive, the vanishing race — to create an official American saga from a victor’s point of view. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Going Mainstream on Their Own Terms', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Going Mainstream on Their Own Terms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poolaw, Horace (1906-84)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Museum of the American Indian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Native Americans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-08T22:02:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7e5e115-6ae9-59a8-b3bc-656c1a6ff859', 'word_count': 1252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7e5e115-6ae9-59a8-b3bc-656c1a6ff859'}, {'abstract': 'Devin Powers’s kaleidoscopic new paintings at Lesley Heller Workspace muster an elaborate physicality while corralling references to various cultures, mediums and artifacts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/arts/design/devin-powerss-paintings-at-lesley-heller-workspace.html', 'snippet': 'Devin Powers’s kaleidoscopic new paintings at Lesley Heller Workspace muster an elaborate physicality while corralling references to various cultures, mediums and artifacts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Devin Powers’s kaleidoscopic new paintings muster an elaborate physicality while corralling a profusion of references to different cultures, mediums and artifacts within their irregular borders. They might more logically be called painted reliefs, but they confuse matters by being so clearly derived from a single surface on which a mongrel process of drawing, painting and chiseling has run a bit rampant. And nothing is quite as rampant as the unhinged geometric patterns, defined first by automatist drawing and then by divots painted with contrasting colors, which give the best works a writhing, billowing energy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09POWERS-GALLERY7/09POWERS-GALLERY7-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 764, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09POWERS-GALLERY7/09POWERS-GALLERY7-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 764}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09POWERS-GALLERY7/09POWERS-GALLERY7-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 804, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09POWERS-GALLERY7/09POWERS-GALLERY7-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1608, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thu