'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0810cefb-12d2-555e-bf14-870b33819473', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0810cefb-12d2-555e-bf14-870b33819473'}, {'abstract': 'In “My Voice Has an Echo in It,” by the Temporary Distortion multimedia theater company, four band members play for six hours in a room-size box walled by mirrors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/theater/my-voice-has-an-echo-in-it-part-of-the-coil-festival.html', 'snippet': 'In “My Voice Has an Echo in It,” by the Temporary Distortion multimedia theater company, four band members play for six hours in a room-size box walled by mirrors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Temporary Distortion’s durational installation “My Voice Has an Echo in It,” part of the Coil festival, has the feeling of a happening in which not much actually happens. The company drew notice with a trilogy of plays — “Welcome to Nowhere,” “Americana Kamikaze” and “Newyorkland” — that juxtaposed skillful video sequences with less compelling live ones. The video sprawled and soared and dazzled. The stage actors hunched in viselike shadow boxes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09MYVOICE/MYVOICE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09MYVOICE/MYVOICE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/09/arts/09MYVOI