: 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68d9eb02-d47a-5a81-8312-023f9efd238e', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68d9eb02-d47a-5a81-8312-023f9efd238e'}, {'abstract': 'Defense officials said that more American troops would be stationed in Germany and Italy as the United States sought to counter a more aggressive Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/world/europe/us-consolidates-forces-in-europe-to-save-money.html', 'snippet': 'Defense officials said that more American troops would be stationed in Germany and Italy as the United States sought to counter a more aggressive Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Defense Department is consolidating American forces in Europe to save what Pentagon officials say will be about $500 million a year while not reducing the number of American military personnel — some 67,000 — who are currently there.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Consolidates Forces in Europe to Save Money', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Consolidates Forces in Europe to Save Money', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Bases and Installations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'F-35 Airplane', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T01:20:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helene Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/080a77a7-7050-5355-a9cf-4d398c9bb613', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/080a77a7-7050-5355-a9cf-4d398c9bb613'}, {'abstract': 'The attack in France reminds us to look closely at our own speech codes, and to remember that offensive speech should be discouraged socially but never legally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/opinion/david-brooks-i-am-not-charlie-hebdo.html', 'snippet': 'The attack in France reminds us to look closely at our own speech codes, and to remember that offensive speech should be discouraged socially but never legally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The journalists at Charlie Hebdo are now rightly being celebrated as martyrs on behalf of freedom of expression, but let’s face it: If they had tried to publish their satirical newspaper on any American university campus over the last two decades it wouldn’t have lasted 30 seconds. Student and faculty groups would have accused them of hate speech. The administration would have cut financing and shut them down. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/brooks-circular/brooks-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/1