: 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/time-warp-56th-and-broadway/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: The sight of a longhaired young woman running in Midtown, coffee in hand, took an observer back to a summer and a heartthrob long ago.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Dear Diary:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Time Warp, 56th and Broadway', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Love (Emotion)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T13:56:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30b028b9-429c-5a44-999a-4927333a343b', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30b028b9-429c-5a44-999a-4927333a343b'}, {'abstract': 'Fun, but beware.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/theories-of-everything/', 'snippet': 'Fun, but beware.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve worked on a lot of interesting things over the course of my career, but I surely had the most intellectual fun working on economic geography. The models were nifty, the stories seemed to shed a lot of light on history. And maybe too much light — the temptation to claim universal explanatory power was strong. I can certainly see why Noah Smith likes to suggest that the New Economic Geography may be a theory of everything. But here’s what Tony Venables and I wrote in the paper (pdf) that comes closest to what Noah is saying:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theories of Everything', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T13:56:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6030c92-1b99-5c32-9e2f-c2187acaee6d', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6030c92-1b99-5c32-9e2f-c2187acaee6d'}, {'abstract': 'The changing complexities, and constant follies, of our autocorrected life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/style/when-autocorrect-goes-wrong-and-so-so-right.html', 'snippet': 'The changing complexities, and constant follies, of our autocorrected life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There were a few things that were sweetly grandmotherly about Patricia Duncan Moran’s Facebook post to her great-granddaughter Devyn on her birthday. For starters, Ms. Moran had posted it to her own wall instead of Devyn’s. She named but did not tag her great-granddaughter. But most striking was the signer.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/fashion/11AUTOCORRECT/11AUTOCORRECT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 206, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/08/fashion/11AUTOCORRECT/11AUTOCORRECT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 206}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/08/fashion/11AUTOCORRECT/11AUTOCORRECT-j