icle', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd6c7d10-79fb-5b98-b94c-a51b2250f7f2', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd6c7d10-79fb-5b98-b94c-a51b2250f7f2'}, {'abstract': 'A pair of Gallic meditations on love come to DVD: one fantastical (“Mood Indigo”), the other observational (“A Summer’s Tale”).', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/movies/homevideo/gondrys-mood-indigo-and-rohmers-a-summers-tale-come-to-dvd.html', 'snippet': 'A pair of Gallic meditations on love come to DVD: one fantastical (“Mood Indigo”), the other observational (“A Summer’s Tale”).', 'lead_paragraph': "“Mood Indigo,” the retro-futurist folly fashioned by Michel Gondry from “L'Écume des Jours” (“The Foam of the Days”), written in the heady aftermath of World War II by the French novelist, jazz critic, musician and Saint-Germain-des-Prés scene-maker Boris Vian (1920-1959), is an evident labor of love. It is often labored but sometimes lovely, particularly in the longer version included on Drafthouse’s DVD and Blu-ray edition.", 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11HOBERMAN/11HOBERMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11HOBERMAN/11HOBERMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11HOBERMAN/11HOBERMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 533, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11HOB