ons', 'value': 'Federal Housing Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:03:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Prevost', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prevost', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de310b94-622e-5b91-9c76-19e8e592fd40', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de310b94-622e-5b91-9c76-19e8e592fd40'}, {'abstract': 'Nebraska’s highest court gave its blessing today to construction of the Keystone XL pipeline, but its ruling hasn’t swayed President Obama.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2015/01/09/despite-pipeline-ruling-white-house-renews-veto-threat/', 'snippet': 'Nebraska’s highest court gave its blessing today to construction of the Keystone XL pipeline, but its ruling hasn’t swayed President Obama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nebraska’s highest court gave its blessing today to construction of the Keystone XL pipeline, but its ruling hasn’t swayed President Obama.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Pipeline Ruling, White House Renews Veto Threat', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Keystone Pipeline System', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:07:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Hirschfeld Davis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': 'Hirschfeld', 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b096307-18b0-5651-b385-99d8a7bd57cf', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b096307-18b0-5651-b385-99d8a7bd57cf'}, {'abstract': 'The Financial Conduct Authority said that Alex Hope, who reportedly spent more than $300,000 in champagne during a single night out, promised large returns for investors but suffered heavy losses.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/trader-with-taste-for-bubbly-convicted-in-7-5-million-investment-scam-in-britain/', 'snippet': 'The Financial Conduct Authority said that Alex Hope, who reportedly spent more than $300,000 in champagne during a single night out, promised large returns for investors but suffered heavy losses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – A trader who reportedly once spent more than $188,000 on a single bottle of Champagne was convicted of fraud on Friday in an investment scam that authorities say took in 5 million pounds, or about $7.54 million, from investors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/10/business/dbpix-midas/dbpix-midas-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 868, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/10/business/dbpix-midas/dbpix-midas-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 868}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/10/business/dbpix-midas/dbpix-midas-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/10/business/dbpix-midas/dbpix-midas-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1417, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/10/business/dbpix-midas/dbpix-midas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/10/business/dbpix-midas/dbpix-midas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/10/business/dbpix-midas/dbpix-midas-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trader With a Taste for Bubbly Convicted in $7.5 Million Investment Scam in Britain', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Conduct Authority (Great Britain)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:09:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72aabb0f-9215-50b2-9d47-14dd8ec0ab67', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72aabb0f-9215-50b2-9d47-14dd8ec0ab67'}, {'abstract': 'In this short documentary filmed at Charlie Hebdo in 2006, cartoonists and editors design a satirical front page image of Muhammad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/opinion/charlie-hebdo-before-the-massacre.html', 'snippet': 'In this short documentary filmed at Charlie Hebdo in 2006, cartoonists and editors design a satirical front page image of Muhammad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In February 2006 the editors of the French magazine Charlie Hebdo met to discuss a matter of what turned out to be deadly consequence: Would they publish a satirical image of Muhammad on their cover? We were making a documentary about Jean Cabut, known as Cabu, one of the most famous cartoonists in France. So we were there, filming his conversation with his colleagues as they chose the cover. The issue that came out of this meeting — with a Cabu cartoon on the cover and the images they discussed here — turned out to be one of the most popular in the magazine’s history. Almost nine years later, gunmen stormed this very meeting and killed 10 editors and cartoonists, including three of the people in this film: Cabu, Bernard Verlhac (known as Tignous) and Georges Wolinski.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Charlie Hebdo, Before the Massacre’', 'kicker': 'Op-Docs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charlie Hebdo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:10:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jérôme Lambert and Philippe Picard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jérôme', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lambert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Philippe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Picard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c863ebe-a66c-5fee-888a-803da71b9a6d', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c863ebe-a66c-5fee-888a-803da71b9a6d'}, {'abstract': 'The author heads for a breakdown as his world implodes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/my-heart-is-a-drunken-compass-by-domingo-martinez.html', 'snippet': 'The author heads for a breakdown as his world implodes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The memoirist Domingo Martinez is irreverent, judgmental and unapologetic. At its best, his writing calls to mind the bravado of Junot Díaz, another foul-mouthed son of contemporary literature. Like “The Boy Kings of Texas” — Martinez’s first memoir, which was a 2012 finalist for the National Book Award and was recently optioned for an HBO series — his latest, “My Heart Is a Drunken Compass,” is a tragic comedy filled with wit and cultural insight. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘My Heart Is a Drunken Compass,’ by Domingo Martinez', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'He’s a Rebel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martinez, Domingo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'My Heart Is a Drunken Compass (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Venegas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Venegas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/20bc5915-92de-5d9f-b5f3-cbb9eaaec6bd', 'word_count': 576, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20bc5915-92de-5d9f-b5f3-cbb9eaaec6bd'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONEYDEW: Stories,  by Edith Pearlman. (Little, Brown, $25.) With simultaneous intimacy and distance, the tales in Pearlman’s majestic collection excel at capturing the complex and surprising turns in seemingly ordinary lives.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent books of particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f2eaa34-e094-51fe-8d67-219933b57365', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f2eaa34-e094-51fe-8d67-219933b57365'}, {'abstract': 'A novel of Vanessa Bell and Virginia Woolf includes invented letters and diary entries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/vanessa-and-her-sister-by-priya-parmar.html', 'snippet': 'A novel of Vanessa Bell and Virginia Woolf includes invented letters and diary entries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rarely do you encounter a woman who commands as much admiration as does the painter Vanessa Bell in Priya Parmar’s multilayered, subtly shaded novel, “Vanessa and Her Sister.” The sister of the title is, of course, Virginia Woolf, who understood how hard it is for a novelist to capture a character. “Few catch the phantom,” Woolf said in a lecture at Cambridge in 1924, the year before the publication of “Mrs. Dalloway.” “Most have to be content with a scrap of her dress or a wisp of her hair.” Parmar’s portrait brings Vanessa out of the shadows, into fully realized, shining visibility. The world remembers Virginia better than her enigmatic older sister: Parmar restores the symmetry of their relationship in the familial landscape, showing how essential Vanessa’s steadying force was to Virginia’s precarious balance. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11schillinger/11schillinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11schillinger/11schillinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11schillinger/11schillinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 748, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11schillinger/11schillinger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 748, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11schillinger/11schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11schillinger/11schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11schillinger/11schillinger-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Vanessa and Her Sister,’ by Priya Parmar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Other Sister', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schillinger, Liesl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parmar, Priya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolf, Virginia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Vanessa', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Vanessa and Her Sister (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/80440f40-9dc9-5414-adc5-cb104991e73f', 'word_count': 1344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80440f40-9dc9-5414-adc5-cb104991e73f'}, {'abstract': 'The stories of Paddington’s adventures bounce along with a Wodehousian lightness and consistency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/please-look-after-this-bear.html', 'snippet': 'The stories of Paddington’s adventures bounce along with a Wodehousian lightness and consistency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Paddington Bear landed in London in 1958, it was still quite a provincial place. Safe, settled, a little gray — no sign of the Beatles or the swinging ’60s yet — it upheld the ceremonial proprieties immortalized in “Brief Encounter” and “84, Charing Cross Road.” Men wore ties to dinner, women skirts; the post-nuclear nightmares and beatnik explosions of America were barely visible on occasional television screens. Yes, the likes of the Trinidad-born novelist Samuel Selvon were beginning to give voice to other realities in works like “The Lonely Londoners,” but if a British family’s name was Brown, you could be fairly sure its skin was not.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11SUB-IYER/11SUB-IYER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 329, 'width': 330, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11SUB-IYER/11SUB-IYER-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 329, 'width': 330, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11SUB-IYER/11SUB-IYER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11SUB-IYER/11SUB-IYER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11SUB-IYER/11SUB-IYER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Please Look After This Bear', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Take', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Please Look After This Bear', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Iyer, Pico', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bond, Michael (1926- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Bear Called Paddington (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pico Iyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pico', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Iyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d64ce8b8-5f16-5bdf-8875-8090b7e94a23', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d64ce8b8-5f16-5bdf-8875-8090b7e94a23'}, {'abstract': 'With the defeat of Japan, the United States hoped to prevent civil war in China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/china-1945-by-richard-bernstein.html', 'snippet': 'With the defeat of Japan, the United States hoped to prevent civil war in China.', 'lead_paragraph': '“To err is human. To blame someone else is politics.” Hubert H. Humphrey’s aphorism came to mind as I was reading “China 1945,” Richard Bernstein’s excellent history of United States policy toward China at the end of World War II. The comment is particularly apt when intractable problems provide myriad paths to failure but no clear right answer — and the civil war in China after Japan’s surrender was just such a problem. (Today, think of Syria.) The debates over China policy were acrimonious and personal, and this was several years before Joseph McCarthy lent his name to sneering political assassination.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11christensen/11christensen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11christensen/11christensen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11christensen/11christensen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11christensen/11christensen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1707, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11christensen/11christensen-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11christensen/11christensen-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11christensen/11christensen-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘China 1945,’ by Richard Bernstein', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chiang vs. Mao', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernstein, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mao Zedong', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "China 1945: Mao's Revolution and America's Fateful Choice (Book)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas J. Christensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Christensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/db8187a5-77bc-5613-8945-25c88d620f9d', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db8187a5-77bc-5613-8945-25c88d620f9d'}, {'abstract': 'Sam Savage’s novel of a South Carolina family in decline is constructed from a daughter’s scattered memories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/it-will-end-with-us-by-sam-savage.html', 'snippet': 'Sam Savage’s novel of a South Carolina family in decline is constructed from a daughter’s scattered memories.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I wasn’t going to begin again, having stopped, apparently, and started up again, foolishly, too many times already.” So protests Eve Taggart, the narrator of Sam Savage’s fifth novel, “It Will End With Us.” Like the first-person narrators of Savage’s earlier work, Eve begins with the intention, or at least the possibility, of ending in mind. This awareness that the narrative may be abandoned gives the book the feel of being cobweb-slight. It seems arbitrary, almost incidental; yet the text’s fragility reads as purity of a kind.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘It Will End With Us,’ by Sam Savage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Like Her Mother Before Her', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Savage, Sam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'It Will End With Us (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenny Hendrix', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hendrix', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/214e2857-1334-5809-8ac7-76ead3af4c73', 'word_count': 705, 'uri': 'nyt://article/214e2857-1334-5809-8ac7-76ead3af4c73'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of the socialite Kate Chase Sprague, daughter of a cabinet minister, wife of a governor, mistress of a senator.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/american-queen-by-john-oller.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of the socialite Kate Chase Sprague, daughter of a cabinet minister, wife of a governor, mistress of a senator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the prelude to her great novel “Middlemarch,” George Eliot meditates on the fate of heroic women “who found for themselves no epic life” equal to their passion and intellect, but instead “only a life of mistakes, the offspring of a certain spiritual grandeur ill-matched with the meanness of opportunity.” One wonders what Eliot would have made of Kate Chase Sprague — daughter of a Civil War-era American cabinet minister, wife of a governor, mistress of a senator — whose career as a \xadbehind-the-scenes kingmaker is chronicled in John Oller’s “American Queen.” Beautiful, wealthy and clever, she could dazzle a Washington salon or (said one historian) “scheme like a cigar-\xadchewing convention rigger.” But her horizons were limited by the same constraints that tied Eliot’s heroine Dorothea Brooke, and the results for her bordered on the tragic.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘American Queen,’ by John Oller', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Belle of Washington', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oller, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "American Queen: The Rise and Fall of Kate Chase Sprague, Civil War 'Belle of the North' and Gilded Age Woman of Scandal (Book)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chase Sprague, Kate (1840-99)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Vaill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vaill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7baeb02a-8d23-56be-b5a9-f24c565a011e', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7baeb02a-8d23-56be-b5a9-f24c565a011e'}, {'abstract': 'A poet and Army veteran presents vignettes that draw on his family history and his deployments, especially in Iraq.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/my-life-as-a-foreign-country-by-brian-turner.html', 'snippet': 'A poet and Army veteran presents vignettes that draw on his family history and his deployments, especially in Iraq.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a persistent idea in our culture that what we experience is “true,” while what we imagine is “untrue.” But without exploring the possibility of imagination in nonfiction, we leave out a fundamental part of the human experience — digressive wanderings, the chaotic interior self and, most important, our empathy. Empathy, after all, starts as an act of fiction. We must think ourselves into the lives of others. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSPERCY/0111-bks-Percy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSPERCY/0111-bks-Percy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 766}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSPERCY/0111-bks-Percy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSPERCY/0111-bks-Percy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1604, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSPERCY/11BKSPERCY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSPERCY/11BKSPERCY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSPERCY/11BKSPERCY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘My Life as a Foreign Country,’ by Brian Turner', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The War Every Soldier Brings Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turner, Brian (1967- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'My Life as a Foreign Country (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jen Percy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Percy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3684699f-be75-581c-94e8-5ffa9b07b448', 'word_count': 1119, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3684699f-be75-581c-94e8-5ffa9b07b448'}, {'abstract': 'A study of cowardice, and of how it’s been understood and misunderstood through history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/cowardice-a-brief-history-by-chris-walsh.html', 'snippet': 'A study of cowardice, and of how it’s been understood and misunderstood through history.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Cowardice” is not quite one of those words, like “honor,” whose meaning was once understood by everyone but is now understood by almost no one. Yet its meaning has lately become more elusive. Are suicide bombers “cowardly,” as we are so often assured they are, or insanely courageous? Is it more cowardly to refuse to fight or to fight for fear of being called a coward for not fighting? Some people claim to have the answers to such questions about this once familiar and unproblematic subject, but they tend to disagree. There is no consensus as to who is a coward and who is not — or even about whether the question is one of any importance. Cowardice, whatever it means, must seem a matter of individual choice, like everything else, and the implied judgment made by the term probably requires that we decline to use it at all.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSBOWMAN/0111-bks-Bowman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 738, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSBOWMAN/0111-bks-Bowman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 738}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSBOWMAN/0111-bks-Bowman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 833, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSBOWMAN/0111-bks-Bowman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1756, 'width': 1427, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSBOWMAN/11BKSBOWMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSBOWMAN/11BKSBOWMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BKSBOWMAN/11BKSBOWMAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Cowardice: A Brief History,’ by Chris Walsh', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Run Away', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walsh, Chris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Cowardice: A Brief History (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Bowman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bowman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8443786c-b16c-5131-9e29-36985754d327', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8443786c-b16c-5131-9e29-36985754d327'}, {'abstract': 'Northerners applauded individual high-achieving African-Americans but opposed collective civil rights agendas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/all-eyes-are-upon-us-by-jason-sokol.html', 'snippet': 'Northerners applauded individual high-achieving African-Americans but opposed collective civil rights agendas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If, as many believe, America’s experiment in postracialism is over, then “All Eyes Are Upon Us” is a prescient book that offers a great deal to explain a national self-deception of stunning brevity. According to Jason Sokol, whose anecdotally rich first book, “There Goes My Everything,” tracked white Southerners variously coping in the civil rights era, historians have paid insufficient attention to the Janus-faced \xadresponses of white Northerners to the struggles of black Americans. To be sure, monographs by James Goodman and Thomas Sugrue have explored the dark side of Northern race relations. They found that although the dominant racial philosophies of whites in the North and South were antithetical, opportunity for a majority of black men and women in the North was not very different from what it was in the South.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11lewis/11lewis-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 458, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11lewis/11lewis-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 458}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11lewis/11lewis-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 782, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11lewis/11lewis-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1564, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11lewis/11lewis-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11lewis/11lewis-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11lewis/11lewis-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘All Eyes Are Upon Us,’ by Jason Sokol', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Segregated North', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sokol, Jason', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'All Eyes Are Upon Us: Race and Politics From Boston to Brooklyn (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Levering Lewis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'Levering', 'lastname': 'Lewis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/182870a6-07c4-50b7-bebb-6b006df331e9', 'word_count': 1285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/182870a6-07c4-50b7-bebb-6b006df331e9'}, {'abstract': 'Two couples are left rudderless in the aftermath of World War I in Frances Itani’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/tell-by-frances-itani.html', 'snippet': 'Two couples are left rudderless in the aftermath of World War I in Frances Itani’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here be dragons. There are the stories we tell our friends, the versions we tell our partners and, most important, the stories we tell ourselves in order to keep on living.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Tell,’ by Frances Itani', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Secret Language', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Itani, Frances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Tell (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kimberly Elkins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kimberly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elkins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2382dcbd-0bed-5a37-acc1-9c888e9e0fe2', 'word_count': 674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2382dcbd-0bed-5a37-acc1-9c888e9e0fe2'}, {'abstract': 'Reporters at The Times recommend books about health care.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/health-care-as-high-drama.html', 'snippet': 'Reporters at The Times recommend books about health care.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With “America’s Bitter Pill,” Steven Brill’s new book, on this week’s cover, we asked reporters at The Times with experience covering health care to name a favorite book on the subject.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/0111bksOpenbook/0111bksOpenbook-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 240, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/0111bksOpenbook/0111bksOpenbook-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 240}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/0111bksOpenbook/0111bksOpenbook-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/0111bksOpenbook/0111bksOpenbook-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/0111bksOpenbook/0111bksOpenbook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/0111bksOpenbook/0111bksOpenbook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/0111bksOpenbook/0111bksOpenbook-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Health Care as High Drama', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Health Care as High Drama', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d362625-1b12-52b5-9215-c13de3ef07c6', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d362625-1b12-52b5-9215-c13de3ef07c6'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE BROKEN ROAD: From the Iron Gates to Mount Athos, by Patrick Leigh Fermor. Edited By Colin Thubron and Artemis Cooper. (New York Review Books, $17.95.) In 1933, an 18-year-old Leigh Fermor set out on a walk across Europe, from Holland to Istanbul. Decades later, he recounted that life-changing journey in “A Time of Gifts” (1977) and “Between the Woods and the Water” (1986). “The Broken Road,” posthumously published last year, covers the final leg of his travels, through Bulgaria, Romania and Greece. In her absorbing biography Patrick Leigh Fermor: An Adventure (New York Review Books, $18.95), Artemis Cooper adds further details and intrigue to the British wayfarer’s life, including his heroic efforts in occupied Crete during World War II.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfb15112-f693-518d-90d3-d25d785e58d7', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfb15112-f693-518d-90d3-d25d785e58d7'}, {'abstract': 'Tess Gerritsen, whose “Die Again” is No. 5 on the hardcover fiction list, briefly assumed a new role last winter: organic farmer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Tess Gerritsen, whose “Die Again” is No. 5 on the hardcover fiction list, briefly assumed a new role last winter: organic farmer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Home to Roost: Fans of the doctor-turned-romance-novelist-turned-thriller-writer Tess Gerritsen (whose latest Rizzoli and Isles mystery, “Die Again,” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 5) may not know that last winter she briefly assumed yet another role: organic farmer. When Gerritsen’s son wanted a vacation from the farm he and his partner own in Maine, his parents agreed to take over for two weeks. “There’s a comforting rhythm to farming,” Gerritsen wrote on her blog. “Every morning, the cows stand eagerly at the fence, excited to see us bringing hay, and the sheep come stam\xadpeding like hungry children. . . . I’ve found out that I love hanging out with chickens. I’ve learned which hen leaves the flock every morning to hide out with the sheep, and which hen sneaks away to spend time with the outcast rooster. I’ve learned the secret places where those two lay their eggs. I’ve learned that the alpha rooster can be brutal with hens (and I’m going to talk to my son about turning him into coq au vin).” The wry sadism of that last sentence won’t surprise Gerritsen’s readers — in the opening pages of “Die Again,” a murdered taxidermist is discovered “hung and dressed like game” in his garage, with organs discarded in a nearby trash can. The inspiration for the organs, at least, may have come from the farm. “Thanks to my son,” Gerritsen wrote when her stint was done, “I’ve also been blessed with a bountiful supply of free-range eggs and chicken livers. Will all this turn up in a book some day? Trust me, I’m thinking about it.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11list-gerritsen/11list-gerritsen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11list-gerritsen/11list-gerritsen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11list-gerritsen/11list-gerritsen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11list-gerritsen/11list-gerritsen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11list-gerritsen/11list-gerritsen-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c6c1396-1c48-5261-a1ca-ca45a46a2436', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c6c1396-1c48-5261-a1ca-ca45a46a2436'}, {'abstract': 'Exploring the world of archaeology, with tips for making ancient alcoholic drinks and fashioning a butcher blade from obsidian.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/lives-in-ruins-by-marilyn-johnson.html', 'snippet': 'Exploring the world of archaeology, with tips for making ancient alcoholic drinks and fashioning a butcher blade from obsidian.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The past has a way of suddenly speaking to us. When images appear, for example, of human hands that archaeologists say were stenciled 40,000 years ago, a haunted feeling of connection and perspective quickly emerges. But many archaeologists themselves seem to experience this sense of connection more or less continuously. As Marilyn Johnson suggests in “Lives in Ruins,” her lively survey of archaeology and the people who practice it, it fuels their dedication to the job, which they desperately need because the actual work and the challenges involved are pretty continuous as well. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11GLASSIE/11glassie-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11GLASSIE/11glassie-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11GLASSIE/11glassie-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11GLASSIE/11glassie-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11GLASSIE/11GLASSIE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11GLASSIE/11GLASSIE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11GLASSIE/11GLASSIE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Lives in Ruins,’ by Marilyn Johnson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bring Up the Bones', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Marilyn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHAEOLOGY', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Lives in Ruins: Archaeologists and the Seductive Lure of Human Rubble (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Glassie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glassie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1c83a03-6f2d-5f45-bdfc-9a82060b6d5a', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1c83a03-6f2d-5f45-bdfc-9a82060b6d5a'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Tolstoy’s “Anna Karenina” and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/letters-found-in-translation.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Tolstoy’s “Anna Karenina” and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Found in Translation', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: Found in Translation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:12:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85e5a782-f00c-506d-97b8-c27369b34d42', 'word_count': 801, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85e5a782-f00c-506d-97b8-c27369b34d42'}, {'abstract': 'A career-spanning collection of poems from one of Scotland’s most intriguing literary voices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/sailing-the-forest-selected-poems-by-robin-robertson.html', 'snippet': 'A career-spanning collection of poems from one of Scotland’s most intriguing literary voices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s impossible to talk about the poetry of Robin Robertson without talking about sex, but to do so is to slip into perilous waters. And by “waters” I mean a hot tub. Because you can’t bring up sexy poetry without thinking of Roger and Virginia Klarvin, the randy and dithyrambic professors (played by Will Ferrell and Rachel Dratch) who always seemed to be in the middle of a steamy soak on “Saturday Night Live.” If even the most masterly fiction writers botch the job of describing the erotic act, it’s more challenging still for poets. One false move and they run the risk of sounding like Mr. Klarvin, the grandiloquent scholar and “love-ah” who proclaimed, in one episode, that “beneath the surface of these very waters a virtual ant farm of activity is taking place.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/books/review/11gordinier/11gordinier-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 843, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/31/books/review/11gordinier/11gordinier-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 843}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/books/review/11gordinier/11gordinier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 730, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/books/review/11gordinier/11gordinier-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1459, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/books/review/11gordinier/11gordinier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/31/books/review/11gordinier/11gordinier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/31/books/review/11gordinier/11gordinier-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Sailing the Forest: Selected Poems,’ by Robin Robertson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Primordial Impulses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robertson, Robin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Sailing the Forest: Selected Poems (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T17:13:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff26431b-0b63-5359-8a66-e25271d883fe', 'word_count': 962, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff26431b-0b63-5359-8a66-e25271d883fe'}, {'abstract': 'A Civil War veteran finds violence and love along the Western frontier in Robert Bausch’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/books/review/robert-bauschs-far-as-the-eye-can-see.html', 'snippet': 'A Civil War veteran finds violence and love along the Western frontier in Robert Bausch’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bobby Hale, the hero of Robert Bausch’s new novel, is a frontier hustler with a heart of gold. A Civil War veteran with a sketchy past, Hale heads west in 1869 simply because it seems like a good idea: “Everybody said it was the place to be — with free land and open skies.” What everybody doesn’t say is that it’s also a land of treachery, deception, double-dealing, torture, warfare and death. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BARCOTT/11barcott-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 504, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BARCOTT/11barcott-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 504}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BARCOTT/11barcott-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 861, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BARCOTT/11barcott-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1174, 'width': 1397, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BARCOTT/11barcott-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BARCOTT/11barcott-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/books/review/11BARCOTT/11barcott-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert Bausch’s ‘Far as the Eye Can See’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disputed Territories', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value