e', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73da24ee-972f-5f14-991a-d7c65909856a', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73da24ee-972f-5f14-991a-d7c65909856a'}, {'abstract': 'A new exhibition at Mana Contemporary, curated by Jeffrey Deitch, highlights the choreographer’s many collaborations with key art and fashion figures.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/09/karole-armitage-making-art-dance/', 'snippet': 'A new exhibition at Mana Contemporary, curated by Jeffrey Deitch, highlights the choreographer’s many collaborations with key art and fashion figures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly four decades ago, in 1978, a twentysomething dancer and choreographer named Karole Armitage tapped Christian Marclay, then a budding artist studying at Cooper Union, to create the set and costumes for her production “Ne.” “It was kind of like ‘Ne’ was destroying all that there was before, that jubilation, that you can remake the world in your own way,” Armitage remembers. The piece was originally supposed to debut in an East Village squat. “There was no heat, which is why we decided to have bright, fluorescent, fake-fur pants — because we just wanted to keep the dancers warm,” Armitage explains. The squatters changed their minds, so “Ne” eventually premiered at a gymnasium in Chelsea.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The “Punk Ballerina” Karole Armitage Brings Together Lacroix, Gaultier, Koons and More', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMITAGE, KAROLE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Merce', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deitch, Jeffrey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gaultier, Jean Paul', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johns, Jasper', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karan, Donna', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kilimnik, Karen', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lacroix, Christian', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marclay, Christian', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rauschenberg, Robert', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salle, David', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Speliopoulos, Peter', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taaffe, Philip', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Armitage Gone! Dance', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mana Contemporary', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chelsea (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-09T21:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Binlot', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Binlot', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7378737-503e-5dfe-8cbd-7daad356d002', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7378737-503e-5dfe-8cbd-7daad356d002'}, {'abstract': 'A few dozen musicians will gather to pay respects to Charlie Haden in words and music, at Town Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/arts/music/a-memorial-for-a-jazz-great.html', 'snippet': 'A few dozen musicians will gather to pay respects to Charlie Haden in words and music, at Town Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The bassist Charlie Haden, who died last year at 76, was a generous collaborator throughout his expansive jazz career, sowing seeds of deep melody and pliable rapport among several generations of fellow musicians. A few dozen of them will gather to pay respects, in words and music, at his memorial celebration on Tuesday at Town Hall. Organized by his widow, Ruth Cameron, the service will include two of his flagship ensembles: the Liberation Music Orchestra, featuring the composer Carla Bley and, for this occasion, the bassist Steve Swallow; and Quartet West, with Scott Colley on bass. Members of Mr. Haden’s musical family — his son, the singer-songwriter Josh Haden, and his daughters, known as the Haden Triplets —\xa0will appear.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11GUIDE1/11GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 887, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11GUIDE1/11GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 887}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11GUIDE1/11GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 693, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11GUIDE1/11GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1386, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/arts/11GUIDE1/