reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c295ad98-5f01-53e9-9636-5d53795fae2f', 'word_count': 1269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c295ad98-5f01-53e9-9636-5d53795fae2f'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Garcia, the New York City sanitation commissioner, has been savoring her non-snowy Sundays.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/nyregion/a-day-of-calm-for-kathryn-garcia.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Garcia, the New York City sanitation commissioner, has been savoring her non-snowy Sundays.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kathryn Garcia remembers having just four snow days when she was a student in Park Slope in the 1970s. Growing up, she would listen to the radio for the announcements. “They’d go through a laundry list of suburban schools,” Ms. Garcia said. " ‘Mount Vernon, Montclair schools are closed, New York public schools are ...open.’ ” Sigh. Now, as the New York City sanitation commissioner, Ms. Garcia, 44, is the one who advises the Education Department as to whether to call a snow day, consulting with the city’s meteorologists and with three weather services. With the city not seeing its first accumulation until Tuesday, Ms. Garcia has been savoring her non-snowy Sundays. She does not venture far from the Park Slope home she shares with her husband, Jerry Garcia, 45 (he does not play guitar), and their daughter, Anna, 16. Their son, Alex, 18, is a freshman at the University of Pennsylvania.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/nyregion/11ROUTINE1/11ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/nyregion/11ROUTINE1/11ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't