_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/69801b0c-40f3-54ba-b23b-4c1eb178f61d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/69801b0c-40f3-54ba-b23b-4c1eb178f61d'}, {'abstract': 'A contest that broke a sports color line in Nashville in 1965 was played without violence breaking out, and is now a landmark in local history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/10/us/tense-scene-on-basketball-court-50-years-ago-recalls-catholic-role-in-civil-rights.html', 'snippet': 'A contest that broke a sports color line in Nashville in 1965 was played without violence breaking out, and is now a landmark in local history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — In the locker room beneath the packed bleachers of the Municipal Auditorium here, Bill Derrick gathered the basketball players he coached at Father Ryan High School. They recited “The Lord’s Prayer” and said a “Hail Mary,” as they always did before tipoff. Then, with unusual emphasis, Mr. Derrick intoned two or three times, “Lady of victory, pray for us.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tense Scene on Basketball Court 50 Years Ago Recalls Catholic Role in Civil Rights', 'kicker': 'On Religion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tense Scene on Basketball Court 50 Years Ago Recalls Catholic Role in Civil Rights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freedman, Samuel G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nashville (Tenn)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 6, 'major