ted States?', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE only time art ever seems to make news here in the West anymore is when a Pollock or Warhol sells for a sum commensurate with the budget of a “Transformers” film. It seems bizarre, then, to find ourselves grappling with international crises in which art is the issue: the imbroglio involving the Sony movie “The Interview,” the massacre at Charlie Hebdo in Paris. The incomprehension, whether bemused or horrified, that we feel toward people who take up arms against the creators of cartoons or comedies is a chastening reminder that there are still cultures in which art is not a harmless diversion or commodity, but something real and volatile, a potential threat to be violently suppressed. These attacks are, in a way, a savage, atavistic show of respect.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Art Is Dangerous (or Not)', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Art Is Dangerous (or Not)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kreider, Tim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charlie Hebdo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-10T21:22:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Kreider', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kreider', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/23f73e3a-741b-52e7-9099-b817d638398c', 'word_count': 1112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23f73e3a-741b-52e7-9099-b817d638398c'}, {'abstract': 'Phil Jackson took responsibility for the Knicks’ terrible season before the team went on to lose its 15th game in a row, a team record.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/11/sports/basketball/phil-jackson-takes-blame-before-yet-another-knicks-loss.html', 'snippet': 'Phil Jackson took responsibility for the Knicks’ terrible season before the team went on to lose its 15th game in a row, a team record.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is a mea culpa, Phil Jackson said, his tall frame leaning slightly against a lectern Saturday. In 30 minutes, his hapless Knicks were due to tip off against the Charlotte Hornets. Jackson had lost sleep, he said, and it was evident. He wore a black suit and a black-and-gray tie, but none of the 13 N.B.A. championship rings he had won over the years. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/sports/KNICKS/KNICKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 795, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/11/sports/KNICKS/KNICKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 795}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/sports/KNICKS/KNICKS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 774, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/11/sports/KNICKS/KNICKS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1547, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbn