ion work. Red carpet coverage begins at 7.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-11T06:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/c76eae96-f163-50e4-9002-91ce297f02da', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c76eae96-f163-50e4-9002-91ce297f02da'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Stone, who died on Saturday at 77, explored the underside of American life in books populated with pilgrims, agitators and crackpots in search of salvation, or what he called “the numinous.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/12/books/in-robert-stones-books-heroes-hedonism-and-old-fashioned-moralism.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Stone, who died on Saturday at 77, explored the underside of American life in books populated with pilgrims, agitators and crackpots in search of salvation, or what he called “the numinous.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Stone was one of the few writers to capture the apocalyptic madness of America in the 1960s and ’70s, as the country lurched deeper into the jungles of Vietnam and the counterculture party at home turned increasingly nihilistic, as Woodstock gave way to Altamont and as violence — propagated by the likes of both Charles Manson and the Weather Underground — rocked the home front.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'ran