 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert D. McFadden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdd88191-e0b5-5bac-8a98-f83157ff0932', 'word_count': 1123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdd88191-e0b5-5bac-8a98-f83157ff0932'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/12/arts/whats-on-tv-monday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9:15 A.M. (IFC) IGBY GOES DOWN (2002) Kieran Culkin portrays Igby, a 17-year-old military school runaway who camps out in a New York loft as he rebels against his preppy older brother (Ryan Phillippe) and high-strung mother (Susan Sarandon), in this sarcastic comedy written and directed by Burt Steers. Amanda Peet, above with Mr. Culkin, is the heroin-addicted choreographer and trophy girlfriend of Igby’s wealthy godfather and benefactor (Jeff Goldblum) with whom Igby finds solace; Bill Pullman is Igby’s father, in a mental hospital after a schizophrenic breakdown. “Even though the movie drifts uneasily between satire and realism, and its visions of military school and bohemia feel secondhand, it maintains a ruthless emotional honesty,” Stephen Holden wrote in The New York Times. “Ultimately, it gets at something that no other recent American movie has captured quite so acutely: a resentful, lurking disappointment in the good life.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Monday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-12T05:20:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Cultu