ue': 'Swift, Taylor', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tatum, Channing', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallis, Quvenzhane', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weinstein, Harvey', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-12T20:27:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ec0e347-9ad4-58ca-a7e6-b7e5b49e28d0', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ec0e347-9ad4-58ca-a7e6-b7e5b49e28d0'}, {'abstract': 'The men’s wear label is attracting notice for its outrageous subculture-inspired silhouettes.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/12/menswear-designer-martine-rose-london-rihanna/', 'snippet': 'The men’s wear label is attracting notice for its outrageous subculture-inspired silhouettes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since her debut collection in 2007, the London-based men’s wear designer Martine Rose has searched for and celebrated what she calls “an awkwardness that is beautiful in its entirety.” The punk, skinhead, ’90s raver and ’70s S-and-M scenes, to name a few, provide references for her odd proportions (outrageously oversize trousers paired with super-cropped or tight tops) and unexpected fabric combinations. The fashion world has taken notice; so, too, has Rihanna, who has worn Rose’s designs on multiple occasions.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/12/t-magazine/12rose-tudela-1/12rose-tudela-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'lega