'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e50fed09-351f-5a59-8d65-ffdaec2e29e3', 'word_count': 1005, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e50fed09-351f-5a59-8d65-ffdaec2e29e3'}, {'abstract': 'The soprano Angela Meade and the baritone Nicholas Pallesen shared the program during a George London Foundation event at the Morgan Library & Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/arts/the-george-london-foundation-series-featuring-angela-meade.html', 'snippet': 'The soprano Angela Meade and the baritone Nicholas Pallesen shared the program during a George London Foundation event at the Morgan Library & Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a telling moment in her recital on Sunday afternoon at the Morgan Library & Museum when the soprano Angela Meade’s voice did something surprising: It didn’t do what she wanted it to. She was finishing the recitative that leads into “Non mi dir,” one of Donna Anna’s arias from Mozart’s “Don Giovanni,” and she sent up a floating high note, like a gymnast launching herself into the air.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/13/arts/MEADE/MEADE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/13/arts/MEADE/MEADE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/13/arts/MEADE/MEADE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width':