{'abstract': 'The directive, called a red notice, does not compel any nation to arrest the officials, but declares them to be wanted in Ukraine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/world/europe/interpol-calls-for-arrest-of-yanukovych-and-other-former-ukrainian-officials.html', 'snippet': 'The directive, called a red notice, does not compel any nation to arrest the officials, but declares them to be wanted in Ukraine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — Interpol issued a worldwide directive on Monday seeking the arrest of Viktor F. Yanukovych, the former president of Ukraine, and other former senior officials including Mykola Azarov, who was prime minister, on charges of embezzlement and misappropriation of public funds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Interpol Calls for Arrest of Yanukovych and Other Former Ukrainian Officials', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fugitives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Interpol (International Criminal Police Organization)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kiev (Ukraine)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yanukovych, Viktor F', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T01:34:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8f15637-2e9a-5701-bc6f-f051280061cd', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8f15637-2e9a-5701-bc6f-f051280061cd'}, {'abstract': 'A federal appeals court on Monday denied the request by four prisoners in Oklahoma to delay their executions, rejecting their claim that the drugs to be used could result in unconstitutional pain and suffering.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/us/oklahoma-request-to-delay-four-executions-is-denied-.html', 'snippet': 'A federal appeals court on Monday denied the request by four prisoners in Oklahoma to delay their executions, rejecting their claim that the drugs to be used could result in unconstitutional pain and suffering.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal appeals court on Monday denied the request by four prisoners in Oklahoma to delay their executions, now scheduled for this winter, rejecting their claim that the drugs to be used are unreliable and could result in unconstitutional pain and suffering. The decision in the United States Court of Appeals for the 10th Circuit, in Denver, appeared to clear the way for the execution on Thursday of Charles F. Warren, which would be the state’s first since the botched lethal injection of Clayton D. Lockett in April. Defense lawyers argued that the sedative used in Mr. Lockett’s case and planned for Mr. Warner, midazolam, has unpredictable effects. But the state said it would multiply the dosage, and has refined procedures to ensure that the drug is properly administered. While defense lawyers said they would appeal to the Supreme Court, legal experts said there was little chance of success. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oklahoma: Request to Delay Four Executions Is Denied', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oklahoma: Request to Delay Four Executions Is Denied', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eckholm, Erik', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sedatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lockett, Clayton Darrell', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T01:45:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c68468cd-4097-52d9-a901-c2331ac792d7', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c68468cd-4097-52d9-a901-c2331ac792d7'}, {'abstract': 'Despite economic choppiness, the country that built the Great Wall is attaining new superlatives in bridges, tunnels, rail links and other projects of immense size — and expense.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/business/international/in-china-projects-to-make-great-wall-feel-small-.html', 'snippet': 'Despite economic choppiness, the country that built the Great Wall is attaining new superlatives in bridges, tunnels, rail links and other projects of immense size — and expense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DALIAN, China — The plan here seems far-fetched — a $36 billion tunnel that would run twice the length of the one under the English Channel, and bore deep into one of Asia’s active earthquake zones. When completed, it would be the world’s longest underwater tunnel, creating a rail link between two northern port cities.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In China, Projects to Make Great Wall Feel Small ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In China, Projects to Make Great Wall Feel Small', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barboza, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T01:47:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31e8f80b-eced-565f-b2c3-a6f2382e4481', 'word_count': 1344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31e8f80b-eced-565f-b2c3-a6f2382e4481'}, {'abstract': 'A grim reminder of a global struggle adds to the challenge of selecting a jury for Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, the defendant in the 2013 Boston Marathon bombing, analysts said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/us/paris-attacks-expected-to-influence-boston-trial.html', 'snippet': 'A grim reminder of a global struggle adds to the challenge of selecting a jury for Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, the defendant in the 2013 Boston Marathon bombing, analysts said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — A pair of brothers viewed as homegrown Islamic terrorists. Horrible acts of violence. Manhunts that forced the lockdown of entire neighborhoods. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Attacks Expected to Influence Boston Trial', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paris Attacks Expected to Influence Boston Trial', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boston Marathon Bombings (2013)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jury System', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsarnaev, Dzhokhar A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank'