ct', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T11:27:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f8e91b2-506c-5a9b-a66d-335e51f1d387', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f8e91b2-506c-5a9b-a66d-335e51f1d387'}, {'abstract': 'The fashion house’s stealth approach seems to be a master class in how to rebrand under the radar.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/maison-martin-margiela-changed-its-name/', 'snippet': 'The fashion house’s stealth approach seems to be a master class in how to rebrand under the radar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yes, I include myself in the above statement, at least initially (might as well get that out of the way early on). Of all the changes that took place on Monday in London as John Galliano unveiled his new look for the house of Margiela (which was mostly an oldish runway look) and a new look for himself (which involved his take on the Margiela uniform: no makeup — or, most likely, no-makeup makeup — hair pulled back in a low ponytail, dark suit under white lab coat), the new look of the brand’s name passed almost unnoticed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Maison Martin Margiela Changed Its Name, and No One Noticed', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Maison Martin Margiela', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T11:35:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vanessa Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vanessa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0450b4f9-cdb6-5f98-bd01-4ecf1f2993b6', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0450b4f9-cdb6-5f98-bd01-4ecf1f2993b6'}, {'abstract': 'Three coffins draped in the French tricolor were laid out in Paris as tribute was paid to the officers who died. In Jerusalem, four other victims were buried.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/14/world/europe/france-iraq-deployment-debate-terrorist-attack.html', 'snippet': 'Three coffins draped in the French tricolor were laid out in Paris as tribute was paid to the officers who died. In Jerusalem, four other victims were buried.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — On a day of mourning and defiance, brave words and choking sadness, funerals were held Tuesday in Israel and France for some of those who died in the attacks in Paris last week, and French leaders sought to prevent further attacks while reaffirming the values that their country regards as its defining strengths.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'France Mourns Attack Victims and Takes Steps Against Further Terrorism', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'France Mourns Attack Victims and Takes Steps Against Further Terrorism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Funerals and Memorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charlie Hebdo', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brinsolaro, Franck (1965-2015)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merabet, Ahmed (1972-2015)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jean-Philippe, Clarissa (1988-2015)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Attacks on Police', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joachin, Fritz-Joly', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T11:39:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Cowell and Maïa de la Baume', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Maïa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de la Baume', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ef80bb4-170a-5653-8cdd-10ae031bc4fa', 'word_count': 1063, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ef80bb4-170a-5653-8cdd-10ae031bc4fa'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Paris’ only American news vendor committed suicide in 1940.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/1940-newsie-in-paris-jumps-to-death/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Paris’ only American news vendor committed suicide in 1940.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ike Meltzer, 41 years old, who brought a Broadway accent and the American style of selling papers to the ‘‘world’s most famous corner’’ — Place de l’Opéra on the Café de la Paix angle — jumped out of the fifth-floor window of a building in the Rue Caumartin Friday noon. He died three hours later in a hospital from injuries sustained in crashing through the skylight on the rear courtyard. The war cut off his supply of American dailies but, even more important, it kept thousands of tourists, who had been his customers, out of the combat zone. Moreover the news-vendor’s wife was seriously ill in London. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Jan. 14, 1940', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1940: Newsie in Paris Jumps to Death', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T11:58:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76f270cf-86a6-500e-a86a-4043794b6a60', 'word_count': 117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76f270cf-86a6-500e-a86a-4043794b6a60'}, {'abstract': 'The far-right National Front has made gains following the attack on the offices of Charlie Hebdo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/14/opinion/patrick-chappatte-charlie-hebdo-the-news-from-france.html', 'snippet': 'The far-right National Front has made gains following the attack on the offices of Charlie Hebdo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The far-right National Front has made gains following the attack on the offices of Charlie Hebdo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/06/opinion/chappatte-circular/chappatte-circular-articleLarge-v3.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/06/opinion/chappatte-circular/chappatte-circular-articleLarge-v3.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'x