': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oil Output Seen Rising, Despite Fall in Its Price', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krauss, Clifford', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Production', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T12:13:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clifford Krauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clifford', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4597b9bc-9ea4-5666-ab52-f28e0fb16ff3', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4597b9bc-9ea4-5666-ab52-f28e0fb16ff3'}, {'abstract': 'S.&P. is nearing a settlement with the Justice Department over inflated ratings. | Antonio Weiss withdraws from consideration to be a Treasury under secretary. | Oil prices plunge. | Ethical questions of investing in pot.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/morning-agenda-s-p-seeks-peace/', 'snippet': 'S.&P. is nearing a settlement with the Justice Department over inflated ratings. | Antonio Weiss withdraws from consideration to be a Treasury under secretary. | Oil prices plunge. | Ethical questions of investing in pot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Standard & Poor’s has publicly fought a Justice Department lawsuit, but behind the scenes, the bond-ratings agency now wants peace, Ben Protess reports in DealBook. “After S.&P. mounted a two-year campaign to defeat civil fraud charges ‒ portraying them as retaliation for cutting the credit rating of the United States ‒ the ratings agency is now negotiating with the Justice Department to settle the case, according to people briefed on the matter,” he writes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: S.&P. Seeks Peace', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T12:24:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3870458b-22d6-5bc5-ac42-5addb96b37c9', 'word_count': 1933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3870458b-22d6-5bc5-ac42-5addb96b37c9'}, {'abstract': 'The designation is meant to identify big players who could gridlock markets. But MetLife’s chief said the insurer was “simply not that interconnected” to other financial firms.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/metlife-to-fight-too-big-to-fail-status-in-court/', 'snippet': 'The designation is meant to identify big players who could gridlock markets. But MetLife’s chief said the insurer was “simply not that interconnected” to other financial firms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MetLife sued a panel of regulators led by the Treasury secretary on Tuesday to fight its designation as “systemically important,” making it the first financial company to go to court over the issue since the government started singling out so-called too-big-to-fail institutions in an effort to stem any future financial crises.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'x