te': '2015-01-13T20:16:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ec377fc-b9b2-5564-9986-67d80b5e16d1', 'word_count': 7, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ec377fc-b9b2-5564-9986-67d80b5e16d1'}, {'abstract': '“The New York School, 1969: Henry Geldzahler at the Metropolitan Museum of Art,” includes many works curated by Geldzahler in his trailblazing contemporary exhibition.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/13/henrys-show-new-york-school-met-reprisal-paul-kasmin-gallery/', 'snippet': '“The New York School, 1969: Henry Geldzahler at the Metropolitan Museum of Art,” includes many works curated by Geldzahler in his trailblazing contemporary exhibition.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’m Henry — just Henry.” Indeed, that sensibility served Henry Geldzahler well. The charismatic curator, short in stature and large in vitality, in 1969 convinced that stodgiest of institutions — the Metropolitan Museum of Art — to, for a blockbuster exhibition, swap its Rembrandts and Renoirs for Rosenquists and Rauschenbergs. Formally titled “New York Painting and Sculpture: 1940-1970,” the exhibition was colloquially dubbed “Henry’s Show,” which stuck in the history books as its proper name. That show contained nearly 410 works by more than 40 artists — the very catalysts of contemporary art whose fabled exploits, fueled by creativity, community and feuds, composed the history of the New York School. And many of these artists counted Geldzahler as a close personal friend. Frank Stella famously said, “The thing about Henry was that he lived with us. Other curators don’t live with artists.” Another thing about Geldzahler, according to David Hockney: “Henry had a fantastic eye. He wasn’t wrong about anything. He was always right.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/13/t-magazine/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/13/t-magazine/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 722}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/13/t-magazine/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 851, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/13/t-magazine/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1702, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/13/t-magazine/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/13/t-magazine/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/13/t-magazine/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX/13symonds-henry-slide-MKHX-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Met’s Groundbreaking “Henry’s Show” Gets a Reprise'