: None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-13T22:18:22+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/2dfb8678-b1ad-5f58-a2a1-9c790cbc0efb', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/2dfb8678-b1ad-5f58-a2a1-9c790cbc0efb'}, {'abstract': 'Three cardinals take the place of the puppets in their traveling theater to teach the history of Christianity in “The Cardinals.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/14/theater/the-cardinals-at-the-public-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Three cardinals take the place of the puppets in their traveling theater to teach the history of Christianity in “The Cardinals.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The long, tumultuous history of Christianity reels by like a silly cartoon in “The Cardinals,” an uninspired and interminable show being presented as part of the Under the Radar festival. The concept behind the production, which comes from a theater troupe called Stan’s Cafe of Birmingham, England, posits that three cardinals are “on an evangelical mission to broaden knowledge of the Bible — through their traveling puppet theater,” according to a note on the Public Theater website. Alas, the puppets get lost in transit, so the cardinals must jump into the fray to enact famous scenes from the Old and New Testaments.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/14/arts/CARDINALS/CARDINALS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/14/arts/CARDINALS/CARDINALS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 369}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/