one, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f38c26a1-e546-58ce-864d-398de5ceff8c', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f38c26a1-e546-58ce-864d-398de5ceff8c'}, {'abstract': 'Jennifer Nocon’s solo show “You See Ocean I See Sky” opens tomorrow and uses pattern to reflect human behavior —\xa0and the possibility of change.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/14/jennifer-nocon-tracy-williams-artist-favored-by-the-u-s-embassy-in-pakistan/', 'snippet': 'Jennifer Nocon’s solo show “You See Ocean I See Sky” opens tomorrow and uses pattern to reflect human behavior —\xa0and the possibility of change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though plenty of ink is spilled about the intersection of politics and aesthetics, less is said about the artwork hanging in the rooms where statesmanship is actually conducted. Recently, one of the most geopolitically sensitive locations in the world, the U.S. embassy in Islamabad, Pakistan, reserved a multimedia wall piece and watercolors by the Los Angeles-based artist Jennifer Nocon, whose solo show “You See Ocean I See Sky” opens tomorrow in New York. Assembled from wool, clay and steel, standout pieces in the exhibition combine the artist’s signature motifs, branching tendrils and small scales, layered into a gathering storm of color.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Artist Favored by the U.S. Embassy in Pakistan', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Islamabad (Pakistan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-14T14:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Su Wu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Su', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d144307f-1996-5fd8-8545-359e878081a9', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d144307f-1996-5fd8-8545-359e878081a9'}, {'abstract': 'The 2.37-square-mile Village of Westbury is known for its economic diversity, shopping convenience and choice of public and parochial schools.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/realestate/westbury-ny-a-melting-pot-village.html', 'snippet': 'The 2.37-square-mile Village of Westbury is known for its economic diversity, shopping convenience and choice of public and parochial schools.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joe Moran, an executive at a nonprofit organization, worried whether he and his partner, Tom Verni, a retired New York City police detective, would find acceptance as they searched for a home on Long Island in the fall of 2012. They were then living in Hell’s Kitchen in Manhattan. “I kind of went kicking and screaming” on the house hunt, Mr. Moran said.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Westbury, N.Y., a ‘Melting Pot’ Village', 'kicker': 'Living In', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A ‘Melting Pot’ Village', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fischler, Marcelle Sussman', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westbury (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-14T14:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marcelle Sussman Fischler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marcelle', 'middlename': 'Sussman', 'lastname': 'Fischler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b41a939-a6c2-5280-811c-083b7daddb89', 'word_count': 1302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b41a939-a6c2-5280-811c-083b7daddb89'}, {'abstract': 'Momentum is growing for a plan to combine seven ski resorts in Utah, but opposition from environmentalists also continues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/travel/in-salt-lake-city-a-proposal-to-link-ski-trails.html', 'snippet': 'Momentum is growing for a plan to combine seven ski resorts in Utah, but opposition from environmentalists also continues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps the biggest news lately in North American skiing has been the announcement in 2014 that seven ski resorts perched high above Salt Lake City hope to join hands to create the largest megaresort in North America. OneWasatch, as it’s being billed, would be a colossus, with one ski pass linking more than 100 lifts across Utah’s Wasatch Mountains, more than double the acreage of Whistler-Blackcomb in Canada, currently the continent’s largest ski resort. Its backers say OneWasatch would offer an experience akin to Europe’s linked valley-to-valley ski systems like Arlberg in Austria or Trois Vallées in France.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/travel/SUB18GETAWAY-copy/SUB18GETAWAY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/18/travel/SUB18GETAWAY-copy/SUB18GETAWAY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/travel/SUB18GETAWAY-copy/SUB18GETAWAY-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non