6df1e2f1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/745f6395-3de0-59cb-b308-aeee6df1e2f1'}, {'abstract': 'Beyond blues, barbecue and Elvis, you can find zydeco and indie rock, newly vibrant neighborhoods and a refurbished civil rights museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/travel/what-to-do-in-36-hours-memphis.html', 'snippet': 'Beyond blues, barbecue and Elvis, you can find zydeco and indie rock, newly vibrant neighborhoods and a refurbished civil rights museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe even more Music City than its country cousin to the east, Memphis has strong affiliations with not one musical genre but three. It is the birthplace of rock ‘n’ roll, the cradle of soul and, though the blues weren’t born here (that distinction goes to the rural parts of the Mississippi Delta), it was the Memphis-based musician and “Father of the Blues,” W. C. Handy, who helped get those “lonesome songs” out into the world. But there is far more to Bluff City than blues and barbecue. A watermelon and feta salad can be found alongside great smokehouse meats; zydeco and indie rock are within earshot of soul; and up-and-coming areas like Cooper-Young are giving the city’s older haunts some stiff competition. For this short visit, leave out the Graceland juggernaut, though if Elvis is on your bucket list, head out any morning to Graceland’s Meditation Garden where the King is buried — it’s peaceful and free.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Memphis', 'kicker': '36 Hours', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Memphis (Tenn)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars and Nightclubs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Presley, Elvis', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Civil Rights Museum', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-14T18:06:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colleen Creamer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colleen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Creamer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/359e4fee-f9d4-58fb-927b-3c13157872f9', 'word_count': 1554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/359e4fee-f9d4-58fb-927b-3c13157872f9'}, {'abstract': 'Satire and self-censorship around religion, from Charlie Hebdo to The Book of Mormon.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/14/blasphemy-revisited-i/', 'snippet': 'Satire and self-censorship around religion, from Charlie Hebdo to The Book of Mormon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My post last week in the immediate aftermath of the Charlie Hebdo murders received an unusual amount of favorable feedback from readers of varying persuasions, for which I’m grateful. It was also written in a mood that was perhaps not completely\xa0nuanced and reflective, so I want to return to the issues now in the hopes of\xa0both clarifying\xa0and qualifying certain elements of the argument. This will be the first of at least two\xa0posts trying to do just that.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Blasphemy Revisited', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians