to make it easier for local governments to get into the broadband business.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/14/opinion/1142015obama/1142015obama-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/14/opinion/1142015obama/1142015obama-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/14/opinion/1142015obama/1142015obama-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/14/opinion/1142015obama/1142015obama-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1373, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/14/opinion/1142015obama/1142015obama-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/14/opinion/1142015obama/1142015obama-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/14/opinion/1142015obama/1142015obama-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting Government Into the Broadband Internet Business', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Communications Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-14T20:50:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vikas Bajaj', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vikas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bajaj', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e606c011-51ae-5e00-9ce0-950ea1da71ff', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e606c011-51ae-5e00-9ce0-950ea1da71ff'}, {'abstract': 'The public editor hears from readers, many of them, about The Times’s decision not to publish the Charlie Hebdo cartoons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2015/01/14/readers-sound-off-to-public-editor-on-decision-not-to-publish-cartoons/', 'snippet': 'The public editor hears from readers, many of them, about The Times’s decision not to publish the Charlie Hebdo cartoons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve seen intense response from Times readers before. When I wrote about the test drive of a Tesla electric car, or Nate Silver’s offer to place a bet on his prognostications, hundreds of readers commented and emailed. When I wrote about coverage of the Israeli-Palestinian conflict, readers certainly let me know what they thought.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Readers Sound Off to Public Editor on Decision Not to Publish Cartoons', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silver, Nate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charlie Hebdo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-14T20:50:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline