e': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Sanctions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-15T08:57:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/46c6f310-ec72-5486-9c63-240eae7c1838', 'word_count': 665, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46c6f310-ec72-5486-9c63-240eae7c1838'}, {'abstract': 'Albany’s treasury is fat with the state’s share of fines, which should go to carefully chosen, one-time investments. Here are a few ideas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/15/opinion/how-to-spend-new-yorks-spare-dollar5-billion.html', 'snippet': 'Albany’s treasury is fat with the state’s share of fines, which should go to carefully chosen, one-time investments. Here are a few ideas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s a scary thought: New York State’s politicians suddenly have an extra $5 billion to spend. Albany’s treasury is fat with the state’s share of fines paid by financial institutions for past misconduct. And in all but a few cases, there are no real strings attached. So the scrum has started, with virtually every politician and interest group and government entity scrambling for a share of the windfall.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/15/opinion/15thu3/15thu3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/15/opinion/15thu3/15thu3-articl