_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Experiment Must Go On', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Experiment Must Go on', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Talking Band', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Golden Toad (Play)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-15T17:44:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Collins-Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins-Hughes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23d47791-6d5e-5c82-9718-f29dcc1fe273', 'word_count': 1333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23d47791-6d5e-5c82-9718-f29dcc1fe273'}, {'abstract': 'Israel’s response to the Charlie Hebdo attack has touched off a divisive new chapter in the war of words between allies turned rivals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/16/world/middleeast/netanyahu-charlie-hebdo-rally-appearance-prompts-turkey-dispute.html', 'snippet': 'Israel’s response to the Charlie Hebdo attack has touched off a divisive new chapter in the war of words between allies turned rivals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — If the Paris attacks unleashed a powerful demonstration of unity, with world leaders gathering to denounce terrorism, they also touched off a divisive new chapter in the war of words between Turkey and Israel, once stalwart allies but now bitter rivals.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Killings Deepen Rift as Turks Accuse Netanyahu of ‘Crimes Against Humanity’ ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Killings Deepen Rift as Turks Accuse Netanyahu of ‘Crimes Against Humanity’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arango, Tim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davutoglu, Ahmet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charlie Hebdo', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erdogan, Recep Tayyip', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-15T17:50:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Arango', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arango', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e77e7af3-7a38-53b7-91a3-48c1720319a1', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e77e7af3-7a38-53b7-91a3-48c1720319a1'}, {'abstract': 'Irving Howe was one of the greatest critics of his generation, a perspicacious reader of difficult texts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/books/review/a-voice-still-heard-selected-essays-of-irving-howe.html', 'snippet': 'Irving Howe was one of the greatest critics of his generation, a perspicacious reader of difficult texts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A voice still heard. What a sad, anxious assertion of the great critic’s relevance. Alas, the claim is not terribly true, not anymore. A generation ago, Irving Howe’s voice rattled around the brain of the American intellectual. When writers sat down to compose essays, they were already imagining the postcard of praise they might receive from Howe. A small legion of his enemies made careers of baiting him. Howe’s hot ire flowed easily, often in the form of elegant sarcasm that could bury alive.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18FOER/18foer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 889, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18FOER/18foer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 889}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18FOER/18foer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 692, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18FOER/18foer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1383, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18FOER/18FOER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18FOER/18FOER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18FOER/18FOER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Voice Still Heard: Selected Essays of Irving Howe’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Partisan Reviewer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foer, Franklin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howe, Irving', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Voice Still Heard: Selected Essays of Irving Howe (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-15T18:08:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Franklin Foer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Franklin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Foer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/79cfc9c2-026b-5f05-b367-73a9f91fc970', 'word_count': 1253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79cfc9c2-026b-5f05-b367-73a9f91fc970'}, {'abstract': 'Michel Houellebecq’s new novel imagines France in 2022, when a Muslim political party has won the presidency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/books/review/houellebecq-provokes-again.html', 'snippet': 'Michel Houellebecq’s new novel imagines France in 2022, when a Muslim political party has won the presidency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The French novelist Michel Houellebecq is a longtime friend of controversy, which has done at least as much as critical praise to sell his books. In the Book Review in 2000, Anthony Quinn called his novel “The Elementary Particles” a “bilious, hysterical and oddly juvenile” book, the tone of which “lurches unpleasantly between the salacious and the psychotic.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18ope