549a-8cc9-c3b8257084f5'}, {'abstract': 'Why should we pretend that aesthetic merit has anything to do with the nomination process?', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/15/lets-retire-the-term-oscar-worthy/', 'snippet': 'Why should we pretend that aesthetic merit has anything to do with the nomination process?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences routinely dispenses Oscars to mediocre performances and snubs terrific ones; the catalog of egregious choices (“Forrest Gump” beat out “Pulp Fiction” for best picture in 1995, just for example) is long. Everyone knows that. It’s also widely acknowledged that roll-out and publicity, the quality of the campaign a studio runs, matters as much or more than the quality of a film. A good politician with a bad communications team probably can’t become president; a good actor with a bad communications team probably can’t win an Oscar.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/15/opinion/1152015selma/1152015selma-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/15/opinion/1152015selma/1152015selma-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/15/opinion/1152015selma/1152015selma-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 625, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/15/opinion/1152015selma/1152015selma-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1251, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/15/opinion/1152015selma/115