f speech.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/17/world/asia/south-korean-journalists-acquitted-on-charges-of-defaming-presidents-brother.html', 'snippet': 'The case against the two, who speculated that Park Geun-hye’s brother might have been involved in a murder, was seen by rights groups as a test of freedom of speech.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — Two journalists who have been vocal critics of South Korea’s president were acquitted Friday on charges of defaming her brother, in a case that rights groups saw as a test of freedom of speech here.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Korean Journalists Acquitted on Charges of Defaming President’s Brother', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Korea: 2 Journalists Cleared in Defamation Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Park Geun-hye', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Choo Chin-woo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Park Ji-man', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libel and Slander', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-16T08:47:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed205544-08db-5b83-bd76-59576361447d', 'word_count': 425, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed205544-08db-5b83-bd76-59576361447d'}, {'abstract': 'DVD vendors said police officers were sometimes accompanied by North Korean officials as they visited shops to seize copies of the controversial film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/17/world/asia/north-korean-hand-suspected-as-myanmar-sweeps-up-interview-bootlegs.html', 'snippet': 'DVD vendors said police officers were sometimes accompanied by North Korean officials as they visited shops to seize copies of the controversial film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YANGON, Myanmar — Long before Sony Pictures released “The Interview,” a comedy about a fictitious C.I.A. plot to kill North Korea’s leader, Pyongyang had declared war against the movie. It has since been blamed for a cyberattack on Sony’s computers. Now, it appears that the country’s famously touchy diplomats have taken the dispute much farther afield: to the streets of Yangon, Myanmar.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Myanmar Sweeps Up Copies of Film Mocking Kim Jong-un', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Myanmar Sweeps Up Copies of Film Mocking Kim Jong-un', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong-un', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DVD (Digital Versatile Disc)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yangon (Myanmar)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search and Seizure', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Pictures Entertainment', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Il', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Interview (Movie)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-16T08:49:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wai Moe and Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wai', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85537c55-fbda-5224-914c-bcbba5bac49f', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85537c55-fbda-5224-914c-bcbba5bac49f'}, {'abstract': 'The governor could play a useful role in the tensions between the city’s police and Mayor Bill de Blasio, but much depends on how he acts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/16/opinion/gov-cuomo-on-new-york-citys-woes.html', 'snippet': 'The governor could play a useful role in the tensions between the city’s police and Mayor Bill de Blasio, but much depends on how he acts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent weeks, Gov. Andrew Cuomo has repeatedly noted his concern about the unhealthy tensions in New York City between the police and Mayor Bill de Blasio. He also sees an alluring political vacuum tempting him to step forward as an impartial healer and arbitrator. “I think everybody in New York City knows that this situation is unsustainable,” he said on Wednesday morning.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/16fri2/16fri2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/16fri2/16fri2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/16fri2/16fri