{'abstract': 'A new Royal Opera House production of Monteverdi’s “Orfeo,” directed by Michael Boyd, ventures out of Covent Garden.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/17/arts/music/a-new-orfeo-steps-off-the-pedestal.html', 'snippet': 'A new Royal Opera House production of Monteverdi’s “Orfeo,” directed by Michael Boyd, ventures out of Covent Garden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — A modern curiosity of opera is that we spend vast amounts on theaters customized with all the comforts and amenities the art form needs, only to get excited by the prospect of performances in train sheds. It is the thrill of living rough. And it explains why the operatic event of the moment in London is a new Covent Garden production of Monteverdi’s “Orfeo” that is not actually playing at Covent Garden but at the Roundhouse: a cavernous industrial building in North London’s edgy Camden Town that once held trains but now holds music, though not often music of this kind.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/17/arts/17iht-orfeo17b/17iht-orfeo17b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 506, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/17/arts/17iht-orfeo17b/17iht-orfeo17b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 506}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/17/arts/17iht-orfeo17b/17iht-orfeo17b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 863, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/17/arts/17iht-orfeo17b/17iht-orfeo17b-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1726, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/17/arts/17iht-orfeo17b/17iht-orfeo17b-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/17/arts/17iht-orfeo17b/17iht-orfeo17b-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/17/arts/17iht-orfeo17b/17iht-orfeo17b-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A New ‘Orfeo’ Steps Off the Pedestal', 'kicker': 'Opera Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In an Unlikely London Setting, a New ‘Orfeo’ Steps Off the Pedestal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Opera House', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Monteverdi, Claudio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyd, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Orfeo (Opera)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-16T15:23:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael White', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'White', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9248f188-0f14-55c6-8e87-16472a51974f', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9248f188-0f14-55c6-8e87-16472a51974f'}, {'abstract': 'Great shows used to be about fallen masculinity. Now they’re about collaboration and resilience. What changed?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/magazine/tvs-new-girls-club.html', 'snippet': 'Great shows used to be about fallen masculinity. Now they’re about collaboration and resilience. What changed?', 'lead_paragraph': "There is a moment in HBO’s “Doll & Em” when Em, a movie star, leaves her trailer to rehearse her lines alone. Doll, Em’s childhood best friend and now her assistant, stays behind. In an echo of “All About Eve,” Doll picks up her friend’s script and starts reading aloud. “\u2009'Why didn’t you call me?’ ‘I didn’t think it was my place,'\u2009” she says, intoning both parts and knowing she’s overstepping. The script is terrible. It’s derivative. But it’s a transgressive moment for Doll and a distillation of her complicated envy toward Em. The sharp parody happening in that scene — of great films, of female friendship, of Hollywood’s idea of better roles for women — coexists with an air of unstudied sincerity that’s in fact subversive. It’s a mood typical of the show and bespeaks a broader sophistication that has finally found its way onto the small screen, over a decade into what we’ve so often heard is a TV renaissance.", 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18riff/18mag-18riff.t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 124, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18riff/18mag-18riff.t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 124}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18riff/18mag-18riff.t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 212, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18riff/18mag-18riff.t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18riff/18mag-18riff.t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18riff/18mag-18riff.t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18riff/18mag-18riff.t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'TV’s New Girls’ Club', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Girls’ Club', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Loofbourow, Lili', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Doll & Em (TV Program)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Breaking Bad (TV Program)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Sopranos (TV Program)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-16T15:25:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lili Loofbourow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lili', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Loofbourow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cdfce2f-8e7a-5d68-a9ae-3a80d8fb9d16', 'word_count': 1554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cdfce2f-8e7a-5d68-a9ae-3a80d8fb9d16'}, {'abstract': 'By providing my children with a better life, was I also depriving them?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/magazine/happy-meals.html', 'snippet': 'By providing my children with a better life, was I also depriving them?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tujunga is not one of those well-to-do Los Angeles suburbs. It does not have a quaint main street or rows of tidy houses with trim lawns or a view of the ocean. When I was growing up, there were boarded-up storefronts and chain-link fences surrounding monstrous pink apartment buildings. There were abandoned houses and empty lots overrun by weeds.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18lives/18lives-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18lives/18lives-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18lives/18lives-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18lives/18lives-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives.t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives.t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives.t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Happy Meals', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Happy Meals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ellison, Jan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fast Food Industry', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-16T15:25:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jan Ellison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ellison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e270cff-86df-52fc-aaad-bc9e225bf4d1', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e270cff-86df-52fc-aaad-bc9e225bf4d1'}, {'abstract': 'Does life imitate art or does art reflect life?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/magazine/try-this-at-home.html', 'snippet': 'Does life imitate art or does art reflect life?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I live and work in Hollywood. Some of my friends are screenwriters, and they tend to be politically progressive. They often criticize conservatives for ignoring scientific evidence when it conflicts with their politics. But when it comes to the negative effects of violence in the media, my friends dismiss the idea and insist that it is unfair to blame artists for societal issues. When confronted with scientific studies that point to media having an impact on violent behavior, most say that this is merely a reflection of what is already there. But if there is evidence of an effect, even nominal, is it unethical to write (or produce or direct) violent films and shows? NAME WITHHELD, LOS ANGELES', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-articleLarge.png', 'height': 356, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 356}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-jumbo.png', 'height': 607, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1215, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Try Th