{'abstract': 'In these stories, common domestic dysfunction segues into fantasy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/books/review/j-robert-lennons-see-you-in-paradise.html', 'snippet': 'In these stories, common domestic dysfunction segues into fantasy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first story of “See You in Paradise,” J. Robert Lennon’s funny and poignant new collection, a family discovers a “magic portal” in their backyard. The “oval, sort of man-sized, shimmering thing” not only transports them to other worlds; it also serves as the reader’s entree to the rest of the book, with its array of futuristic and surreal psychic landscapes. The breezy blend of realism and fantasy is characteristic: The narrator, a world-weary dad, describes the first giddy era of exploration, which soon declines into domestic dysfunction as his children hit puberty and his wife grows distant. The portal, mirroring this entropy, becomes “lopsided, with a sort of hernia in the lower left corner,” and the narrator is left bereft and aching, trying to find his way back to some semblance of enchantment. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18elliott-sub/18elliott-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 585, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18elliott-sub/18elliott-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 585}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18elliott-sub/18elliott-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 701, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18elliott-sub/18elliott-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 701, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18elliott-sub/18elliott-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18elliott-sub/18elliott-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18elliott-sub/18elliott-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'J. Robert Lennon’s ‘See You in Paradise’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suburban Dystopia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lennon, J Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'See You in Paradise: Stories (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-16T17:38:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/99898246-3595-5351-b96d-1cb4e0f58f2a', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99898246-3595-5351-b96d-1cb4e0f58f2a'}, {'abstract': 'A perverse houseguest shakes the world of the initially passive protagonist of Miranda July’s first novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/books/review/the-first-bad-man-by-miranda-july.html', 'snippet': 'A perverse houseguest shakes the world of the initially passive protagonist of Miranda July’s first novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cheryl Glickman has a psychosomatic lump in her throat that sometimes prevents her from swallowing. She also has a burning crush on a man named Phillip Bettelheim who wants her blessing before he consummates his love for an underage girl, and a karmic connection with a child she calls Kubelko Bondy, whose spirit continually appears to her in other people’s babies. Cheryl is the protagonist of Miranda July’s very funny debut novel, “The First Bad Man,” and she’s such an acute observer that her life is never as pathetic to the reader as it appears to the people around her. She has developed systems for everything, including a rigid housekeeping asceticism that helps her stave off the kind of debilitating sadness that, if left unchecked, can snowball from letting dirty dishes pile up in the sink to eating off said dirty dishes, to no longer bathing, to urinating in cups because they’re closer to the bed. Her ideal is unruffled contentment. When things really flow, she says, “my days become dreamlike, no edges anywhere, none of the snags and snafus that life is so famous for. After days and days alone it gets silky to the point where I can’t even feel myself anymore, it’s as if I don’t exist.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18GROFF/0118-bks-GROFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18GROFF/0118-bks-GROFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/18/books/review/18GROFF/0118-bks-GROFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 6