_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9144457-5168-5072-84a4-60f56385e287', 'word_count': 1095, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9144457-5168-5072-84a4-60f56385e287'}, {'abstract': 'Humans and fairies, cave men and astronauts: Two fantasy books for young adults move across boundaries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/books/review/the-darkest-part-of-the-forest-and-the-ghosts-of-heaven.html', 'snippet': 'Humans and fairies, cave men and astronauts: Two fantasy books for young adults move across boundaries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As discerning readers are well aware, some of the most interesting fiction currently being written is published under the catchall category of young adult — a term that encompasses such a diversity of work as to be nearly meaningless, but that does suggest a book’s concerns will focus primarily on the doings of adolescent characters (though, as we shall see, even this definition does not always hold true). The young adult fiction \xadpowerhouses Holly Black and Marcus Sedgwick are masters of the genre, with well-deserved reputations for pushing it to its limits, and both writers’ newest books ably demonstrate that the line between young and adult is fluid indeed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Darkest Part of the Forest’ and ‘The Ghosts of Heaven’', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strange Places', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Holly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sedgwick, Marcus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Ghosts of Heaven (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Darkest Part of the Forest (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-16T17:51:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah McCarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCarry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/74533825-abbb-5a19-811c-8b28ef273efe', 'word_count': 656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74533825-abbb-5a19-811c-8b28ef273efe'}, {'abstract': 'In Jennifer Niven’s young adult debut, a suicidal teenager shows a grief-stricken girl how to live again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/books/review/jennifer-nivens-all-the-bright-places.html', 'snippet': 'In Jennifer Niven’s young adult debut, a suicidal teenager shows a grief-stricken girl how to live again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the opening moments of Jennifer Niven’s “All the Bright Places,” a 12th grader named Theodore Finch poses on the narrow sixth-story ledge of his school’s bell tower, arms extended, welcoming the spectators below to his death scene. And then he notices the popular Violet Markey on the same ledge, terrified, thinking about taking a leap of her own.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jennifer Niven’s ‘All the Bright Places’', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Picking Themselves Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Niven, Jennifer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'All the Bright Places (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-16T17:51:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/61c3e97d-edad-5cee-81a9-af0e1ac1b1ec', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61c3e97d-edad-5cee-81a9-af0e1ac1b1ec'}, {'abstract': 'The head of the Central Board of Film Certification said government ministers regularly telephoned board members to press them to change decisions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/17/world/asia/leela-samson-india-film-censor-resigns.html', 'snippet': 'The head of the Central Board of Film Certification said government ministers regularly telephoned board members to press them to change decisions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MUMBAI — India’s chief film censor, Leela Samson, resigned this week, saying she could no longer tolerate the interference of high-ranking government officials in her work. A second member of the film certification board followed suit on Friday, also citing interference.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'India Film Censor Resigns, Complaining of Government Interference', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Samson, Leela (1951- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-16T17:54:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neha Thirani Bagri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neha', 'middlename': 'Thirani', 'lastname': 'Bagri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 