c9f-da39-5ee8-9665-7be0eaf97c06', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c133c9f-da39-5ee8-9665-7be0eaf97c06'}, {'abstract': 'Pecknold’s latest project isn’t an album but a score —\xa0for “Wyoming,” an off-Broadway family drama written by his cousin Brian Watkins.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/16/robin-pecknold-fleet-foxes-wyoming-theater/', 'snippet': 'Pecknold’s latest project isn’t an album but a score —\xa0for “Wyoming,” an off-Broadway family drama written by his cousin Brian Watkins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With a world’s worth of inspiration at a writer’s fingertips, real creativity often comes from constraints. That’s true at least for the musician Robin Pecknold, best known as the principal songwriter for Fleet Foxes, the pop-folk band that got a Grammy nomination and a top-five debut with its last album, “Helplessness Blues,” in 2012. “Too much freedom can be creatively paralyzing,” Pecknold says. He’s currently preparing for his musical return: the score for the off-Broadway play “Wyoming,” which he co-composed with Neal Morgan, who has been the drummer for Joanna Newsom and Bill Callahan. “With the score, I had the ability to play with genre without worrying about appropriation,” Pecknold says. “There is less of the self in it, compared to writing an album.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/16/t-magazine/16foxes-muller/16foxes-muller-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/16/t-magazine/16foxes-muller/16foxes-muller-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/16/t-magazine/16foxes-muller/16foxes-muller-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo',