, 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/593ca559-4a2d-5c31-9751-b6ae451afbaf', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/593ca559-4a2d-5c31-9751-b6ae451afbaf'}, {'abstract': 'In his native Panama, the pianist Danilo Pérez spearheads a festival, a foundation and a jazz club, as well as musical opportunities for Panama’s youth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/19/arts/danilo-perez-builds-a-magnet-for-musicians-in-panama.html', 'snippet': 'In his native Panama, the pianist Danilo Pérez spearheads a festival, a foundation and a jazz club, as well as musical opportunities for Panama’s youth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PANAMA — In the wee hours of Friday morning, Danilo Pérez climbed onstage at his jazz club here to play a set. Mr. Pérez, the Panama-born pianist and composer who has for more than a decade been a member of Wayne Shorter’s acclaimed quartet, had already headlined shows for the Panama Jazz Festival, which he founded 12 years ago. There he had performed alongside the Puerto Rican saxophonist Miguel Zenón and his fellow Panamanian, the salsa singer Rubén Blades, and in the festival’s gala concert at the 106-year-old National Theater. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aiming to Create a Jazz Capital ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aiming to Create a Jazz Capital', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Panama', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perez, Danilo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Perez, Danilo, Foundation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-18T22:42:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb86837a-a668-53f1-8cd8-6be4ca55b582', 'word_count': 1306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb86837a-a668-53f1-8cd8-6be4ca55b582'}, {'abstract': 'ASAP Yams, who died on Jan. 18 at 26, exerted a prescient influence over hip-hop both online and through ASAP Worldwide records.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/19/arts/music/asap-yams-of-asap-worldwide-bridged-hip-hop-regions-and-generations.html', 'snippet': 'ASAP Yams, who died on Jan. 18 at 26, exerted a prescient influence over hip-hop both online and through ASAP Worldwide records.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hip-hop’s past is littered with secret histories, scenes and stories and sounds that never got their full hearing, remembered only by a few. Thanks to the Internet, though, secret histories aren’t so secret anymore. Those stories are now rescued, amplified and recontextualized by a new wave of agents who are fluent in the past, and who use it to build a more creative future.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/19/arts/ASAP/ASAP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/19/arts/ASAP/ASAP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/19/arts/ASAP/ASAP-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 