l forests have vanished, equivalent in size to over half of the continental United States. The rate of cutting, burning and clearing shows no signs of abating.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20Melnick/20Melnick-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20Melnick/20Melnick-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 667}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20Melnick/20Melnick-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 921, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20Melnick/20Melnick-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1575, 'width': 1416, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20Melnick/20Melnick-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20Melnick/20Melnick-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20Melnick/20Melnick-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Make Forests Pay', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Make Forests Pay', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Melnick, Don', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearl, Mary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warfield, James', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Logging Industry', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-19T22:48:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Don J. Melnick, Mary C. Pearl and James Warfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Don', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Melnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Pearl', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb8f1939-d34c-554c-b5e3-8edc1b9f0108', 'word_count': 765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb8f1939-d34c-554c-b5e3-8edc1b9f0108'}, {'abstract': 'In the wake of the deaths of James Byrd Jr. and Matthew Shepard, the process of writing poems felt like a reprieve.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/19/a-poets-boyhood-at-the-burning-crossroads/', 'snippet': 'In the wake of the deaths of James Byrd Jr. and Matthew Shepard, the process of writing poems felt like a reprieve.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The year I started writing poems, I dreamed about chains dragging along a dusty country road. It was June 1998, six months before my 13th birthday. Earlier that evening, my mother and I watched, encased in a heavy silence, as the local news station reported on the murder of James Byrd Jr. in Jasper, Tex.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20draft/20draft-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 364, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20draft/20draft-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 364}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20draft/20draft-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 622, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20draft/20draft-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1243, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20draft/20draft-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20draft/20draft-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/opinion/20draft/20draft-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Poet’s Boyhood at the Burning Crossroads', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-19T22:54:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82b34cc8-01c5-5184-8ac9-3219a54d02d0', 'word_count': 1257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82b34cc8-01c5-5184-8ac9-3219a54d02d0'}, {'abstract': 'The Museum of Modern Art is screening two films by the Senegalese director Mati Diop: “Mille Soleils” and “Atlantiques.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/20/arts/mille-soleils-a-portrait-of-senegal-by-mati-diop.html', 'snippet': 'The Museum of Modern Art is screening two films by the Senegalese director Mati Diop: “Mille Soleils” and “Atlantiques.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the beginning of Mati Diop’s “Mille Soleils,” a man drives a herd of skinny long-horned cattle toward a slaughterhouse in a busy section of Dakar, Senegal, bringing car traffic to a halt. The incongruities within the image — animals mingling with machines, a pastoral activity in an urban setting — are intensified by the music, Tex Ritter’s “The Ballad of High Noon,” a solemn, stirring song of longing and vengeance. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/MILLE/MILLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 269, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/MILLE/MILLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 269}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/MILLE/MILLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 459, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/MILLE/MILLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 758, 'width': 1691, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/MILLE/MILLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/MILLE/MILLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/MILLE/MILLE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stuck, but Trying to Leave', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stuck, but Trying to Leave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diop, Mati', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Mille Soleils (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-19T22:56:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/32b22abd-38d9-5bc6-8179-7ac4eefec759', 'word_count': 453, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32b22abd-38d9-5bc6-8179-7ac4eefec759'}, {'abstract': 'Barbara Carroll extols living in the present in her latest show, in an open-ended run at Birdland.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/20/arts/barbara-carroll-performs-at-birdland.html', 'snippet': 'Barbara Carroll extols living in the present in her latest show, in an open-ended run at Birdland.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The elegant jazz pianist Barbara Carroll opened her show at Birdland on Saturday evening with a lightly swinging performance of Irving Berlin’s “Let’s Face the Music and Dance,” a song that never loses its relevance as it seesaws between dread and exuberance. From its opening phrase, “There may be trouble ahead,” it addresses the age-old question: How is it possible to live happily in a world that often seems on the verge of collapse? The answer is the same today as it was 1936: Whenever possible, live in the moment. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Midst of Uncertainty, Seizing the Moment in Song', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Midst of Uncertainty, Seizing the Moment in Song', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Birdland (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carroll, Barbara', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-19T22:57:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/10d8e7a8-2ac9-5289-800e-b7b3bf221c83', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10d8e7a8-2ac9-5289-800e-b7b3bf221c83'}, {'abstract': 'The Decemberists, Hanni El Khatib and Duane Eubanks all have new albums out this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/20/arts/music/new-releases-from-the-decemberists-hanni-el-khatib-and-duane-eubanks.html', 'snippet': 'The Decemberists, Hanni El Khatib and Duane Eubanks all have new albums out this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Colin Meloy, the grandiloquent bard at the heart of the Decemberists, plants his silver tongue firmly in cheek at the start of “What a Terrible World, What a Beautiful World,” the group’s new album. By way of a deadpan disclaimer titled “The Singer Addresses His Audience” — “We had to change some, you know, to belong to you,” he sings, briefing the fervent faithful — the album opens knowingly, making an end run around at least one line of critique.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/NEWMUSIC/NEWMUSIC-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 822, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/NEWMUSIC/NEWMUSIC-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 822}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/NEWMUSIC/NEWMUSIC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 748, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/NEWMUSIC/NEWMUSIC-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1496, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/NEWMUSIC/NEWMUSIC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/NEWMUSIC/NEWMUSIC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/NEWMUSIC/NEWMUSIC-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Indie Folk Polished to a Bright, Sly Sheen', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In