T17:53:45+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'AP', 'person': [], 'organization': 'AP'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/bf516cb9-13a5-5efc-a455-e5c218ee3b1c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/bf516cb9-13a5-5efc-a455-e5c218ee3b1c'}, {'abstract': 'The announcement by the state comptroller was Israel’s latest effort to head off an International Criminal Court inquiry into its actions in the war last summer with Hamas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/21/world/middleeast/israel-hamas-gaza-strip-war-investigation.html', 'snippet': 'The announcement by the state comptroller was Israel’s latest effort to head off an International Criminal Court inquiry into its actions in the war last summer with Hamas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Israel’s government watchdog, the state comptroller, said on Tuesday that he had opened an investigation into decisions made by military and political leaders during last summer’s 50-day war with the Hamas militant group in Gaza.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israeli Government Watchdog Investigates Military’s Conduct in Gaza War', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Israeli Government Watchdog Investigates Military’s Conduct in Gaza War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Criminal Court', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Physicians for Human Rights', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shapira, Joseph Haim', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-20T17:55:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b345c5fb-a127-5965-bfa8-25525f191b98', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b345c5fb-a127-5965-bfa8-25525f191b98'}, {'abstract': 'A slowdown in the pace of growth of China’s economy is leading foreign investors to reassess the country as a destination for their money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/21/business/international/to-invest-or-not-as-china-shifts.html', 'snippet': 'A slowdown in the pace of growth of China’s economy is leading foreign investors to reassess the country as a destination for their money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — On a tour of central Henan Province in May of last year, China’s president, Xi Jinping, first raised the notion of the economy adjusting to a “new normal.” Eight months later, with the economy growing at its slowest pace in more than two decades, it is increasingly clear what this adjustment will mean for China.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Invest, or Not, as China Shifts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-20T17:57:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Gough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ff8d262-245b-5c64-b996-0bd25722269e', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ff8d262-245b-5c64-b996-0bd25722269e'}, {'abstract': 'Free enterprise and globalization are the cornerstones of the worldview at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland, but there should be a dose of fairness as well.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/21/business/international/tempering-a-worldview-with-freedom-and-fairness.html', 'snippet': 'Free enterprise and globalization are the cornerstones of the worldview at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland, but there should be a dose of fairness as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How is it possible to construct a free-market economy that is also fair? That should be the central question for this year’s annual gathering of the global elite at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tempering a Worldview With Freedom and Fairness', 'kicker': 'Political Economy', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Economic Forum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-20T18:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Dixon | Reuters', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01f1d9c3-9a29-5eb7-bad0-e7c61d0962f2', 'word_count': 957, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01f1d9c3-9a29-5eb7-bad0-e7c61d0962f2'}, {'abstract': 'The New York-based designer recently took an inspiration-seeking trip to the Chinese city. Here, she discusses what caught her eye.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/20/misha-nonoo-beijing-travel-adventures/', 'snippet': 'The New York-based designer recently took an inspiration-seeking trip to the Chinese city. Here, she discusses what caught her eye.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a designer in the CFDA Fashion Incubator, an initiative supported by W Hotels Worldwide that fosters young fashion brands, Misha Nonoo was given a round-trip ticket to travel abroad this winter to seek inspiration for her forthcoming resort and spring 2016 collections. She spun the globe and put her finger on Beijing. “I’ve always been fascinated by the Great Wall and the immense history of Beijing,” she says. “And now that China has the number-one economy, I was curious to see how it would change in the future.” Having grown up in Bahrain and London before moving to Paris and later New York, Nonoo has something of a gypsy spirit, which is often evident in her collections. For spring 2013, she nodded to Havana’s languid climes with crisp white silhouettes and colorful prints, while the following season she featured a palette of black-and-red fabrics and furry hats reminiscent of a trip to the Russian countryside.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Misha Nonoo’s Adventures in Beijing', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Travel Diary: Beijing Inspiration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Council of Fashion Designers of America', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-20T18:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kari Molvar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Molvar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8aee120-4ce4-557e-97c8-520e522293ea', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8aee120-4ce4-557e-97c8-520e522293ea'}, {'abstract': 'The passenger jet, which normally ascends at 1,000 to 2,000 feet per minute, was climbing at about 6,000 feet per minute, Indonesian investigators said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/21/world/asia/airasia-jets-climb-before-crash-was-unusually-fast-investigators-say.html', 'snippet': 'The passenger jet, which normally ascends at 1,000 to 2,000 feet per minute, was climbing at about 6,000 feet per minute, Indonesian investigators said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JAKARTA, Indonesia — The AirAsia plane that crashed into the Java Sea last month had climbed at excessive speeds to an unusually high altitude before plunging and disappearing from radar, Indonesia’s top transportation official said Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/21/world/21plane/21plane-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/21/world/21plane/21plane-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'x