iginal': 'By Joe Cochrane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cochrane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04eb1f6b-3444-5bea-8174-60d877dac240', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04eb1f6b-3444-5bea-8174-60d877dac240'}, {'abstract': 'Ellen Lewis, who is being honored by the Casting Society of America for her work on films like “Goodfellas” and “Forrest Gump,” talks about the changes she’s seen in her profession.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/20/the-art-of-matching-actor-and-role/', 'snippet': 'Ellen Lewis, who is being honored by the Casting Society of America for her work on films like “Goodfellas” and “Forrest Gump,” talks about the changes she’s seen in her profession.', 'lead_paragraph': ' For almost three decades, the Casting Society of America’s annual Artios Awards, which recognize achievement in the often overlooked profession of casting, have been announced long after the Oscars, making the casting directors seem less relevant to the awards season conversation. But after the 2013 documentary “Casting By” questioned why there wasn’t a dedicated category at the Academy Awards, the society moved its ceremony, putting it in line with the similar events of guilds and other Hollywood organizations. The 30th Artios awards will take place Thursday, in both New York City (with Michael Urie as host) and Los Angeles (with Patton Oswalt doing the honors). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/robbie/robbie-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/20/arts/robbie/robbie-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', '