ename': None, 'lastname': 'Koh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fe4345e-c11e-58e1-a8f6-aa5454316eb7', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fe4345e-c11e-58e1-a8f6-aa5454316eb7'}, {'abstract': 'Viet Cong, founded by members of the art-rock band Women, released its eagerly anticipated first self-titled full-length LP this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/21/t-magazine/a-sci-fi-inspired-music-video-from-a-buzzy-indie-band.html', 'snippet': 'Viet Cong, founded by members of the art-rock band Women, released its eagerly anticipated first self-titled full-length LP this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a few short years and just two albums, the Canadian band Women released some of the best art rock of the new millennium. Following the dissolution of the group in 2012 due to the death of the guitarist Christopher Reimer, the band members Matt Flegel and Mike Wallace enlisted their fellow Canadian musicians Scott Munro and Daniel Christiansen — of Lab Coast and Sharp Ends, respectively — to form Viet Cong, an indie-rock band with a shared penchant for charred, factious punk. “We all embrace all of our musical histories and wouldn’t be making the kind of music we make now if we hadn’t learned from all the music we’ve played in the past,” says Flegel, who plays bass and sings in the new outfit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/21/t-magazine/21symonds-vc/21symonds-vc-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/21/t-magazine/21symonds-vc/21symonds-vc-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/21/t-m