mes, and Fans', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-21T08:15:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Duerden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Duerden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40057c36-e45b-5c01-9a3e-6dff4728e481', 'word_count': 905, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40057c36-e45b-5c01-9a3e-6dff4728e481'}, {'abstract': 'Did Alberto Nisman commit suicide, or was he murdered hours before he was to testify against the government?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/21/opinion/a-suspicious-death-in-argentina.html', 'snippet': 'Did Alberto Nisman commit suicide, or was he murdered hours before he was to testify against the government?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some Argentines are calling Alberto Nisman, the maverick prosecutor, the 86th casualty of one of the deadliest, unsolved terrorists attacks in modern history: the 1994 bombing of a Jewish community center in Buenos Aires. That seems fitting whether his death over the weekend turns out to be a suicide, as the government of President Cristina Fernández de Kirchner seems eager to establish, or the murder of a man who had learned too much. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/21/opinion/21wed2/21wed2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/21/opinion/21wed2/21wed2-article