'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coscarelli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41611221-82e8-53c1-b351-abbdae29f7d3', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41611221-82e8-53c1-b351-abbdae29f7d3'}, {'abstract': 'Esa-Pekka Salonen will embark on three years as composer in residence at the New York Philharmonic, and series will be devoted to Rachmaninoff, Messiaen and new music.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/22/arts/music/philharmonic-announces-2015-16-concerts-and-residencies.html', 'snippet': 'Esa-Pekka Salonen will embark on three years as composer in residence at the New York Philharmonic, and series will be devoted to Rachmaninoff, Messiaen and new music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The composer and conductor Esa-Pekka Salonen will begin a three-year stint as composer in residence, and the bass-baritone Eric Owens will be named artist in residence. A Rachmaninoff festival will feature the pianist Daniil Trifonov, a Messiaen week will explore the legacy of the French composer and organist, and the NY Phil Biennial of new music will live up to its name by returning two years after it made its debut.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Philharmonic Outlines 2015-16 Season ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Philharmonic Outlines 2015-16 Season', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cooper, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salonen, Esa-Pekka', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-21T16:02:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85de4ee7-d152-56f4-9861-649dfdd78416', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85de4ee7-d152-56f4-9861-649dfdd78416'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama takes a stab at shaping the post-Obama era.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/21/state-of-the-union-state-of-the-presidency/', 'snippet': 'President Obama takes a stab at shaping the post-Obama era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last night’s State of the Union found President Obama in a somewhat better position politically than in the weeks immediately following his party’s big midterm defeat. The improving economy has finally boosted his poll numbers toward at least the mid-40s, probably because plunging gas prices have finally delivered an effective wage increase. His power grab on immigration also seems to have been a limited political success: The move was broadly unpopular but neither immigration nor executive power are top-of-mind issues for most voters, the president’s standing with Hispanics has clearly improved, a brief period of media chiding soon gave way (predictably) to media forgetfulness, and the Republican opposition has been (also predictably) divided and disorganized. And on foreign policy, while the days of presidential rhetoric about allegedly ending wars and routing Al Qaeda are over, there hasn’t been a return to the crisis atmosphere of last spring and summer, giving the president breathing room to claim (plausibly in the case of Russia, less so in the case of ISIS) that his strategies are working.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'State of the Union, State of the Presidency', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-21T16:08:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df129135-98a7-5ca8-830a-586d9bb689ad', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df129135-98a7-5ca8-830a-586d9bb689ad'}, {'abstract': 'Dr. Michael Davidson was shot by a gunman who entered the Boston hospital Tuesday morning, opened fire and then killed himself, the authorities said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/22/us/dr-michael-davidson-brigham-womens-dies-after-shooting.html', 'snippet': 'Dr. Michael Davidson was shot by a gunman who entered the Boston hospital Tuesday morning, opened fire and then killed himself, the authorities said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — A cardiac surgeon who was shot when a man opened fire inside a Boston medical center Tuesday has died of his wounds, hospital and police officials said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/us/SHOOTING-web/SHOOTING-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015