'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T02:09:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'AP', 'person': [], 'organization': 'AP'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/fd61ec31-6441-5096-b46f-ed1ebffb8296', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/fd61ec31-6441-5096-b46f-ed1ebffb8296'}, {'abstract': 'With the economy on more solid ground, leading Republicans are tempering complaints about overall economic growth and refocusing on income disparity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/22/business/economy/talk-of-inequality-prods-republicans-to-refocus.html', 'snippet': 'With the economy on more solid ground, leading Republicans are tempering complaints about overall economic growth and refocusing on income disparity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama’s push for a new “middle-class economics” may go nowhere in Congress, but his ambitious array of proposals to raise stagnant incomes and provide more government support for struggling working families will frame his last two years in office and help make the politics of rich and poor a central issue in the campaign to succeed him.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/us/JP-INEQUALITY1/JP-INEQUALITY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/22/us/JP-INEQUALITY1/JP-INEQUALITY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/us/JP-INEQUALITY1/JP-INEQUALITY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jum