fare, illustrated.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/22/for-the-love-of-capital-income/', 'snippet': 'Class warfare, illustrated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shorter Glenn Hubbard: Means-tested programs and tax credits are a terrible thing, because benefits that fall when your income rises discourage work. Also, we must save money by means-testing Social Security and Medicare.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For The Love Of Capital Income', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T06:54:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ac7d4e8-e952-57f5-ac17-a4f71006b68d', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ac7d4e8-e952-57f5-ac17-a4f71006b68d'}, {'abstract': 'The program, worth about $69.7 billion a month, is more than analysts had been expecting as the European Central Bank tries to fend off stagnation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/business/european-central-bank-bond-buying.html', 'snippet': 'The program, worth about $69.7 billion a month, is more than analysts had been expecting as the European Central Bank tries to fend off stagnation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT — The European Central Bank said on Thursday that it would begin buying hundreds of billions of euros worth of government bonds in an aggressive — though some say belated — attempt to prevent the eurozone from becoming trapped in long-term economic stagnation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im