at Alpine Championships', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup (Skiing)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fenninger, Anna', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cheetah Conservation Fund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALPINE SKIING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Austria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T08:04:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Skiing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kelley McMillan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kelley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McMillan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3085c892-ba83-5556-ac2e-ae4306160b4d', 'word_count': 795, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3085c892-ba83-5556-ac2e-ae4306160b4d'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/22/arts/television/whats-on-tv-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:25 A.M. (Encore) BLUE JASMINE (2013) Cate Blanchett won an Oscar for her role as Jasmine French, a Park Avenue matron fallen on hard times after her husband, Hal (Alec Baldwin), lands in prison for bilking his clients out of millions, leaving her with little more than her pearls, white Chanel jacket, Hermès bag and monogrammed Louis Vuitton luggage. Destitute, in a manner of speaking, Jasmine flies first-class to San Francisco to begin life anew with her sister, Ginger (Sally Hawkins), whose scrappy existence and brutish men (Andrew Dice Clay and Bobby Cannavale) Jasmine can barely stomach without self-medicating with vodka and Xanax. Then she meets a diplomatic type (Peter Sarsgaard) with an appreciation for her decorating talents and designer threads and starts to see light at the end of her dark tunnel. With intimations of Ruth Madoff and Blanche DuBois, this shattered woman is “brilliantly brought to quivering life by Ms. Blanchett,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times, in Woody Allen’s “most sustained, satisfying and resonant film since ‘Match Point.’ ”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T08:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c3acef8-1dd1-5b19-9ca2-05b0e7d7334c', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c3acef8-1dd1-5b19-9ca2-05b0e7d7334c'}, {'abstract': 'Blacks and Hispanics have faced unjust biases in getting mortgages and renting homes in the past. The Supreme Court should protect the Fair Housing Act.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/22/opinion/discrimination-with-a-smile.html', 'snippet': 'Blacks and Hispanics have faced unjust biases in getting mortgages and renting homes in the past. The Supreme Court should protect the Fair Housing Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Under Chief Justice John Roberts Jr., the Supreme Court has developed a remarkably sanguine view of race in America. “Things have changed dramatically,” Mr. Roberts wrote in 2013 in striking down the heart of the Voting Rights Act. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Discrimination With a Smile’', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Discrimination With a Smile’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, John G Jr', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T08:27:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1815918-dcbe-5683-9e3e-e3a0f72a5bf8', 'word_count': 641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1815918-dcbe-5683-9e3e-e3a0f72a5bf8'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama has taken two actions that are a refreshing departure from six years of aggressively attacking investigative journalism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/22/opinion/lessons-of-the-james-risen-case.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama has taken two actions that are a refreshing departure from six years of aggressively attacking investigative journalism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Obama administration has taken two actions that seem a refreshing departure from six years of aggressively attacking investigative journalism. The Justice Department abandoned an attempt to force James Risen, a New York Times reporter, to testify about a confidential source. And it temper