 treason.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — South Korea’s Supreme Court on Thursday upheld a nine-year prison sentence for a former lawmaker who was convicted of encouraging followers to take up arms to overthrow the government in the event of war with North Korea.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/world/23korea/23korea-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/23/world/23korea/23korea-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/world/23korea/23korea-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/world/23korea/23korea-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1320, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/world/23korea/23korea-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/23/world/23korea/23korea-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/world/23korea/23korea-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ex-Lawmaker’s Sentence for Encouraging Revolt Is Upheld in South Korea', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Korea: Treason Sentence Upheld', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee Seok-ki', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Progressive Party (South Korea)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T12:06:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de83cbfa-57fe-560a-8904-491679206ebe', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de83cbfa-57fe-560a-8904-491679206ebe'}, {'abstract': 'Goldman Sachs’s investments are testing the Volcker Rule. | The European Central Bank is expected to announce it will begin its bond-buying program. | Uber closes $1.6 billion in financing from clients of Goldman’s private wealth arm. | Regulators address “too big to fail.”', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2015/01/22/morning-agenda-goldman-trumps-volcker-rule/', 'snippet': 'Goldman Sachs’s investments are testing the Volcker Rule. | The European Central Bank is expected to announce it will begin its bond-buying program. | Uber closes $1.6 billion in financing from clients of Goldman’s private wealth arm. | Regulators...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Goldman Sachs’s recent acquisitions include apartments in Spain, a mall in Utah and a European ink company ‒ exactly the sorts of investments that many had assumed would be prohibited by the Volcker Rule, DealBook’s Nathaniel Popper reports. “While its competitors have been abandoning the business of making big bets with their own money,” he writes, “Goldman has been quietly coming up with several new ways to put its own money to work in formats that appear to stay on the right side of Volcker.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Goldman Trumps Volcker Rule', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T12:17:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8d3d057-6cca-50e6-9490-c23420db9542', 'word_count': 2020, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8d3d057-6cca-50e6-9490-c23420db9542'}, {'abstract': 'The difference is not only in details, like optimized forms, but also in an overall approach that gives digital campaign workers more authority.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/upshot/why-democrats-still-lead-republicans-in-online-fund-raising.html', 'snippet': 'The difference is not only in details, like optimized forms, but also in an overall approach that gives digital campaign workers more authority.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there were any reason for Republicans to pay attention to over-the-top fund-raising emails from the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee last fall, it would be this: Even in a terrible year for House Democrats, the party committee showed it could raise millions online, much of it in small amounts that are the lifeblood of large campaigns.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/upshot/23up-online_sub/23up-online_sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'wid