: {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9a45571-eb0f-56d4-b3b7-9cbae269ac06', 'word_count': 962, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9a45571-eb0f-56d4-b3b7-9cbae269ac06'}, {'abstract': 'In an Indian city, little remains of the glorious past a father told stories about.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/travel/once-a-land-of-princes-and-palaces.html', 'snippet': 'In an Indian city, little remains of the glorious past a father told stories about.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It took 15 minutes for my car to creep forward about 20 feet, giving me ample time to admire the crumbling filigreed balconies jutting precariously overhead.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Returning to Hyderabad, Once a Land of Princes and Palaces', 'kicker': 'Personal Journeys', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Once a Land of Princes and Palaces', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khan, Sarah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hyderabad (India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T14:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Khan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc032fcc-fd57-526b-98d2-cd62b43ae1a9', 'word_count': 2003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc032fcc-fd57-526b-98d2-cd62b43ae1a9'}, {'abstract': 'Bat Sheva Marcus tries to help Orthodox women find pleasure — without breaking the (Talmudic) law.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/magazine/the-orthodox-sex-guru.html', 'snippet': 'Bat Sheva Marcus tries to help Orthodox women find pleasure — without breaking the (Talmudic) law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one of her early sessions with the patient, Bat Sheva Marcus, an Orthodox Jewish sex counselor, drew up a list. The patient, who was in her 20s, wore the uniform of her rigidly devout sect: a dark suit with a shapeless skirt reaching well below the knee, dark stockings, a plain blouse buttoned up to the neck and both a wig and a crocheted hat covering her head. She had come to Marcus’s offices in Midtown Manhattan from a cloistered community in Borough Park, Brooklyn, because she had been recoiling from sex with her husband. There was pain, and, more problematic for Marcus, there was no desire. The pain, Marcus and her staff deduced right away, was a result of muscle constrictions stemming from childbirth; this could be treated effectively with dilators — and without objection from the patient’s rabbi. But the deeper aversion was more complex. Talking with the woman at a round table in a room decorated with still lifes of pears and berries, Marcus wrote a list of ways that the patient and her husband could make sex, for her, more appealing.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25guru1/25mag-25guru.t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 516, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25guru1/25mag-25guru.t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 516}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25guru1/25mag-25guru.t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 880, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25guru1/25mag-25guru.t_CA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1711, 'width': 1990, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25guru1/25mag-25guru.t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25guru1/25mag-25guru.t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25guru1/25mag-25guru.t_CA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Orthodox Sex Guru', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flesh of My Flesh', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bergner, Daniel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marcus, Bat Sheva', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Talmud', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T14:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Bergner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bergner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2e4c493-1099-5417-9312-612e106ab1d8', 'word_count': 3174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2e4c493-1099-5417-9312-612e106ab1d8'}, {'abstract': 'Alone together with the warehouse workers, soldiers and paramedics of late-night social media.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/magazine/instagrams-graveyard-shift.html', 'snippet': 'Alone together with the warehouse workers, soldiers and paramedics of late-night social media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The photograph that Markisha McClenton posted on Instagram is a self-portrait, a close-up that is muted in dim light. She might be on her way to work. She might be coming home. Her workdays begin and end in the dark, and they are dark in between. She’s a lab technician in Jacksonville, Fla. Her specialty is blood. She has worked these dark hours since her son was 7. “Freedom,” she told me over the phone from her lab. That’s why she works these hours: The freedom to work at night and to raise her children during the days. To her, this is good fortune. She is smiling in this photo. But her eyes are midnight eyes, 3 a.m. eyes. Why take a photo at that hour? “People forget about us, the night shift,” she said. The #nightshift. That’s the hashtag she used. It’s how I found her.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25lede1/25lede1-articleLarge-v7.gif', 'height': 502, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25lede1/25lede1-articleLarge-v7.gif', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 502}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/magazine/25lede1/25lede1-jumbo-v2.gif', 'height': 792, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt