 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Isaac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e63ad8bd-d8d2-553b-b960-5e9b28b1f675', 'word_count': 548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e63ad8bd-d8d2-553b-b960-5e9b28b1f675'}, {'abstract': 'This short documentary explores what we can learn from the Black Panther Party in confronting police violence 50 years later.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/22/opinion/the-black-panthers-revisited.html', 'snippet': 'This short documentary explores what we can learn from the Black Panther Party in confronting police violence 50 years later.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When we think of the civil rights struggles of the 1960s, certain events tend to come to mind: the March on Washington, for example, or perhaps the Freedom Rides or sit-ins. Others, however, have faded from our collective memory. One of those is the story of the Black Panther Party, the subject of this Op-Doc video. Founded in 1966 in Oakland, Calif., to combat police violence, the Black Panther Party and its story are a key part of our nation’s still-complicated racial narrative.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Black Panthers, Revisited’', 'kicker': 'Op-Docs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Black Panther Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T15:02:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opini