': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T22:53:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fd4042b-31f4-54ff-9547-03fc532cf372', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fd4042b-31f4-54ff-9547-03fc532cf372'}, {'abstract': 'A reporter’s look at how the art world has changed since 1991 and where it appears to be headed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/arts/design/degenerate-art-to-koonss-made-and-heaven-and-much-in-between.html', 'snippet': 'A reporter’s look at how the art world has changed since 1991 and where it appears to be headed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I started covering the art world for The New York Times in 1991, the market was reeling from a worldwide recession. Gone were the Japanese who had been snapping up Impressionist and post-Impressionist paintings at a frenzied pace, spending huge sums for what one dealer described as “pretty pictures across a crowded room.” After the Japanese disappeared, sales at the auction giants Sotheby’s and Christie’s plummeted to about half of what they had been.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Flashbacks and Forecasts ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flashbacks and Forecasts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vogel, Carol', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Christie's", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gagosian, Larry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vogel, Carol', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T22:55:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75a618c4-7499-5d1f-b7db-ff475f97f02f', 'word_count': 1838, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75a618c4-7499-5d1f-b7db-ff475f97f02f'}, {'abstract': 'The Winter Antiques Show at the Park Avenue Armory overflows with singular items from dozens of periods and genres.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/arts/design/at-armorys-winter-antiques-show-volumes-of-variety.html', 'snippet': 'The Winter Antiques Show at the Park Avenue Armory overflows with singular items from dozens of periods and genres.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Celebrated masterpieces are all well and good, but the less-well-lit nooks and crannies of art history are also needed to sustain a true art lover. That’s what a big fair like the Winter Antiques Show at the Park Avenue Armory is especially good for. With 73 purveyors of rare and remarkable objects, from ancient Egyptian sculpture to Modernist painting, it offers innumerable opportunities to encounter and learn about aspects of painting, decorative arts, furniture, jewelry or glass that might be new to even the best-informed visitors.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': ' Forgotten Creators, Memorable Works', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forgotten Creators, Memorable Works', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Winter Antiques Show', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Park Avenue Armory (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-22T22:55:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4513730-a82d-5f73-8c19-80c20f20ff57', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4513730-a82d-5f73-8c19-80c20f20ff57'}, {'abstract': 'Al Pacino plays a revered stage and screen actor who fears he is losing his mojo in “The Humbling.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/movies/in-the-humbling-al-pacino-plays-an-aging-performer.html', 'snippet': 'Al Pacino plays a revered stage and screen actor who fears he is losing his mojo in “The Humbling.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his later fiction, Philip Roth has addressed the indignities of aging with a squirm-inducing candor. How many readers (or filmgoers) are eager to contemplate the loss of their powers until it is staring them in the face? ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/arts/23HUMBLING/HUMBLING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/23/arts/23HUMBLING/HUMBLING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'