'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T00:15:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Stuart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stuart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89d96ca5-840f-5e31-96cf-5aee1b6e7916', 'word_count': 1650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89d96ca5-840f-5e31-96cf-5aee1b6e7916'}, {'abstract': 'In his case, the injustice may lie not in the conviction, but in the failure to negotiate the charges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/opinion/why-adnan-syed-of-serial-should-have-pleaded-guilty.html', 'snippet': 'In his case, the injustice may lie not in the conviction, but in the failure to negotiate the charges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OUR modern criminal justice system is designed to avoid jury trials. Through investigation and considered use of discretion, prosecutors are expected to charge only when there is sufficient evidence to convict. Once charged, defendants are encouraged to plead guilty in part to avoid a “trial penalty” — a longer sentence after a trial, often a much longer one. And 95 percent of them do just that. The Supreme Court acknowledged this reality in 2012 when it described our criminal process as “a system of pleas.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/22murray/22murray-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 492, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/22murray/22murray-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 492}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/22murray/22murray-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/22murray/22murray-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1680, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/22murray/22murray-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/22murray/22murray-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/opinion/22murray/22murray-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Adnan Syed of ‘Serial’ Should Have Pleaded Guilty', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Better Way to Plead Guilty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Syed, Adnan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, JaneAnne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plea Bargaining', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maryland', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Hae Min (1980-99)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Serial (Radio Program)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Impri