rticle/5dc7cd1e-0219-55b9-a60f-33a36e0eea6e'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Ignace Jan Paderewski addressed the Polish National Council in 1940.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/1940-paderewski-vows-deliverance/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Ignace Jan Paderewski addressed the Polish National Council in 1940.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In words as resonant as the notes of Chopin’s “Polonaise Militaire,” which he has played thousands of times for encore-demanding audiences the world over, Ignace Jan Paderewski, seventy nine year old pianist-patriot, stood before his people yesterday at the Polish National Council and pledged the deliverance of his 35,000,000 countrymen. Although Paderewski was speaking not as a musician but as a Pole in the service of his nation, and although the voice of an aged man still weak from illness could not develop those crescendos that have never failed to thrill listeners as his long delicate fingers hit the keyboard, the nobility of his ideas and the dramatic turn of his phrases made the speech a series of forensic fortissimos. In the awareness that he might not live to see again the state of which he had the honor to be the first President, Paderewski stamped this discourse as the last will and testament to Poland of one of its servants. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Jan. 24, 1940', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/blogs/23iht-old24-Paderewski/23iht-old24-Paderewski-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 535, 'width': 430, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/blogs/23iht-old24-Paderewski/23iht-old24-Paderewski-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 535, 'width': 430, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/blogs/23iht-old24-Paderewski/23iht-old24-Paderewski-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/23/blogs/23iht-old24-Paderewski/23iht-old24-Paderewski-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/blogs/23iht-old24-Paderewski/23iht-old24-Paderewski-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '1940: Paderewski Vows Deliverance', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paderewski, Ignace Jan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Poland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T16:05:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d72bff2-c8ac-5f6d-8108-4b940131aff8', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d72bff2-c8ac-5f6d-8108-4b940131aff8'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Shear, a White House correspondent, describes what it is like to cover the State of the Union address.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2015/01/23/leaks-and-ad-libs-how-to-cover-the-state-of-the-union-address/', 'snippet': 'Michael Shear, a White House correspondent, describes what it is like to cover the State of the Union address.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, reporters and editors in The Times’s Washington and New York offices prepared for President Obama’s State of the Union address, knowing that they would write, and rewrite, articles before, during and after the speech, all on tight deadlines. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/blogs/sotu/sotu-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/22/blogs/sotu/sotu-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/blogs/sotu/sotu-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/blogs/sotu/sotu-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/blogs/sotu/sotu-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/22/blogs/sotu/sotu-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/22/blogs/sotu/sotu-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Leaks and Ad-Libs: How to Cover the State of the Union Address', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baker, Peter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bumiller, Elisabeth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kenny, Steve', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T16:09:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1baba7bb-61ae-5aac-9a5f-c9b15a8f0576', 'word_count': 659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1baba7bb-61ae-5aac-9a5f-c9b15a8f0576'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: A “countess” was convicted in an assassination plot in 1965.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/1965-smoking-countess-convicted/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: A “countess” was convicted in an assassination plot in 1965.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS —\xa0A pipe-smoking ‘‘countess,’’ who\xa0 was once an interpreter on Gen. Dwight\xa0 D. Eisenhower’s civilian staff, was sentenced to 18 years’ imprisonment for\xa0 plotting to assassinate President de\xa0 Gaulle. The State Security Court found\xa0 Mrs. Paule de Roussoulet, 56, who adopted the title ‘‘Countess of Liffiac,’’ guilty\xa0 of planning to shoot Gen. de Gaulle last\xa0 Feb. 15 when he visited the École Militaire here. Two other defendants were\xa0 also convicted. A civilian Jean Bichon,\xa0 51, was given 20 years and Capt. Robert\xa0 Poinard, 38, was sentenced to 15 years.\xa0 Security police uncovered the plot about\xa0 two weeks before it was to take place.\xa0 Bichon allegedly was the ringleader of\xa0 the group. Poinard and Mrs. de Roussoulet, a teacher of English at the École\xa0 Militaire, were accused of encouraging\xa0 the intended killers and planning to facilitate their entry to the school. Gen. de\xa0 Gaulle was to have been shot by a high- powered rifle equipped with a telescopic\xa0 sight as he crossed the École courtyard.\xa0—New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Jan. 24, 1965', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1965: Smoking ‘Countess’ Convicted', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Gaulle, Charles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T16:14:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13512139-dfcb-5831-a411-02f7c4cfeb24', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13512139-dfcb-5831-a411-02f7c4cfeb24'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PLATO AT THE GOOGLEPLEX: Why Philosophy Won’t Go Away,  by Rebecca Newberger Goldstein. (Vintage, $16.95.) Goldstein revives the art of the dialogue, bringing the Greek philosopher to life in 21st-\xadcentury America and sending him on a multi\xadcity book tour. As he probes some of the deepest questions confronting us — from sexuality and child-rearing to morality and the meaning of life — “we hear authentic Platonic arguments brought nicely up to date,” Anthony Gottlieb wrote here.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T16:20:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/626d889c-ee15-558c-bfaf-107d7b2b04ab', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/626d889c-ee15-558c-bfaf-107d7b2b04ab'}, {'abstract': 'As the Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum observes the 70th anniversary of the liberation of the concentration camps, it is focusing on new generations of visitors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/24/world/europe/for-auschwitz-museum-and-survivors-a-moment-of-passage.html', 'snippet': 'As the Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum observes the 70th anniversary of the liberation of the concentration camps, it is focusing on new generations of visitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OSWIECIM, Poland — For what is likely to be the last time, a large number of the survivors of the Nazi concentration camps at Auschwitz will gather next week under an expansive tent, surrounded by royalty and heads of state, to commemorate the 70th anniversary of the liberation of those held there at the end of World War II.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/world/24AUSCHWITZ1/24AUSCHWITZ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/24/world/24AUSCHWITZ1/24AUSCHWITZ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/world/24AUSCHWITZ1/24AUSCHWITZ1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/world/24AUSCHWITZ1/24AUSCHWITZ1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/world/24AUSCHWITZ1/24AUSCHWITZ1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/24/world/24AUSCHWITZ1/24AUSCHWITZ1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/world/24AUSCHWITZ1/24AUSCHWITZ1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Auschwitz Museum, a Time of Great Change', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Auschwitz Museum, a Time of Great Change', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lyman, Rick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Auschwitz Concentration Camp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Poland', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concentration Camps', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lauder, Ronald S', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Birkenau (Poland)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T16:23:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Lyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/123d12a3-647f-5806-ab08-ef3e293fa9f1', 'word_count': 1167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/123d12a3-647f-5806-ab08-ef3e293fa9f1'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LEAVING BEFORE THE RAINS COME,  by Alexandra Fuller. (Penguin Press, $26.95.) Fuller, who described her idiosyncratic African childhood in “Don’t Let’s Go to the Dogs Tonight,” here offers an account of the collapse of her long marriage.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T16:24:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbe7b3cf-2d04-5769-860a-54a874ee7b73', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbe7b3cf-2d04-5769-860a-54a874ee7b73'}, {'abstract': 'The Most Rev. Justin Welby speaks about income inequality, the recent terrorist attacks in Paris, divisions in the Church of England and Pope Francis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/24/us/archbishop-of-canterbury-inequality.html', 'snippet': 'The Most Rev. Justin Welby speaks about income inequality, the recent terrorist attacks in Paris, divisions in the Church of England and Pope Francis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the two years since the Most Rev. Justin Welby was installed as archbishop of Canterbury, he has traveled the world to talk with other church leaders, and his assessment of those visits has been, in many ways, grim.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/us/24CANTERBURY-web/24CANTERBURY-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/24/us/24CANTERBURY-web/24CANTERBURY-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/us/24CANTERBURY-web/24CANTERBURY-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/us/24CANTERBURY-web/24CANTERBURY-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/us/24CANTERBURY-web/24CANTERBURY-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/24/us/24CANTERBURY-web/24CANTERBURY-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/us/24CANTERBURY-web/24CANTERBURY-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inequality as a Religious Issue: A Conversation With the Archbishop of Canterbury', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANGLICAN CHURCHES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Church of England', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Welby, Justin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T16:25:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Paulson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paulson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/0dff7a40-9a72-57db-9b76-77935fe66adf', 'word_count': 1709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0dff7a40-9a72-57db-9b76-77935fe66adf'}, {'abstract': 'Paula Hawkins, whose thriller “The Girl on the Train” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 2, previously wrote romantic comedies under a pseudonym.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Paula Hawkins, whose thriller “The Girl on the Train” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 2, previously wrote romantic comedies under a pseudonym.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Starting Over: When is a debut not a debut? When a writer changes names and genres, for one thing — as did the British author Paula Hawkins, whose “debut thriller,” “The Girl on the Train,” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 2. To judge from the Q. and A. at her publisher’s website, you’d never know that Hawkins, a financial journalist for 15 years, previously wrote romantic comedies under a pseudonym. “That was women’s fiction, I suppose,” Hawkins told NPR in a recent interview. “Much more lighthearted, not so much killing. . . . I did enjoy writing them, but they kept getting darker and darker. And once I started writing this book I knew this was what I was supposed to be doing. It just felt completely right for me. This sounds awful, but I’m not, you know, a joyful, romantic person. Well, I can be, but I’ve got a proper dark side and I enjoy indulging it.” She’s not kidding: “The Girl on the Train” is about an alcoholic commuter, fired from her job, who spins an obsessive fantasy about a couple along her train route and who may know something about the wife’s disappearance. (Comparisons to “Gone Girl” are not misplaced.) I had no luck unearthing Hawkins’s earlier pseudonym, but I did find a financial guide she published under her own name in Britain, “The Money Goddess,” which invents a group of “Sex and the City"-like friends as it dispenses advice about shopping for mortgages or planning for retirement. It’s the “Bridget Jones of finance,” according to one Amazon customer, who awarded it four out of five stars.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01list-hawkins/01list-hawkins-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 456, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01list-hawkins/01list-hawkins-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 456, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01list-hawkins/01list-hawkins-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01list-hawkins/01list-hawkins-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01list-hawkins/01list-hawkins-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T16:25:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad1984b7-c0ad-5801-b8dd-6f390070238c', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad1984b7-c0ad-5801-b8dd-6f390070238c