5+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenny Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b9fb268-a6b3-5e42-8825-af9e78b403e5', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b9fb268-a6b3-5e42-8825-af9e78b403e5'}, {'abstract': 'A debut novel about family, phobias and the high-flying freedoms of skateboarding.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/if-i-fall-if-i-die-by-michael-christie.html', 'snippet': 'A debut novel about family, phobias and the high-flying freedoms of skateboarding.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The skateboard, that preferred mode of transportation for the alienated and disaffected — and also able to “black out the rest of the world,” as Tony Hawk put it in his 2000 autobiography, “Hawk. Occupation: Skateboarder” — appears midway through Michael Christie’s novel of a troubled childhood, “If I Fall, if I Die.” It’s a transformative object, this skateboard, redemptive and cathartic, and it helps the 11-year-old hero, Will Cardiel, convey himself, literally and figuratively, from what has been up to then a life of privation with his single mother. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/10/books/review/SAYRAFIEZADEH/SAYRAFIEZADEH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/10/books/review/SAYRAFIEZADEH/SAYRAFIEZADEH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 902}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/10/books/review/SAYRAFIEZADEH/SAYRA