DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quantitative Easing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Draghi, Mario', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T17:11:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Mukherjee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mukherjee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91df31e9-48e0-5888-ac0b-663512bc07df', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91df31e9-48e0-5888-ac0b-663512bc07df'}, {'abstract': 'In 2012, Marissa Mayer was tasked with reversing the fortunes of a struggling Internet pioneer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/marissa-mayer-and-the-fight-to-save-yahoo-by-nicholas-carlson.html', 'snippet': 'In 2012, Marissa Mayer was tasked with reversing the fortunes of a struggling Internet pioneer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One day in 2012, Marissa Mayer was whirling around Yahoo’s office in Sunnyvale, Calif., directing a redesign of the home page and the company’s uncool but still widely used email application. The next, she was the proud mother of her first child, who was nicknamed BBBB in the hospital, for Big Baby Boy Bogue (her husband’s last name). This would hardly be considered extraordinary if it weren’t for the fact that she had recently been named the company’s C.E.O. Female chief executives typically live in glass boxes where every move they make is held up for intense public scrutiny, and most get themselves there through brutal amounts of work and by avoiding anything that draws attention to the fact that they’re women. Yet right after starting her job running a troubled $30 billion technology giant, Mayer announced that she would be taking a maternity leave, albeit a stunningly short one. (She took two weeks.) The audacity of the move — accepting a top executive job knowing that she was five months pregnant and then taking so little time off — prompted an Internet outcry (“The Pregnant C.E.O.: Should You Hate Marissa Mayer?” Forbes asked in one representative example), in which even progressives grumbled about the privilege involved, including Mayer’s household staff and in-office nursery. And so it was that her upsetting of the women’s\xad-issues industrial complex became all that most people know about Mayer. Yet there is much more to the picture. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25kolhatkar/25kolhatkar-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 173, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25kolhatkar/25kolhatkar-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 173}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25kolhatkar/25kolhatkar-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 296, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25kolhatkar/25kolhatkar-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 591, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 's