ction_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzanne Berne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2a45e1c-72d4-5c2a-9fba-450c32d5ca3b', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2a45e1c-72d4-5c2a-9fba-450c32d5ca3b'}, {'abstract': 'A story of activists and unlikely soulmates in Victorian London.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/the-match-girl-and-the-heiress-by-seth-koven.html', 'snippet': 'A story of activists and unlikely soulmates in Victorian London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Faith-based social policies have gotten a bad name lately, but not long ago they formed the core of many admirable progressive movements. Some late Victorian Christians arguably stood between London’s poorest and the Dickensian horrors suffered by the likes of Oliver Twist.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Match Girl and the Heiress,’ by Seth Koven', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Between Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burleigh, Nina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koven, Seth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T17:33:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nina Burleigh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burleigh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2466279-705d-5d4e-8fb6-df95c2fb5837', 'word_count': 766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2466279-705d-5d4e-8fb6-df95c2fb5837'}, {'abstract': 'In World War II, books helped lift the spirits of the troops.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/when-books-went-to-war-by-molly-guptill-manning.html', 'snippet': 'In World War II, books helped lift the spirits of the troops.', 'lead_paragraph': '“From the Airborne Infantry of the front lines to the chair-borne Finance Corps of the rear,” an American soldier observed from an English hospital in the midst of World War II, “you can find the boys reading as they never have before.” He was referring to the ubiquitous presence, among troops stationed abroad, of Armed Services Editions, or A.S.E.s: disposable pocket-size paperback reprints of a wide range of current and classic titles paid for by the United States government and produced by a consortium of the nation’s publishers. The editions, as the soldier’s remark suggests, met the demand for print among American military personnel overseas while opening up possibilities for greater consumption of books in the postwar period.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25rubin/25rubin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25rubin/25rubin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 667}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25rubin/25rubin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 922, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25rubin/25rubin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, '