rotagonist of Jo Baker’s first novel is an Oxford graduate working at a Belfast pub.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Offcomer” is the Lancashire word for an outsider, a person from somewhere else who will never quite fit in. It’s the perfect term for the underachieving and dispirited Claire Thomas, the central character in Jo Baker’s first novel — published only last month in the United States, 12 years after it appeared in Britain and 11 years before the spectacular success of Baker’s fifth novel, “Longbourn,” in 2013. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Offcomer,’ by Jo Baker', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Underachiever', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weber, Katharine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baker, Jo (1973- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T17:38:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9895b356-37dd-55a7-89f3-3e78ea6c75dc', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9895b356-37dd-55a7-89f3-3e78ea6c75dc'}, {'abstract': 'Julian Bell looks at van Gogh’s brief, explosively creative life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/van-gogh-by-julian-bell.html', 'snippet': 'Julian Bell looks at van Gogh’s brief, explosively creative life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a fledgling artist, Vincent van Gogh hired a carpenter to build a perspective frame: a wire-grid window. He used it to draw the Dutch countryside, his eyes darting between his pencil and the views through the frame. A few years later, van Gogh suffered a spate of psychiatric crises that sent him to an asylum in Saint Rémy, France. There his bedroom’s barred window doubled as a new perspective frame, albeit one with only verticals, through which he sketched the wheat field below. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/0125-bks-Albers/0125-bks-Albers-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 523, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/0125-bks-Albers/0125-bks-Albers-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 523}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/0125-bks-Albers/0125-bks-Albers-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 892, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/0125-bks-Albers/0125-bks-Albers-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1784, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/0125-bks-Albers/0125-bks-Albers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/0125-bks-Albers/0125-bks-Albers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/0125-bks-Albers/0125-bks-Albers-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Van Gogh,’ by Julian Bell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Starry Nights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Julian (1952- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Gogh, Vincent', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T17:39:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Albers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Albers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c5aa36f-3764-55d1-8986-6440ffb15616', 'word_count': 605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c5aa36f-3764-55d1-8986-6440ffb15616'}, {'abstract': 'A novel of van Gogh’s anguished time in Belgium.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/the-season-of-migration-by-nellie-hermann.html', 'snippet': 'A novel of van Gogh’s anguished time in Belgium.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Poor van Gogh. Impossibly tenderhearted yet forbiddingly irascible. Push-pulled by scorching genius and helpless servitude to his own morality. Before becoming a painter, he put in a disastrous stint at an evangelical school, his failure there due in part to his obstinacy. As one of his classmates put it, “he did not know what submission was.” But to the clarity and purity of his vision he submitted in full, to the point of madness, to the point of utter self-abnegation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Season of Migration,’ by Nellie Hermann', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sun in His Hands', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hermann, Nellie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Gogh, Vincent', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T17:39:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leah Hager Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leah', 'middlename': 'Hager', 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/659a9456-0860-53e0-8e84-d19e5b58984f', 'word_count': 612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/659a9456-0860-53e0-8e84-d19e5b58984f'}, {'abstract': 'Chefs are coming up with all sorts of inspiring ideas for grains.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/get-your-freekeh-on/', 'snippet': 'Chefs are coming up with all sorts of inspiring ideas for grains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chefs are coming up with all sorts of inspiring ideas for grains, and I was lucky enough to learn about some of them at last November’s Whole Grains: Breaking Barriers conference organized in Boston by Oldways and the Whole Grains Counsel. I moderated a panel of three local chefs: Ana Sortun of Oleana and Sarma, Jason Bond of Bondir, and Barry Maiden of Hungry Mother, as well as Liz l’Etoile of Four Stars Farms, a small Massachusetts farm that is producing and milling wheat, corn, rye, barley and hops. Boston area chefs are delighted that they can now get local grains, milled only days before delivery.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/17/science/recipehealthwellpromo/recipehealthwellpromo-articleLarge.