 None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/0be1b33b-5d98-5d6e-9a9d-fb595a9d10d8', 'word_count': 1317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0be1b33b-5d98-5d6e-9a9d-fb595a9d10d8'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Metcalf’s novel, set in Virginia, takes issue with the American idealization of the back-to-the-land life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/ben-metcalfs-against-the-country.html', 'snippet': 'Ben Metcalf’s novel, set in Virginia, takes issue with the American idealization of the back-to-the-land life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A remarkable thing about the novel is that it can incorporate almost anything — essays, short stories, mock memoirs, screenplays, emails — and remain a novel. This elasticity is also a sign that unlike, say, the epic or the ode, the novel is a living, evolving form. But if its outer limits are virtually nonexistent, the minimum requirement is generally that there be a narrator telling us something. In this way, any manner of book can find a way to justify calling itself a novel. But the label should not be worn lightly, since it invites scrutiny of the highest and most exacting kind. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25ZIOLKOWSKI/0125-bks-Ziolkowski-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25ZIOLKOWSKI/0125-bks-Ziolkowski-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 734}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25ZIOLKOWSKI/0125-bks-Ziolkowski-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 837, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25ZIOLKOWSKI/0125-bks-Ziolkowski-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1724, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25ZIOLKOWSKI/0125-bks-Ziolkowski-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25ZIOLKOWSKI/0125-bks-Ziolkowski-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25ZIOLKOWSKI/0125-bks-Ziolkowski-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ben Metcalf’s ‘Against the Country’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Southern Grotesque', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ziolkowski, Thad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Metcalf, Ben (1976- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T18:05:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thad Ziolkowski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ziolkowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c3d5bb3-c349-523b-9994-0d3ce2d9fb93', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c3d5bb3-c349-523b-9994-0d3ce2d9fb93'}, {'abstract': 'In Esther Freud’s novel, an artistic boy befriends the architect Charles Rennie Mackintosh in 1914.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/esther-freuds-mr-mac-and-me.html', 'snippet': 'In Esther Freud’s novel, an artistic boy befriends the architect Charles Rennie Mackintosh in 1914.', 'lead_paragraph': '“See everything. Hear everything. Say nothing,” reads a poster outside the town hall in Esther Freud’s evocative eighth novel, “Mr. Mac and Me.” It’s 1914, at the dawn of World War I, and the coastal Suffolk village of Walberswick is on high alert, its citizens patrolling the beaches and casting suspicious glances at newcomers, since anyone might be a spy. Our guide through this world is Thomas Maggs, age 13 when the book begins. The son of a drunk who runs the local pub, Thomas loves to draw (mostly ships) and dreams of being a sailor, though a pronounced limp, an overprotective mother and a controlling father conspire to keep him close to home.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25GRAVER/0125-bks-Graver-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 351, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25GRAVER/0125-bks-Graver-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 351}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25GRAVER/0125-bks-Graver-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/review/25GRAVER/0125-bks-Graver-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'w