rd_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf191ec2-fb94-5a8e-a36f-8b6409efee04'}, {'abstract': 'David Kynaston’s multivolume history of postwar Britain continues in this tapestry of political, socioeconomic and cultural change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/books/review/modernity-britain-1957-1962-by-david-kynaston.html', 'snippet': 'David Kynaston’s multivolume history of postwar Britain continues in this tapestry of political, socioeconomic and cultural change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On May 25, 1962, two decades after the original medieval building had been bombed by the Germans, the new Coventry Cathedral was consecrated. Alas, the service was a fiasco. Queen Elizabeth’s yellow outfit clashed with the canary-hued cope worn by the bishop of Coventry. The city’s lord mayor, whether overawed or overheated, fainted in a cacophony of metal regalia and had to be revived by the bishop of Birmingham. An unfortunate sidesman dropped the overflowing collection plate right in front of Her Majesty. Even the building, designed by Sir Basil Spence, failed for some to live up to the occasion: “The worst setback to English church architecture for a very long time,” the ultramodernist critic Reyner Banham sniffed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/25ALDOUS/25ALDOUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/books/25ALDOUS/25ALDOUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 553}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/books/25ALDOUS/25ALDOUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 944, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 