 'Antony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Currie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24e03691-77a1-502c-84bc-80e3a213f82f', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24e03691-77a1-502c-84bc-80e3a213f82f'}, {'abstract': 'In cities across Iran, tens of thousands of people took to the streets to protest against the satirical newspaper Charlie Hebdo for its cartoon of the Prophet Muhammad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/24/world/middleeast/protesters-in-iran-condemn-charlie-hebdo-over-muhammad-cartoon.html', 'snippet': 'In cities across Iran, tens of thousands of people took to the streets to protest against the satirical newspaper Charlie Hebdo for its cartoon of the Prophet Muhammad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEHRAN — Iranian protesters, encouraged by state-sanctioned Friday Prayer leaders, condemned the French satirical newspaper Charlie Hebdo for publishing a cartoon of the Prophet Muhammad.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Protesters in Iran Condemn Charlie Hebdo Over Muhammad Cartoon', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charlie Hebdo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-23T19:39:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Erdbrink', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erdbrink', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b709ace1-2c5f-53cb-a1ac-67cfe2e46b51', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b709ace1-2c5f-53cb-a1ac-67cfe2e46b51'}, {'abstract': 'On the eve of the Paris shows (and the release of her next novel), the author shares the details of her fashionable life there with T.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/danielle-steel-couture-week-icon/', 'snippet': 'On the eve of the Paris shows (and the release of her next novel), the author shares the details of her fashionable life there with T.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As familiar a sight at the Paris couture shows as models and gowns is Danielle Steel: author of 139 books that have sold 650 million copies; member of the Ordre des Arts et des Lettres and Chevalier of the Légion d’honneur; Guinness world record holder for longest time spent on The New York Times best-seller list; mother of nine and all-around avatar of elegance. Though Steel, who divides her time between San Francisco and Paris, keeps busy writing several books per year, if there’s one thing that will tear her away from her desk, it’s fashion. “I grew up going to haute couture shows, and began taking my daughters when they were very little girls,” she says. “Couture is an extraordinary experience and a dying art; each show is a thrill. It is art in one of its finest and most magical forms.” Here, on the eve of couture week (and the release of her next novel, “Prodigal Son,” out Feb. 24 from Random House), Steel shared the details of her fashionable life in Paris (written, of course, in an email). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/23/t-magazine/23steel-gilchrist/23steel-gilchrist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600