'headline': {'main': 'Ocwen Reaches Settlement With California Regulator', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-24T00:19:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Corkery', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corkery', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0135fea7-0414-5d90-8680-2f8c6f7ebc2a', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0135fea7-0414-5d90-8680-2f8c6f7ebc2a'}, {'abstract': 'With “Private Lives,” now in Hartford, Noël Coward wrote about ex-spouses who are reminded how the “ex-” got there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/nyregion/a-review-of-noel-cowards-private-lives-in-hartford.html', 'snippet': 'With “Private Lives,” now in Hartford, Noël Coward wrote about ex-spouses who are reminded how the “ex-” got there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Noël Coward’s “Private Lives” is to be believed — and with this shiny, new, perfectly polished production at Hartford Stage, how can it not be? — sexual mores in England changed a great deal between 1924 and 1930. There is poor Lady Mary in the recent episode of “Downton Abbey,” an elegant young widow being preached to by her grandmother because she has spent the night in a hotel with a man to whom she is not married. Then there is the rather cavalier Amanda Chase Prynne, the heroine of “Private Lives,” casually informing her ex-husband, Elyot Chase, of the men she has had affairs with since their divorce five years ago.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/nyregion/25ARTSCT1/25ARTSCT1-articleLarge.jp