om', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Belichick, Bill', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-24T02:04:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Rohan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bac6f743-e868-5229-bff6-b0c0aa504bf5', 'word_count': 1388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bac6f743-e868-5229-bff6-b0c0aa504bf5'}, {'abstract': 'The secretary of state called for efforts to reduce unemployment and other programs that could dissuade young people from joining militant causes, even if such initiatives were costly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/24/world/kerry-at-davos-urges-multipronged-fight-against-violent-extremism.html', 'snippet': 'The secretary of state called for efforts to reduce unemployment and other programs that could dissuade young people from joining militant causes, even if such initiatives were costly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVOS, Switzerland — Secretary of State John Kerry on Friday called for an ambitious campaign to combat the root causes of violent extremism, which he said represented one of the most grave threats to international order since the end of World War II.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kerry, at Davos, Urges Multipronged Fight Against Violent Extremism', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kerry, at Davos, Urges Multipronged Fight Against Violent Extremism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Davos (Switzerland)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-24T02:18:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael R. Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc033560-4337-53d0-8596-cba7de2eef8d', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc033560-4337-53d0-8596-cba7de2eef8d'}, {'abstract': 'The introduction of the HoloLens by Microsoft brought back memories of the history of its Kinect device.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/23/microsofts-holographic-vision-triggers-a-flashback/', 'snippet': 'The introduction of the HoloLens by Microsoft brought back memories of the history of its Kinect device.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — This week, Microsoft did what it usually does when it has an intriguing new technology that isn’t fully baked: It released a video. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Microsoft’s Holographic Vision Triggers a Flashback', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Virtual Reality (Computers)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Xbox One (Video Game System)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-24T02:30:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Wingfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wingfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c39b6ace-ab88-5c14-9efd-1fa9de3fde5b', 'word_count': 727, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c39b6ace-ab88-5c14-9efd-1fa9de3fde5b'}, {'abstract': 'China’s desire for everything from tiger parts to copper is threatening the environment and political stability.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/24/opinion/the-plunder-of-myanmar.html', 'snippet': 'China’s desire for everything from tiger parts to copper is threatening the environment and political stability.', 'lead_paragraph': 'China’s exploding appetites have unleashed a wholesale looting of Myanmar’s valuable natural resources. While this often involves outright theft, it also comes in the form of crony capitalism. Myanmar’s military elite has deals with Chinese companies that are eager to exploit the land, with little concern for the environment or people. The Kachin Independence Army, a rebel group that controls part of northeastern Myanmar, also takes a cut of some of this trade with China, especially jade hacked out of the earth by impoverished, heroin-addicted laborers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Plunder of Myanmar', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Plunder of Myanmar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thein Sein', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-24T02:34:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ae1bbf0-8ce0-575a-abd6-f885ba5d4b00', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ae1bbf0-8ce0-575a-abd6-f885ba5d4b00'}, {'abstract': 'New emergency measures are in place at Aqueduct Racetrack. More may be needed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/24/opinion/raced-to-death-at-the-big-a.html', 'snippet': 'New emergency measures are in place at Aqueduct Racetrack. More may be needed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The tragic degradation of thoroughbred racing continues at New York’s Aqueduct Racetrack, where the fatality rate for horses — 7.8 per 1,000 horses — has reached more than four times the national average as heedless owners put unproven horses at risk for lucrative purses. Twelve horses broke down in competition and had to be put to death in just 22 days in the track’s current winter meet. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/opinion/24RacehorseWEB/24RacehorseWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/24/opinion/24RacehorseWEB/24RacehorseWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/24/opinion/24RacehorseWEB/24RacehorseWEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}