'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Park City (Utah)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-24T20:02:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b81f247-986c-589d-8fda-125cac3b63c9', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b81f247-986c-589d-8fda-125cac3b63c9'}, {'abstract': 'A program at the 92nd Street Y offers works by Schubert and by contemporary composers he influenced.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/arts/music/moments-in-time-with-schubert.html', 'snippet': 'A program at the 92nd Street Y offers works by Schubert and by contemporary composers he influenced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Schubert’s music, in which a single moment can blissfully expand into a spacious, contemplative arc, offers an ideal forum to explore the concept of time. When devising the program for his two coming concerts at the 92nd Street Y, the pianist Shai Wosner was inspired by differing notions of time — comparing the multitasking, contemporary 140-character attention span with Schubert’s era, when a seven-minute piece was deemed short. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE2/25GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 878, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE2/25GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 878}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE2/25GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 701, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE2/25GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1401, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE2/25GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE2/25GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE2/25GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Moments in Time With Schubert', 'kicker': 'Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moments in Time With Schubert', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-24T20:11:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50fe7d2b-c987-523d-a229-f65f06903077', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50fe7d2b-c987-523d-a229-f65f06903077'}, {'abstract': 'Five performers offer kinetic explorations, both subtle and athletic, of what it means to be alone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/25/arts/dance/the-meaning-of-being-alone.html', 'snippet': 'Five performers offer kinetic explorations, both subtle and athletic, of what it means to be alone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Solitudes solo,” the Montreal choreographer Daniel Léveillé highlights five performers — Mathieu Campeau, Esther Gaudette, Justin Gionet, Jason Martin and Emmanuel Proulx — who offer kinetic explorations, both subtle and athletic, of what it means to be alone. This grouping of spare, elegant dances, performed in silence and to Bach, also introduces a new performance series at Abrons Arts Center: Travelogues, organized by Laurie Uprichard, formerly the director of Danspace Project and the Dublin Dance Festival. During Ms. Uprichard’s tenure at Danspace Project, Mr. Léveillé’s unadorned choreography was regularly on display; his 2012 production, which features Marc Parent’s austere lighting, is a chance to revisit Mr. Léveillé’s unaffected choreographic voice. The series will continue in May with a shared program by Kimberly Bartosik and Dylan Crossman, as well as in October with separate engagements by the Irish company ponydance and the Los Angeles choreographer Lionel Popkin. \xa0 (Friday and Saturday at 8 p.m., Sunday at 3 p.m., Abrons Arts Center, 466 Grand Street, at Pitt Street, Lower East Side; 212-352-3101, abronsartscenter.org). ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE3/25GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 284, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE3/25GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 284}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/25/arts/25GUIDE3/25GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 484, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': 