ong-abandoned train station where you can buy a ticket straight to hell.”) The long-gestating musical, from the songwriters John Kander and Fred Ebb (“Chicago”) and the playwright Terrence McNally, arrives on Broadway this spring. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/26/theater/26visit/26visit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 943, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/26/theater/26visit/26visit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 943}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/26/theater/26visit/26visit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 652, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/26/theater/26visit/26visit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1584, 'width': 1008, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/26/theater/26visit/26visit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/26/theater/26visit/26visit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/26/theater/26visit/26visit-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind the Poster: ‘The Visit’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Posters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rivera, Chita', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-26T19:59:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab2f80a0-e34f-5c85-a7de-4ed206308273', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab2f80a0-e34f-5c85-a7de-4ed206308273'}, {'abstract': 'The South African artist Robyn Orlin and the dancers of Compagnie Jant-Bi explore issues surrounding gender and homophobia in African life, at Montclair State University.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/27/arts/dance/robyn-orlin-and-compagnie-jant-bi-evoke-senegalese-tradition.html', 'snippet': 'The South African artist Robyn Orlin and the dancers of Compagnie Jant-Bi explore issues surrounding gender and homophobia in African life, at Montclair State University.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTCLAIR, N.J. — Some choreographers build up to audience participation, or infiltration, during the course of an evening, but not the South African artist Robyn Orlin. Within the first five minutes of her expansively titled “At the same time we were pointing a finger at you, we realized we were pointing three at ourselves,” her fluorescent-clad dancers clamber into the audience, swatting at the seats and the people in them with rubber flip-flops.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/arts/27ORLIN/27ORLIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/27/arts/27ORLIN/27ORLIN-articl