None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mariinsky Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-26T20:44:41+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/1dc95ad0-1b50-5d2b-8c32-66625a4662d4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/1dc95ad0-1b50-5d2b-8c32-66625a4662d4'}, {'abstract': 'After wrapping up a Chopin weekend at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, the Mariinsky Ballet is bringing a program of late-19th- and early-20th-century choreography to the Kennedy Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/27/arts/dance/mariinsky-ballet-concludes-brooklyn-run-with-chopin.html', 'snippet': 'After wrapping up a Chopin weekend at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, the Mariinsky Ballet is bringing a program of late-19th- and early-20th-century choreography to the Kennedy Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A reader recently left a voice mail message telling me, “The Russians invented ballet!” No, they certainly didn’t, but you can see why many people believe that they must have — especially the Mariinsky Ballet of St. Petersburg. Last weekend, the company ended a two-week season of three different programs at the Brooklyn Academy of Music; this week it moves on to the Kennedy Center in Washington with a program of late-19th- and early-20th-century choreography. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tweaking an Illustrious Tradition to Incorporate Western Notions ', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tweaking an Illustrious Tradition to Incorporate Western Notions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank':