ming-at-britains-glass-ceilings-gets-results/', 'snippet': 'The fund chief executive’s campaign to put women on British boards has succeeded by concentrating on corporate leaders and on company returns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Suggest to Helena Morrissey that she is Britain’s version of Sheryl Sandberg, and she smiles politely.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/business/morrissey/morrissey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/27/business/morrissey/morrissey-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/business/morrissey/morrissey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/business/morrissey/morrissey-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1343, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/business/morrissey/morrissey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/27/business/morrissey/morrissey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/business/morrissey/morrissey-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Helena Morrissey, Aiming at Britain’s Glass Ceilings, Gets Results', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aiming at Glass Ceilings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boards of Directors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morrissey, Helena', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-27T00:11:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenny Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09c1bb46-4d62-50a3-a3f0-b51cc0214925', 'word_count': 1676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09c1bb46-4d62-50a3-a3f0-b51cc0214925'}, {'abstract': 'Greek debt is a fiction, Greek surpluses are real.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/26/greece-think-flows-not-stocks/', 'snippet': 'Greek debt is a fiction, Greek surpluses are real.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How should we think about the bargaining that may or may not now take place between the new Greek government and the troika? (No bargaining if the troika basically says no concessions.) Most discussion is framed in terms of what happens to the debt. But as both Daniel Davies and James Galbraith point out — with very different de facto value judgments, but never mind for now — at this point Greek debt, measured as a stock, is not a very meaningful number. After all, the great bulk of the debt is now officially held, the interest rate bears little relationship to market prices, and the interest payments come in part out of funds lent by the creditors. In a sense the debt is an accounting fiction; it’s whatever the governments trying to dictate terms to Greece decide to say it is.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greece: Think Flows, Not Stocks', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-27T00:26:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b0a06ec-7347-5d03-8c3b-7c384b3fd591', 'word_count': 670, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b0a06ec-7347-5d03-8c3b-7c384b3fd591'}, {'abstract': 'Denise Colella, chief executive of Maxifier, recalls a flight where, traveling in a “ridiculous” outfit, she found herself seated next to an old acquaintance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/27/business/not-dressed-to-impress-and-running-into-a-high-school-classmate.html', 'snippet': 'Denise Colella, chief executive of Maxifier, recalls a flight where, traveling in a “ridiculous” outfit, she found herself seated next to an old acquaintance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’M lucky enough to have a lot of friends all around the world, which makes business travel kind of fun. I’ve been flying my entire career, and now as chief executive of Maxifier, an advertising technology company, I’m traveling as much as ever.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/business/27FLIER/27FLIER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/27/business/27FLIER/27FLIER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewid