on_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e304d76e-fdf1-59ad-a377-375162cb65b1', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e304d76e-fdf1-59ad-a377-375162cb65b1'}, {'abstract': 'Do you have any snow day rituals? Do you like to play in the snow? Would you go sledding or have a snowball fight? Or would you rather stay inside and keep warm?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/27/what-would-you-do-on-a-snow-day/', 'snippet': 'Do you have any snow day rituals? Do you like to play in the snow? Would you go sledding or have a snowball fight? Or would you rather stay inside and keep warm?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Officials from New York City warned that the winter storm bearing down on the region this week could be one of the most powerful blizzards ever recorded. That might well mean a snow day for millions of students from New Jersey to Maine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Would You Do on a Snow Day?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-27T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e14eb85-4205-5500-9a47-7c4095c33b65', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e14eb85-4205-5500-9a47-7c4095c33b65'}, {'abstract': 'Rather than flee to safe havens in America or Israel, some 2,700 Jews who survived Auschwitz returned to their European town in what is now Romania.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/27/staying-put-and-bearing-witness-to-the-holocaust/', 'snippet': 'Rather than flee to safe havens in America or Israel, some 2,700 Jews who survived Auschwitz returned to their European town in what is now Romania.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While recent terror attacks and anti-Semitism have led a growing number of French Jews to flee to Israel, another European Jewish community is determined to stay put, and has been for a long time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Staying Put and Bearing Witness to the Holocaust', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-27T10:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rena Silverman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silverman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7991296-fdaf-54c8-9e18-45bbb8ef1ff7', 'word_count': 830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7991296-fdaf-54c8-9e18-45bbb8ef1ff7'}, {'abstract': 'Like my novels, it enabled me to make sense of a world that often seemed senseless.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/27/psychotherapy-as-a-kind-of-art/', 'snippet': 'Like my novels, it enabled me to make sense of a world that often seemed senseless.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the day, some decades ago, that I sent off the manuscript of what would become my sixth published book, I was suddenly possessed — there is no other word — by the desire to leave this world, and to do so by stepping in front of an oncoming bus. I walked to the edge of the sidewalk, stepped down, hesitated, let the bus go by, and decided to go home, where, if one of my children, then ages 4, 2 and 1, defied me in any way, I imagined picking that child up and throwing the child against a wall or through a window.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/04/opinion/opinionator-pog-couch/opinionator-pog-couch-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/04/opinion/opinionator-pog-couch/opinionator-pog-couch-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/04/opinion/opinionator-pog-couch/opinionator-pog-couch-jumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/04/opinion/opinionator-pog-couch/opinionator-pog-couch-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/04/opinion/opinionator-pog-couch/opinionator-pog-couch-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/04/opinion/opinionator-pog-couch/opinionator-pog-couch-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/04/opinion/opinionator-pog-couch/opinionator-pog-couch-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Psychotherapy as a Kind of Art', 'kicker': 'Couch', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None,