anization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/b3c307be-121a-5fcf-a8b9-2c658ebabce7', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/b3c307be-121a-5fcf-a8b9-2c658ebabce7'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: King George conferred the Victoria Cross to an Indian soldier in 1915.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/27/1915-indian-receives-victoria-cross/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: King George conferred the Victoria Cross to an Indian soldier in 1915.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — At Buckingham Palace, yesterday, King George conferred the Victoria Cross on Sepoy Khudadad Khan, the first Indian soldier to earn the coveted distinction. Khudadad Khan, or the 129th Duke of Connaught’s Own Baluchis, was the sole survivor of a gallant machine-gun team, who on October 30 heroically fought to the last, every man being bayoneted at his post. Though badly wounded, Khudadad Khan managed some time afterward to rejoin his company, but never left his gun until he had rendered it useless to the enemy. — The New York Herald, European Edition, Jan. 28, 1915', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/blogs/27iht-old28/27iht-old28-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 552, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/blogs/27iht-old28/27iht-old28-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 552, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/27/blogs/27iht-old28/27iht-old28-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': '