ndu Goel and Michael J. de la Merced report in The New York Times. Shareholders will directly reap all the remaining profit from Yahoo’s investment, which cost almost nothing a decade ago but is now worth about $39.5 billion. The Alibaba stake alone now makes up nearly 85 percent of Yahoo’s market value. Shares of Yahoo rose about 7 percent in after-hours trading after disclosure of the plan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Yahoo’s Alibaba Split', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T12:34:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c5a5d61-75f5-5bec-a775-9dbbaaa8c0a7', 'word_count': 1982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c5a5d61-75f5-5bec-a775-9dbbaaa8c0a7'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Foner, a historian, explores the network of blacks and whites who helped slaves escape to freedom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/gateway-to-freedom-by-eric-foner.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Foner, a historian, explores the network of blacks and whites who helped slaves escape to freedom.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I dreaded the approach of summer, when snakes and slaveholders made their appearance,” wrote Harriet Jacobs, author, educator, servant, mother of two — and escaped slave. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01BAKER/01baker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 594, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01BAKER/01baker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 594, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01BAKER/01baker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01BAKER/01baker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Gateway to Freedom,’ by Eric Foner', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fugitives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baker, Kevin (1958- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foner, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Underground Railroad (19th Century)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Gateway to Freedom: The Hidden History of the Underground Railroad (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T13:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d279c7bc-0c3d-5d20-a339-feb85a696f4f', 'word_count': 1271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d279c7bc-0c3d-5d20-a339-feb85a696f4f'}, {'abstract': 'Yasmina Reza’s novel tells interlinked stories of yearning.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/yasmina-rezas-happy-are-the-happy.html', 'snippet': 'Yasmina Reza’s novel tells interlinked stories of yearning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like a handful of the 18 people who populate her latest novel, “Happy Are the Happy,” Yasmina Reza is formidably accomplished. A prolific novelist and playwright — two of her works for the stage, “Art” and “God of Carnage,” won Tony Awards for best play — as well as a screenwriter and actress, she haunts the halls of power in her native France. “Happy Are the Happy” is another coup, a quick and delicate book that’s as funny as it is humane. Each of its short chapters, titled with the name of the character speaking, is told in the first person and concerns, for the most part, other characters in the book soon to introduce themselves if they haven’t already. Everyone gets a word in, and the style is feathery as gossip. Characters chime with one another in ways they never realize, a conviviality that is bittersweet. Their voices are self-aware, a little jaundiced, vulnerable, sometimes plaintive, and entirely authentic. Accomplishment, Reza suggests, is no hedge against yearning or regret. Status, wealth, celebrity, sex appeal and good health are unevenly distributed and \xadunderappreciated, until they are in decline. Life isn’t sparkly, it’s a slog: “People, practically oldsters, lurching around Paris, bearing their lives.” Perhaps chasing the impractical is simply a way to stay in motion.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01SIEFF/01sieff-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01SIEFF/01sieff-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 851}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01SIEFF/01sieff-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01SIEFF/01sieff-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1445, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01SIEFF/01SIEFF-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01SIEFF/01SIEFF-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01SIEFF/01SIEFF-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Yasmina Reza’s ‘Happy Are the Happy’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love Actually', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reza, Yasmina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T13:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gemma Sieff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gemma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sieff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/24043a2c-bd9a-55a7-9a41-747a4f17482d', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24043a2c-bd9a-55a7-9a41-747a4f17482d'}, {'abstract': 'The painter Kehinde Wiley, the subject of a coming Brooklyn Museum retrospective, is known for vibrant, photo-based portraits of young black men in poses culled from art history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/arts/design/kehinde-wiley-puts-a-classical-spin-on-his-contemporary-subjects.html', 'snippet': 'The painter Kehinde Wiley, the subject of a coming Brooklyn Museum retrospective, is known for vibrant, photo-based portraits of young black men in poses culled from art history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kehinde Wiley began thinking about the stereotypes that shadow black men long before events in Ferguson, Mo., pushed the phrase “unarmed black man” back into the headlines and inaugurated a new wave of the civil rights movement. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kehinde Wiley Puts a Classical Spin on His Contemporary Subjects', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A New Royalty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Solomon, Deborah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiley, Kehinde', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T13:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c660dd5-6935-556e-b39e-f01b000dbe9c', 'word_count': 1934, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c660dd5-6935-556e-b39e-f01b000dbe9c'}, {'abstract': 'Loretta E. Lynch’s dealings with the Republican-led Senate Judiciary Committee were mostly cordial as she faced questions on immigration and other hot-button issues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/29/us/politics/lynch-attorney-general-congress-confirmation-hearing.html', 'snippet': 'Loretta E. Lynch’s dealings with the Republican-led Senate Judiciary Committee were mostly cordial as she faced questions on immigration and other hot-button issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Again and again, Senate Republicans made one thing clear Wednesday: They would not vote to confirm Eric H. Holder Jr. as attorney general. They were less passionate, however, about the person they were considering to replace him, Loretta E. Lynch.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Criticism of Holder Dominates Hearing on Loretta Lynch, Attorney General’s Possible Successor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Criticism of Holder Dominates Senate Panel’s Hearing on Possible Successor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lynch, Loretta E', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hulse, Carl', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holder, Eric H Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Attorneys General', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on the Judiciary', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T13:13:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Hulse and Matt Apuzzo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qu