Dear Diary:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Talented Acquaintance at the Gym', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T13:30:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sonia Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sonia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbafdd7b-c34f-5c66-bc69-b1084004c028', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbafdd7b-c34f-5c66-bc69-b1084004c028'}, {'abstract': 'A neighborhood in north-central Queens deemed by the city to have the most heavily Latin American immigrant community in the borough.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/realestate/corona-queens-affordable-with-latin-flavor.html', 'snippet': 'A neighborhood in north-central Queens deemed by the city to have the most heavily Latin American immigrant community in the borough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A walk through Corona shows ample evidence of a recent finding that it is home to the most heavily Latin American immigrant community in Queens. Colombian bakeries, Mexican taquerias and Dominican lunch counters dot commercial areas. Spanish conversations fill the streets. And sporting goods stores are more likely to display Ecuadorean national soccer jerseys than fan gear from the Jets and Giants.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corona, Queens, Affordable, With Latin Flavor', 'kicker': 'Living In', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Affordable, With Latin Flavor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Corona (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T13:58:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vera Haller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vera', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/234161e9-c37e-568f-b234-90b1336bda4e', 'word_count': 1272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/234161e9-c37e-568f-b234-90b1336bda4e'}, {'abstract': 'Not even carrying a child could keep Lynsey Addario from going to the world’s most dangerous places.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/magazine/what-can-a-pregnant-photojournalist-cover-everything.html', 'snippet': 'Not even carrying a child could keep Lynsey Addario from going to the world’s most dangerous places.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You have two options when you approach a hostile checkpoint in a war zone, and each is a gamble. The first is to stop and identify yourself as a journalist and hope that you are respected as a neutral observer. The second is to blow past the checkpoint and hope the soldiers guarding it don’t open fire on you.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/magazine/01addario_ss-slide-60QU/01addario_ss-slide-60QU-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/magazine/01addario_ss-slide-60QU/01addario_ss-slide-60QU-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlar