rganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8f48f89-c427-55c3-afa0-334b130e4cb7', 'word_count': 821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8f48f89-c427-55c3-afa0-334b130e4cb7'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/18/arts/television/whats-on-tv-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10 P.M. (13, 49) GRANTCHESTER James Norton (“Happy Valley”), above, portrays Sidney Chambers, a small-town vicar with a love of jazz, whiskey and complicated women who discovers an enthusiasm for sleuthing after a concerned parishioner compels him to investigate a grisly suicide in this new “Masterpiece Mystery!” series, based on the mysteries by James Runcie. “\xa0‘Grantchester’ will be breezy fun for fans of the form, though the more discerning will be put off by how rudimentary the actual murder mysteries are after being squeezed into 50 minutes (half the norm for this type of show),” Mike Hale wrote in The New York Times about what he called “cozy mysteries.” And while “it benefits from the presence of the veteran Robson Green (‘Wire in the Blood,’ ‘Touching Evil’) as Sidney’s sidekick, Inspector Keating,” Mr. Hale added, he found Mr. Norton, though amiable and good to look at, “a little dim”: “As religiously inclined young British sleuths go — and I apologize for a reference that only my fellow British-mystery zealots will understand — he’s no Hathaway.” In “Downton Abbey,” which leads in at 9, Lady Mary (Michelle Dockery) and Lord Gillingham (Tom Cullen) check into a Liverpool hotel — the better to check out their love. The art historian Simon Bricker (Richard E. Grant) turns his attention from a Piero della Francesca painting to its owner, Lady Cora (Elizabeth McGovern). A secret from the past of the Dowager Countess (Maggie Smith) resurfaces. And the police continue to investigate the death of Lord Gillingham’s valet.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T21:12:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/75286158-0f04-55a7-a096-4fccd42495ee', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75286158-0f04-55a7-a096-4fccd42495ee'}, {'abstract': 'The documentary “Ballet 422,” directed by Jody Lee Lipes, follows the choreographer Justin Peck.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/movies/ballet-422-a-dance-documentary-by-jody-lee-lipes.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary “Ballet 422,” directed by Jody Lee Lipes, follows the choreographer Justin Peck.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Justin Peck is not a lonely guy. He has a girlfriend, close colleagues, a loving family. But you wouldn’t necessarily know this from watching him in “Ballet 422,” the documentary that chronicles his third piece of choreography for New York City Ballet from creation to performance. In the film, to be released on Feb. 6, Mr. Peck is so intensely focused, so singular of purpose, that you wonder whether he has anything left for a personal life.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01LIPES1/01LIPES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 442, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01LIPES1/01LIPES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'x