 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CB2', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ART INSTITUTE OF CHICAGO', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T23:18:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fa2e2c2-643f-52d2-bc83-93378736ade3', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fa2e2c2-643f-52d2-bc83-93378736ade3'}, {'abstract': 'The national political conversation has progressed since the reflexive “tough on crime” days.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/28/loretta-lynch-revisits-an-ultra-long-prison-sentence/', 'snippet': 'The national political conversation has progressed since the reflexive “tough on crime” days.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Loretta Lynch fielded mostly predictable questions at her confirmation hearing on Wednesday. Senators weighing her nomination to be the next attorney general wondered: Does she believe President Obama has the legal authority to grant amnesty to five million undocumented immigrants? Will she just be another Eric Holder? Duke or UNC? And so on.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/28/opinion/1282015lynch/1282015lynch-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/28/opinion/1282015lynch/1282015lynch-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 376}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/28/opinion/1282015lynch/1282015lynch-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/28/opinion/1282015lynch/1282015lynch-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1284, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/28/opinion/1282015lynch/1282015lynch-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/28/opinion/1282015lynch/1282015lynch-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/28/opinion/1282015lynch/1282015lynch-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Loretta Lynch Revisits an Ultra-Long Prison Sentence', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gleeson, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-28T23:18:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Wegman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wegman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cdeb2e8-0355-5bf0-b5d0-6b64ba7b5e00', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cdeb2e8-0355-5bf0-b5d0-6b64ba7b5e00'}, {'abstract': 'Rebecca Louise Law’s art installations are constructed from cut flowers, often threaded on wire and suspended overhead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/29/garden/rebecca-louise-law-on-her-installation-at-the-viacom-building-in-times-square.html', 'snippet': 'Rebecca Louise Law’s art installations are constructed from cut flowers, often threaded on wire and suspended overhead.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xadThere were thistles, Kangaroo Paw, ranunculus and peonies, hydrangeas, delphiniums and over 40 varieties of roses. In all, 14 species of 16,000 individual flowers lay in sweet-smelling drifts on lunch tables set up last weekend at the Viacom building in Times Square, home of MTV. There, more than 200 volunteers threaded blooms onto lengths of copper wire that were then gathered into bundles and hung above the building’s two entrances and in the visitors’ center. Standing under them and looking up, you were caught in a different sort of blizzard than the one that was expected this week. It was spring, abstracted, designed and orchestrated by Rebecca Louise Law, a British artist whose medium is flowers.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/29/garden/29QNA1-copy/29QNA1-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/29/garden/29QNA1-copy/29QNA1-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/29/garden/29QNA1-copy/29QNA1-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/29/garden/29QNA1-copy/29QN