pe_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b5de0f7-2469-5208-bbfc-e94d7a11ce59', 'word_count': 566, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b5de0f7-2469-5208-bbfc-e94d7a11ce59'}, {'abstract': 'How a band of vigilantes helped defend a Nigerian city from Islamist militants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/28/magazine/the-people-vs-boko-haram.html', 'snippet': 'How a band of vigilantes helped defend a Nigerian city from Islamist militants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Through the fall and winter, the Islamist militant group Boko Haram has seemed nearly unstoppable. It has captured town after town in northeastern Nigeria and laid siege to the fishing hamlet Baga and nearby villages, killing hundreds of people. But over the weekend, the group finally suffered a significant setback. The militants tried to invade Maiduguri, a city of two million in the northeast, as well as two towns in the area, Monguno and Konduga. While Monguno — which is 80 miles north of Maiduguri and houses a military base — fell under Boko Haram control on Sunday, Maiduguri and Konduga held their ground, in no small part because of vigilantes who fought alongside the Nigerian military.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/28/magazine/28mag-28bokoharam/28mag-28bokoharam-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/28/magazine/28mag-28bokoharam/28mag-28bokoharam-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/28/magazine/28mag-28bokoharam/28mag-28bokoharam-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/28/magazine/28mag-28bokoh