ation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/512b31d6-3606-5772-9021-d2bdf302086d', 'word_count': 1207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/512b31d6-3606-5772-9021-d2bdf302086d'}, {'abstract': 'Some things do not change: Harvard’s $36 billion endowment is still the largest of any university, according to the 2014 NACUBO-Commonfund Study of Endowments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/29/us/harvards-endowment-remains-biggest-of-all.html', 'snippet': 'Some things do not change: Harvard’s $36 billion endowment is still the largest of any university, according to the 2014 NACUBO-Commonfund Study of Endowments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some things do not change: Harvard’s $36 billion endowment is still the largest of any university, according to the 2014 NACUBO-Commonfund Study of Endowments. The University of Texas System endowment, at $25 billion, was in second place, edging last year’s No. 2, Yale, at $24 billion. The average market value of college and university endowments increased 15.5 percent in the 2014 fiscal year, the study found, compared with 11.7 in 2013. A separate survey, by the Council for Aid for Education, found that charitable contributions to colleges and universities in the United States increased 10.8 percent in 2014, the largest increase since 2000. The five institutions that raised the most money took in more than 10 percent of the $37.45 billion in charitable contributions to higher education. In that study, too, Harvard led the pack, taking in $1.16 billion. Stanford was second, at $928 million.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Harvard’s Endowment Remains Biggest of All ', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Education', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Harvard’s Endowment Remains Biggest of All', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endowments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Texas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-29T01:12:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamar Lewin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d827cf50-73b7-5be8-8cea-7130a2dfaa45', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d827cf50-73b7-5be8-8cea-7130a2dfaa45'}, {'abstract': 'After new border restrictions were put in place, a policy of isolating and frustrating rebel-held regions has taken a major and maddening turn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/29/world/europe/ukraine-clamps-down-on-travel-to-and-from-rebel-areas.html', 'snippet': 'After new border restrictions were put in place, a policy of isolating and frustrating rebel-held regions has taken a major and maddening turn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DONETSK, Ukraine — For Larisa Horlova, a cashier, making the trip from rebel-held territory to Ukraine proper had been a routine matter, despite the shells that sometimes pounded into fields near the road as she drove. She did it every week or so to get money from her Ukrainian bank account, since A.T.M.s are no longer functioning in Donetsk.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/29/world/29UKRAINE1/29UKRAINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xla