lamic Republic News Agency said Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/29/world/middleeast/iran-washington-post-reporters-trial-to-be-soon-but-charges-are-unclear.html', 'snippet': 'Jason Rezaian, the Washington Post’s Tehran correspondent, who has been incarcerated since July 22, “will be tried soon,” Iran’s state-run Islamic Republic News Agency said Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Jason Rezaian, The Washington Post’s Tehran correspondent, who has been incarcerated for unexplained reasons since July 22, “will be tried soon,” Iran’s state-run Islamic Republic News Agency said Wednesday, but there was still no word on the charges he faces. The agency’s report came two weeks after it announced that Mr. Rezaian, 38, had been formally charged and that his case would be heard by Iran’s Revolutionary Court. Mr. Rezaian’s family, American officials and The Post have all urged Iran to free him and have questioned why the charges have not been made public. Mr. Rezaian, a dual Iranian-American citizen, has been denied access to a lawyer. Martin Baron, The Post’s executive editor, said in a statement this month, “It is appalling and outrageous that Jason remains behind bars.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran: Washington Post Reporter’s Trial to Be ‘Soon,’ but Charges Are Unclear', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran: Washington Post Reporter’s Trial to Be ‘Soon,’ but Charges Are Unclear', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rezaian, Jason', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Post', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-29T03:12:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c191271a-054a-53f0-b75e-f2dd0ecc6060', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c191271a-054a-53f0-b75e-f2dd0ecc6060'}, {'abstract': 'Russia’s Supreme Court on Thursday dismissed a government effort to shut down Memorial, the country’s oldest human rights advocacy group.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/29/world/europe/russia-court-rules-for-rights-group.html', 'snippet': 'Russia’s Supreme Court on Thursday dismissed a government effort to shut down Memorial, the country’s oldest human rights advocacy group.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Russia’s Supreme Court\xa0on Wednesday\xa0dismissed a government effort to shut down Memorial, the country’s oldest human rights advocacy group, which has been singled out for its investigations of Soviet-era and current civil violations. The Ministry of Justice had accused Memorial of structuring its organization in violation of its own charter. The judge threw out the lawsuit\xa0on Thursday\xa0after the Justice Ministry said Memorial had rectified the charter violation. In a separate case, Memorial is challenging a decision by the ministry to register it as a foreign agent, a term defined under a 2012 law as a nongovernmental organization that receives foreign funds and engages in politics. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Court Rules for Rights Group', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Court Rules for Rights Group', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roth, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Memorial', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court of Russia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-29T03:14:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Roth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/33b26ec4-36f7-5378-b635-d25467ae98bd', 'word_count': 104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33b26ec4-36f7-5378-b635-d25467ae98bd'}, {'abstract': 'Cuba’s former leader Fidel Castro met with Frei Betto, a Brazilian intellectual, this week, the first such meeting reported in the state-run Cuban news media since a recent swell of rumors that he was dead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/29/world/americas/cuba-party-paper-reports-on-meeting-of-fidel-castro-and-brazilian-author.html', 'snippet': 'Cuba’s former leader Fidel Castro met with Frei Betto, a Brazilian intellectual, this week, the first such meeting reported in the state-run Cuban news media since a recent swell of rumors that he was dead.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Cuba’s former leader Fidel Castro met with Frei Betto, a Brazilian intellectual, this week, the first such meeting reported in the state-run Cuban news media since a recent swell of rumors that Mr. Castro was dead. A brief article in Granma, the Communist Party’s official newspaper, reported late Tuesday that Mr. Castro had had a “friendly conversation” that day with Frei Betto, a leftist Dominican friar who wrote a book in the 1980s on Mr. Castro’s views on Christianity, “Fidel and Religion.” Granma did not publish a photograph of the meeting. On Monday Granma published Mr. Castro’s first remarks on the restoration of diplomatic relations with the United States, which was announced in December. His lack of comment then fueled intense speculation that he was dead or so mentally frail that he could not comment. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuba: Party Paper Reports on Meeting of Fidel Castro and Brazilian Author', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cuba: Party Paper Reports on Meeting of Fidel Castro and Brazilian Author', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burnett, Victoria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castro, Fidel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Betto, Frei', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-29T03:18:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victoria Burnett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victoria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burnett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/995b587d-f557-59da-aa19-4b509592bf9d', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/995b587d-f557-59da-aa19-4b509592bf9d'}, {'abstract