+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Food', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/14e529c8-77af-5622-a37c-47a1f666d27c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/14e529c8-77af-5622-a37c-47a1f666d27c'}, {'abstract': 'Sariling Atin, whose name in Tagalog means “our own,” opened in April in Elmhurst, Queens, on a block once dominated by furniture showrooms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/04/dining/hungry-city-sariling-atin-in-elmhurst-queens.html', 'snippet': 'Sariling Atin, whose name in Tagalog means “our own,” opened in April in Elmhurst, Queens, on a block once dominated by furniture showrooms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fish are stacked on the glass over the steam table, fried whole without interfering batter, all crackly veneer and quick-tearing flesh. There are galunggong, like swollen cigars with the flavor of a shyer mackerel; pompano in puckering silver; and, best of all, bangus (pronounced ban-GOOSE), or milkfish, flaky and sweet, stripped of bones and butterflied flat, but with the brittle fletch of a tail intact.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Buffet of Filipino Specialties', 'kicker': 'Hungry City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Buffet of Filipino Specialties', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mishan, Ligaya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Elmhurst (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sariling Atin (Queens, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-29T16:53:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya',