nowstorms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wysocki, Mark W', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-29T18:51:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4618761b-911b-5d4b-9fce-b80dc19c5333', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4618761b-911b-5d4b-9fce-b80dc19c5333'}, {'abstract': 'Tis the gift to be simplistic, tis the gift to be free.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/01/29/i-do-not-think-that-number-means-what-you-think-it-means/', 'snippet': 'Tis the gift to be simplistic, tis the gift to be free.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Rattner has a very puzzling piece in today’s Times. Leave aside the dismissal of the austerity debate — which has, if nothing else, been a dramatic illustration of the immense relevance of macroeconomic analysis — as “simplistic”; Dean Baker has that one covered. What really gets me is this chart:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I Do Not Think That Number Means What You Think It Means', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-29T18:51:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f9dd9c9-9ce8-5f70-ac85-abc3c5f65dad', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f9dd9c9-9ce8-5f70-ac85-abc3c5f65dad'}, {'abstract': 'A little defense, some kick returns, a lot of receptions and one critical pass make wide receiver Julian Edelman invaluable to the Patriots.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/sports/football/julian-edelman-embraces-role-as-patriots-swiss-army-knife.html', 'snippet': 'A little defense, some kick returns, a lot of receptions and one critical pass make wide receiver Julian Edelman invaluable to the Patriots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHOENIX — In professional sports, being listed as 6 feet tall often means you are somewhere between 5-8 and 5-11. The rounding up is considered a courtesy, since being undersize carries something of a stigma. Julian Edelman, New England’s jack-of-all-trades wide receiver, freely admits that he is 5 feet 10 even if the Patriots’ website used to give him a couple of extra inches.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/sports/30receivers/30receivers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/30/sports/30receivers/30receivers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/sports/30receivers/30receivers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 732, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/sports/30receivers/30receivers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1464, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/sports/30receivers/30receivers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail'