apher with The New York Times, talks about the differences between covering the White House and the Super Bowl.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Doug Mills, a staff photographer for The New York Times based in the Washington bureau for the better part of two decades, has seen it all: from presidential inaugurations to Super Bowls, from the campaign trail to court-side champions. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From the White House to the Super Bowl, With a Times Photographer', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-01-29T22:03:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb3dd9d2-e938-5158-bab3-024fe4c814c8', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb3dd9d2-e938-5158-bab3-024fe4c814c8'}, {'abstract': 'Art museum websites have become more useful, with entire collections online, some accompanied by essays and videos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/arts/design/art-museums-are-increasingly-adding-their-collections-online.html', 'snippet': 'Art museum websites have become more useful, with entire collections online, some accompanied by essays and videos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The construction of new art museum buildings like that of the Whitney Museum of American Art in Manhattan’s meatpacking district naturally receives a lot of attention. But there’s another kind of construction going on that tells more about where museums are at and where they are going than any shiny new edifice: their websites. That might sound surprising to anyone not professionally involved in museum work, but if you want to know how museums are changing their philosophies and programs in a time of increasing financial pressure and the continuing rise of private museums like Eli Broad’s in Los Angeles, websites are the most visible and informative places to look.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/arts/30MUSEUMSITESSUB/30MUSEUMSITESSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 789, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/30/arts/30MUSEUMSITESSUB/30MUSEUMSITESSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 789}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/arts/30MUSEUMSITESSUB/30MUSEUMSITESSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 779, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/arts/30MUSEUMSITESSUB/30MUSEUMSITESSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1303, 'width': 991, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/arts/30MUSEUMSITESSUB/30MUSEUMSITESSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/30/arts/30MUSEUMSITESSUB/30MUSEUMSITESSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/arts/30MUSEUMSITESSUB/30MUSEUMSITESSUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Detail Goes Unnoticed When Art Is a Click Away ', 'kicker':