r and better ways to socialize at college.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/opinion/skip-the-fraternity-parties.html', 'snippet': 'A reader urges women to find safer and better ways to socialize at college.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor: ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Skip the Fraternity Parties', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Skip the Fraternity Parties', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fraternities and Sororities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T08:27:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f005f09-72b6-5cea-a019-7cb78ff3a566', 'word_count': 218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f005f09-72b6-5cea-a019-7cb78ff3a566'}, {'abstract': 'Sandra G. Hassink, president of the American Academy of Pediatrics, responds to an online column by Charles M. Blow.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/opinion/child-health-and-poverty.html', 'snippet': 'Sandra G. Hassink, president of the American Academy of Pediatrics, responds to an online column by Charles M. Blow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Child Health and Poverty', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Child Health and Poverty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Anxiety and Stress', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Malnutrition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T08:27:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/74376285-d65b-5f12-b9eb-2be1d43033ab', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74376285-d65b-5f12-b9eb-2be1d43033ab'}, {'abstract': 'The wired classroom may actually widen the learning gap.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/opinion/can-students-have-too-much-tech.html', 'snippet': 'The wired classroom may actually widen the learning gap.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PRESIDENT OBAMA’s domestic agenda, which he announced in his State of the Union address this month, has a lot to like: health care, maternity leave, affordable college. But there was one thing he got wrong. As part of his promise to educate American children for an increasingly competitive world, he vowed to “protect a free and open Internet” and “extend its reach to every classroom and every community.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/30Pinker/30Pinker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 852, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/30Pinker/30Pinker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 852}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/30Pinker/30Pinker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 722, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/30Pinker/30Pinker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2044, 'width': 1440, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/30Pinker/30Pinker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/30Pinker/30Pinker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/30Pinker/30Pinker-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Students Have Too Much Tech?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can Students Have Too Much Tech?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T08:27:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Pinker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pinker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/19e9ccfd-499d-5b78-8700-4d1b070aedd8', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19e9ccfd-499d-5b78-8700-4d1b070aedd8'}, {'abstract': 'Credit the new secretary of veterans affairs for embracing, in Los Angeles, the once radical-sounding idea that the sick and the troubled need housing first.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/opinion/a-huge-victory-for-homeless-veterans.html', 'snippet': 'Credit the new secretary of veterans affairs for embracing, in Los Angeles, the once radical-sounding idea that the sick and the troubled need housing first.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Veterans Affairs Department is going to provide housing for disabled homeless veterans on the vast property it owns in Los Angeles. On Wednesday, it announced an agreement with plaintiffs in a lawsuit “that restores the West Los Angeles V.A. campus to serving veterans in need,” by housing them there, and by ending the practice of leasing parts of the 387-acre property to outside parties in deals “that do not meet the goal of serving veterans.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Huge Victory for Homeless Veterans', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Huge Victory for Homeless Veterans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDonald, Robert A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T08:27:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/24c761e4-86d2-5572-be75-1c0c585fc4ee', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24c761e4-86d2-5572-be75-1c0c585fc4ee'}, {'abstract': 'My ever-pregnant mother, a devout Catholic, agonized over what to do. If only Pope Francis had been around then.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/opinion/rabbits-and-rebels.html', 'snippet': 'My ever-pregnant mother, a devout Catholic, agonized over what to do. If only Pope Francis had been around then.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The babies came one after the other in my family, six in eight years’ time, then a miscarriage, a seventh, and finally a dire medical warning: An eighth child might leave the rest without the young woman who had brought us into this world. My mother went to her general practitioner first, then the Roman Catholic bishop. The doctor said stop. The cleric said shame if you do.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rabbits and Rebels', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T08:27:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ad27f3e-a5f7-58fa-9368-74de750242bb', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ad27f3e-a5f7-58fa-9368-74de750242bb'}, {'abstract': 'Can Europe get past the myths and moralizing, and deal with reality in a way that respects the Continents core values?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/opinion/paul-krugman-europes-greek-test.html', 'snippet': 'Can Europe get past the myths and moralizing, and deal with reality in a way that respects the Continents core values?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the five years (!) that have passed since the euro crisis began, clear thinking has been in notably short supply. But that fuzziness must now end. Recent events in Greece pose a fundamental challenge for Europe: Can it get past the myths and the moralizing, and deal with reality in a way that respects the Continent’s core values? If not, the whole European project — the attempt to build peace and democracy through shared prosperity — will suffer a terrible, perhaps mortal blow.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Europe’s Greek Test', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Europe’s Greek Test', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krugman, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eurozone', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T08:27:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4c2dae6-43ab-5ef1-aa9f-9dcc57379a1b', 'word_count': 818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4c2dae6-43ab-5ef1-aa9f-9dcc57379a1b'}, {'abstract': 'A sociology professor discusses growing inequality in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/opinion/defining-the-middle-class.html', 'snippet': 'A sociology professor discusses growing inequality in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Defining the Middle Class', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Defining the Middle Class', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3,