Let’s Go to the Dogs Tonight” (about her childhood in war-torn Rhodesia) and “Cocktail Hour Under the Tree of Forgetfulness” (about her indomitable mother). Fuller’s new book describes the collapse of her marriage, and in a recent interview with the Times reporter Penelope Green she bristled at the idea that she would have shown a draft to her ex-husband. “There’s almost this expectation you need to get approval,” she said. “I doubt Ernest Hemingway was asked what his ex-wives thought of his writing. I think women have to stop asking for permission.” She didn’t say, though, whether she had shared the memoir with her children, who are 21, 17 and 9. So I asked. “There’s a very fine line,” she said by phone from Washington, D.C., where she was on book tour, “between protecting your children, as you should, and smothering them to the point that they don’t know what’s going down in the world.” She had offered to show relevant passages to her older two, and Sarah, the 21-year-old, took her up on it. “She’s a fantastic writer in her own way,” Fuller said. “At the same time I was working on this book, she wrote an essay about the divorce for her student magazine. It was so poignant. My ex-husband read it and said, That’s not what happened. But of course it was totally from her perspective. It’s another prism. We’re reading each other’s stuff now not just as people who were personally involved, but as writers able to appreciate each other’s talent.” And her 17-year-old son? Fuller laughed. “He has better boundaries than anyone I know. He said, Mum, I lived it. I don’t need to read it.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/08InsideList/08InsideList-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 471, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/08InsideList/08InsideList-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 471, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/08InsideList/08InsideList-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/books/08InsideList/08InsideList-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/08InsideList/08InsideList-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:20:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4868322-07a5-5f32-9a14-e38dce2cc815', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4868322-07a5-5f32-9a14-e38dce2cc815'}, {'abstract': 'A year after the New York Fed raised questions, the inquiries are the latest to examine reporting procedures at one of Europe’s largest lenders.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2015/01/30/german-and-british-regulators-examining-deutsche-banks-controls-and-reporting/', 'snippet': 'A year after the New York Fed raised questions, t