97-5d77-b694-191c7cf57813', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ffc223c-f297-5d77-b694-191c7cf57813'}, {'abstract': 'A new law enables the creation of special accounts, modeled after the popular Section 529 education plans that many families use to save for college.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/28/business/special-savings-accounts-for-disabled-people.html', 'snippet': 'A new law enables the creation of special accounts, modeled after the popular Section 529 education plans that many families use to save for college.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People with disabilities, and their families, will soon have a tax-favored way to save money for a range of expenses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Law Creates Special Savings Accounts for Disabled People', 'kicker': 'Your Money Adviser', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Savings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:33:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f327a21-590b-5e1b-9463-575fc49571d0', 'word_count': 730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f327a21-590b-5e1b-9463-575fc49571d0'}, {'abstract': 'The characters of Thomas Pierce’s stories inhabit a strange world where reality meets irreality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/hall-of-small-mammals-bythomas-pierce.html', 'snippet': 'The characters of Thomas Pierce’s stories inhabit a strange world where reality meets irreality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was that moment starting in the late 1980s when the culture was seized with so-called recovered memories. Some were clearly real, but a surprising number couldn’t have been; they were too fantastic — elephants butchered in church basements, ordinary suburban parents gathered on the outskirts of town, in forests, to perform satanic rituals (though really, who wouldn’t thrill at the idea of one’s mother doing that while one slept?). A lot of the stories came from children, so one hesitated to call them “lies.” They were fictions. They were the great outrageous stories made up to express the more regular feelings of loss or disenchantment or anger.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Hall of Small Mammals,’ by\xa0Thomas Pierce', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Survival Strategies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pierce, Thomas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Hall of Small Mammals (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:35:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad775141-4bcd-5cc5-a8aa-8adad5602c3f', 'word_count': 612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad775141-4bcd-5cc5-a8aa-8adad5602c3f'}, {'abstract': 'Jenny Uglow’s multifaceted history of the British home front during the Napoleonic wars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/jenny-uglows-in-these-times.html', 'snippet': 'Jenny Uglow’s multifaceted history of the British home front during the Napoleonic wars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most Americans probably have an indistinct idea of the warfare between Britain and France at the turn of the 19th century. So far as Britain is concerned, we are likely to recall that Admiral Nelson won a great victory in 1805 at Trafalgar, off the coast of Spain, and was mortally wounded during the battle. In words that became famous, he had signaled to his fleet, “England expects that every man will do his duty.” We are likelier to remember the Duke of Wellington’s triumph at Waterloo 10 years later, which ended Napoleon’s sensational career. A comment Wellington supposedly made afterward is also frequently quoted, that the engagement had been “a damned close-run thing.” And as a sidelight to the international conflict there was our own American War of 1812, during which Francis Scott Key was inspired to write his verses about the rockets’ red glare and bombs bursting in air. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01DAMROSCH/01DAMROSCH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 584, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01DAMROSCH/01DAMROSCH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 584}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01DAMROSCH/01DAMROSCH-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 996, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01DAMROSCH/01DAMROSCH-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1993, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01DAMROSCH/01damrosch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01DAMROSCH/01damrosch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01DAMROSCH/01damrosch-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jenny Uglow’s ‘In These Times’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Roads to Waterloo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Uglow, Jenny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:35:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leo Damrosch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Damrosch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/09ec1a72-6224-5814-85ad-215fc6a6e8b3', 'word_count': 1223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09ec1a72-6224-5814-85ad-215fc6a6e8b3'}, {'abstract': 'A French philosopher asks what drones mean for the moral basis of war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/a-theory-of-the-drone-by-gregoire-chamayou.html', 'snippet': 'A French philosopher asks what drones mean for the moral basis of war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For thousands of years, the outstanding characteristic of war was the violent interaction known as fighting. It was the fighters’ willingness and ability to cope with and overcome danger that distinguished them from assassins and often made them into heroes — alive or dead — deserving of the highest rewards society could bestow.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01CREVELD/01creveld-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01CREVELD/01creveld-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01CREVELD/01creveld-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01CREVELD/01creveld-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1403, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01CREVELD/01creveld-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01CREVELD/01creveld-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01CREVELD/01creveld-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Theory of the Drone,’ by Grégoire Chamayou', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rise of the 