www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/letters-among-the-disrupted.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent essay by Leon Wieseltier.', 'lead_paragraph': '‘Among the Disrupted’', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: ‘Among the Disrupted’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:35:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cabd0c7c-0ad2-585f-ae1f-5cb303f9f76b', 'word_count': 2684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cabd0c7c-0ad2-585f-ae1f-5cb303f9f76b'}, {'abstract': 'A psychological thriller of intersecting lives and betrayals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/the-girl-on-the-train-by-paula-hawkins.html', 'snippet': 'A psychological thriller of intersecting lives and betrayals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alfred Hitchcock may have said all there is to say about the fallibility of making assumptions about what you see through a window, but, like most important lessons, this one can bear some repeating. To the limited scope of a window frame, the former London journalist Paula Hawkins, in her debut thriller, “The Girl on the Train,” offers a few additional obfuscations. First, her novel’s protagonist, Rachel, looks out through the window of a moving train on her daily commute. Second, Rachel is your basic hot mess: depressed, unemployed, still in mourning for the death of her marriage and prone to alcoholic blackouts that coincide with critical moments in the tale of a missing woman later found dead. Rachel might as well be wearing a sign that reads “Unreliable Narrator.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Girl on the Train,’ by Paula Hawkins', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forget What You Saw', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Korelitz, Jean Hanff', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hawkins, Paula (1972- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Girl on the Train (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:35:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jean Hanff Korelitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': 'Hanff', 'lastname': 'Korelitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/51decdbd-0ec1-5693-9e35-6a67d5591388', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51decdbd-0ec1-5693-9e35-6a67d5591388'}, {'abstract': 'Debunking the myths and stereotypes about France.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/they-eat-horses-dont-they-by-piu-marie-eatwell.html', 'snippet': 'Debunking the myths and stereotypes about France.', 'lead_paragraph': 'French women do get fat. Their children do throw food. And they don’t look the other way when their husbands cheat.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘They Eat \xadHorses, Don’t They?’ by Piu Marie Eatwell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Les Visiteurs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eatwell, Piu Marie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:35:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Warner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/04229479-5c2c-5c82-aca4-35117dba4ddd', 'word_count': 768, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04229479-5c2c-5c82-aca4-35117dba4ddd'}, {'abstract': 'From Lithuania to South Africa, England, America and Israel, a journalist chronicles his family’s story of repeated upheaval.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/the-girl-from-human-street-by-roger-cohen.html', 'snippet': 'From Lithuania to South Africa, England, America and Israel, a journalist chronicles his family’s story of repeated upheaval.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the years around the turn of the 20th century, Roger Cohen’s family joined the mass exodus of Jews out of their long, mostly dismal residency in the Pale of Settlement. Like thousands of their Lithuanian landsmen, Cohen’s relatives headed for the sunny, still-undeveloped, resource-\xadrich country of South Africa. In this new world they began with nothing, worked hard and achieved much. As is often true of successful immigrant families, their grandchildren could live as though the old world had never been. Cohen’s father became a doctor, his mother was college educated. Both were raised with the comforts that only numerous servants can provide: “They never had to boil an egg,” Cohen writes. Being a Jew in South Africa was not unlike being a Jew in the American South: Anti-Semitism was not absent, but it was greatly mitigated by a segregated black underclass all but legally enslaved.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/31/books/review/01gallagher-sub/01gallagher-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 319, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/31/books/review/01gallagher-sub/01gallagher-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 319}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/31/books/review/01gallagher-sub/01gallagher-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 545, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/31/books/review/01gallagher-sub/01gallagher-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1090, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/31/books/review/01gallagher-sub/01gallagher-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/31/books/review/01gallagher-sub/01gallagher-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/31/books/review/01gallagher-sub/01gallagher-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Girl From Human Street,’ by Roger Cohen', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Those Who Went Before', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Roger', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:35:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dorothy Gallagher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dorothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gallagher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dae1af41-66b1-59e4-aa88-f2cb7afc5454', 'word_count': 1193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dae1af41-66b1-59e4-aa88-f2cb7afc5454'}, {'abstract': 'In Joyce Carol Oates’s novel, a teenage girl is the alleged victim of a brutal rape.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/the-sacrifice-by-joyce-carol-oates.html', 'snippet': 'In Joyce Carol Oates’s novel, a teenage girl is the alleged victim of a brutal rape.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Writing difference is a challenge, particularly in fiction. How do men write women and vice versa? How do writers of one race or ethnicity write about people of another race or ethnicity? More important, how do writers tackle difference without reducing their characters to caricatures or stereotypes? Some handle the challenge with aplomb. Bill Cheng’s “Southern Cross the Dog” and Louise Erdrich’s “The Round House” come to mind. Others fail: At one point in Kathryn Stockett’s “The Help,” a black woman compares her skin color with that of a cockroach. To write difference well demands empathy, an ability to respect the humanity of those you mean to represent. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01GAY/01gay-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 875, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01GAY/01gay-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 875}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01GAY/01gay-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 703, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01GAY/01gay-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1405, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01GAY/01GAY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01GAY/01GAY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01GAY/01GAY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Sacrifice,’ by Joyce Carol Oates', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Warning Signs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gay, Roxane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oates, Joyce Carol', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:35:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roxane Gay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roxane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c39b0f94-fb7c-5bcb-b338-2944cc8be25b', 'word_count': 1296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c39b0f94-fb7c-5bcb-b338-2944cc8be25b'}, {'abstract': 'Inspired by real events, Scott Blackwood’s novel imagines the aftermath of the murder of three teenage girls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/scott-blackwoods-see-how-small.html', 'snippet': 'Inspired by real events, Scott Blackwood’s novel imagines the aftermath of the murder of three teenage girls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the busy corner of Rockwood and West Anderson Lanes in north Austin, Tex., stands a beige-bricked strip mall. At first glance, nothing about it is remarkable. The usual suspects — Jimmy John’s, a pet grooming shop — line the row of one-story facades. Near one end, Classy Nails Spa is wedged between a hair salon and a furniture showroom. In a bed of white granite rocks shaded by an oak, a bronze plaque commemorates the lives of four young women — Amy Ayers, Eliza Thomas, and Sarah and Jennifer Harbison. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scott Blackwood’s ‘See How Small’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ghost Town', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blackwood, Scott', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:36:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By S. Kirk Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'S.', 'middlename': 'Kirk', 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a12b8e7-878e-5876-916e-2ccccdd68d53', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a12b8e7-878e-5876-916e-2ccccdd68d53'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to the science of obsessive-compulsive disorder, offered by a sufferer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/david-adams-the-man-who-couldnt-stop.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to the science of obsessive-compulsive disorder, offered by a sufferer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One cold Northern England evening in November 1990, when David Adam was a first-year college student in Leeds, he enjoyed an encouraging first date with a young woman that culminated in a bit of physical intimacy ending somewhere short of intercourse. The next day when a friend asked Adam if he had sex with the woman, he lied in the traditional manner of boastful, insecure young men everywhere and said that he had, without a condom. “You could have AIDS,” the friend said. That wasn’t true — or was, at most, exceedingly unlikely. Nevertheless, the idea lodged in Adam’s mind, where it set about establishing permanent residency. By the following summer, the fear that he might already have AIDS, or would someday contract it, had hijacked his brain. The “snowflake” of a single intrusive thought, as he puts it, had become a “blizzard” that “blew the snow into every corner of my mind, and laid down a blanket that muffled every surface.” For the next 20 years, Adam was consumed by the fear that he had contracted, or would contract, H.I.V.: He called the National AIDS Helpline so many times daily that the half-dozen staffers there came to recognize his voice; he got blood tests; he compulsively examined and re-examined every red stain he ever came across to determine whether it might be AIDS-tainted blood, and whether it might somehow have contaminated him. He knew this to be irrational — he was a science writer! — yet he couldn’t resist his compulsions. He had developed obsessive-compulsive disorder, or O.C.D.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01STOSSEL/01STOSSEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01STOSSEL/01STOSSEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01STOSSEL/01STOSSEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01STOSSEL/01STOSSEL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 929, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01STOSSEL/01STOSSEL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01STOSSEL/01STOSSEL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01STOSSEL/01STOSSEL-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'David Adam’s ‘The Man Who Couldn’t Stop’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Preoccupied', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adam, David (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Man Who Couldn't Stop: OCD and the True Story of a Life Lost in Thought (Book)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:36:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Stossel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stossel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f50e9e5-7518-5105-bd89-950daa30659c', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f50e9e5-7518-5105-bd89-950daa30659c'}, {'abstract': 'The photographer Reinier Gerritsen captures the reading habits of New York’s subway riders.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/reading-in-transit.html', 'snippet': 'The photographer Reinier Gerritsen captures the reading habits of New York’s subway riders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Peeking at what your fellow passengers are reading is a time-honored sport on the New York subway. The Amsterdam-based photographer Reinier Gerritsen captures his peeks for posterity in “The Last Book,” a collection of candid photos of subterranean readers. Gerritsen comes to New York for two weeks three times a year and spent 13 weeks shooting straphangers for the project. No e-readers were allowed. “I read only printed books,” Gerritsen said via email, though he owned up to having an iPad.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01openbook-promo/01openbook-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 238, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01openbook-promo/01openbook-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 238}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01openbook-promo/01openbook-promo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01openbook-promo/01openbook-promo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01openbook-promo/01openbook-promo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01openbook-promo/01openbook-promo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01openbook-promo/01openbook-promo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reading in Transit', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reading in Transit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:36:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ae10145-f944-54d9-9fa8-64c284316030', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ae10145-f944-54d9-9fa8-64c284316030'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to Shakespeare’s 154 sonnets, and an argument that they contain an underlying narrative.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/ideas-of-order-by-neil-l-rudenstine.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to Shakespeare’s 154 sonnets, and an argument that they contain an underlying narrative.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Time has formed its judgment on the 154 poems that make up what we call “Shakespeare’s sonnets.” Most are known to some and some are known to all. Eight or 10 are probably here for as long as we are, two or three perhaps longer, lighting out for deep space in binary code, beyond praise or burial. Any critical word a living poet might say about them now would necessitate the wearing of a jester’s motley — with first-person “I” as one’s little stick to shake — so excuse me while I don this costume, leaf haplessly hither and thither through all 154 and cry that I would take the least of the plays over the whole blooming thing.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01MAXWELL/01maxwell-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01MAXWELL/01maxwell-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 767}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01MAXWELL/01maxwell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01MAXWELL/01maxwell-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1603, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01MAXWELL/01maxwell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01MAXWELL/01maxwell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/books/review/01MAXWELL/01maxwell-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ideas of Order,’ by Neil L. Rudenstine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eternal Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rudenstine, Neil L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:36:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glyn Maxwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maxwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f5cf0a0-7236-5655-a9c0-51496f20b194', 'word_count': 1195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f5cf0a0-7236-5655-a9c0-51496f20b194'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GHETTOSIDE: A True Story of Murder in America, by Jill Leovy. (Spiegel & Grau, $28.) Focusing on South Los Angeles, Leovy examines the circumstances of the country’s disturbingly high rate of murders of African-American men.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:36:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc424f3f-a1c6-595a-a210-af7141b90682', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc424f3f-a1c6-595a-a210-af7141b90682'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PRISCILLA: The Hidden Life of an Englishwoman in Wartime France,  by Nicholas Shakespeare. (Harper Perennial, $15.99.) Shakespeare knew that Priscilla Thompson, his glamorous but enigmatic aunt, had lived in Vichy France, and family lore even had her jailed by the Gestapo for her work in the Resistance. But long after her death in 1982, the author has pieced together a far more intriguing story: that of a femme fatale who made painful compromises to survive the Nazi occupation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-30T17:37:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', '