ment_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raphael Minder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raphael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Minder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/403b9276-cb70-59f9-9c62-830db4c65536', 'word_count': 573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/403b9276-cb70-59f9-9c62-830db4c65536'}, {'abstract': 'Super Bowl week is following the trend of uniformity in the N.F.L., where players and teams and stadiums have been slowly dulled of personality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/sports/football/super-bowl-2015-at-age-xlix-outsize-event-has-no-sense-of-place.html', 'snippet': 'Super Bowl week is following the trend of uniformity in the N.F.L., where players and teams and stadiums have been slowly dulled of personality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHOENIX — Most of what makes the setting of this Super Bowl different from all the rest is a climbing wall meant to evoke the Grand Canyon. In a downtown parking lot, visible from the raised set of the N.F.L. Network and near a giant light-up cutout of letters reading XLIX, the wall, made of fake rock, has an enormous video screen in the middle that plays commercials. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/01jpbranch/01jpbranch-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/01jpbranch/01jpbranch-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/01jpbranch/01jpbranch-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 690, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jum