: 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-31T18:52:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian McFadden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McFadden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a494fff5-b5cc-5774-81d0-b8d69eaecfb7', 'word_count': 9, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a494fff5-b5cc-5774-81d0-b8d69eaecfb7'}, {'abstract': 'Since its founding in 1959, Nederlands Dans Theater has been remarkably venturesome.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/arts/dance/young-talent-taking-new-steps.html', 'snippet': 'Since its founding in 1959, Nederlands Dans Theater has been remarkably venturesome.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since its founding in 1959, Nederlands Dans Theater has been remarkably venturesome, assembling an impressive contemporary repertory. In 1978, it established Nederlands Dans Theater 2 (NDT2, for short) for dancers between 17 and 22 years old. More than a training ground, NDT2 has a distinct repertory, several pieces from which will be shown in its first season here in five years, including some unusual examples of collaborative choreography. Paul Lightfoot, NDT2’s director, and Sol León, its artistic adviser, teamed up for the highly emotional “Subject to Change” and the eccentric “Shutters Shut,” which unites choreographic play with wordplay as dancers interpret a poem by Gertrude Stein. Working together, the Israeli choreographers Sharon Eyal and Gai Behar created “Sara,” an exploration of memories and dreams. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE4/01GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE4/01GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE4/01GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 706, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE4/01GUIDE4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 964, 'width': 1399, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE4/01GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE4/01GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE4/01GUIDE4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Talent, Taking New Steps', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Young Talent, Taking New Steps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nederlands Dans Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-31T18:53:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b8ac7e3-cb9c-5f9c-b853-80413783993d', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b8ac7e3-cb9c-5f9c-b853-80413783993d'}, {'abstract': 'The Pennsylvania Germans produced the handsome, lavishly embellished documents called fraktur, 200 outstanding examples of which are at the Philadelphia Museum of Art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/arts/design/folk-works-known-as-fraktur.html', 'snippet': 'The Pennsylvania Germans produced the handsome, lavishly embellished documents called fraktur, 200 outstanding examples of which are at the Philadelphia Museum of Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Pennsylvania Germans produced the handsome ceramics known as redware and vibrantly painted furniture. They also made lavishly embellished documents called fraktur, 200 outstanding examples of which are in “Drawn With Spirit: Pennsylvania German Fraktur From the Joan and Victor Johnson Collection” opening on Sunday at the Philadelphia Museum of Art. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Folk Works Known as Fraktur', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Folk Works Known as Fraktur', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Museum of Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-31T18:53:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abe7a464-b8c7-518d-8dd0-d9654fe524cf', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abe7a464-b8c7-518d-8dd0-d9654fe524cf'}, {'abstract': 'An enormous wildfire in August 1910 in the northern Rockies is the focus of an episode of “American Experience.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/arts/television/promethean-fire-smoldering-still.html', 'snippet': 'An enormous wildfire in August 1910 in the northern Rockies is the focus of an episode of “American Experience.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The great fires in American history are generally identified with a city or a building: Chicago in 1871, Boston in 1872, the Iroquois Theater in 1903, the Triangle shirtwaist factory in 1911. But a wildfire in August 1910 in the northern Rockies dwarfed all of those in geographic scope. An episode of “American Experience” on Tuesday on PBS that examines that blaze borrows one of its nicknames for its title: “The Big Burn.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 424, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 424}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 724, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1449, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Promethean Fire, Smoldering Still', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Promethean Fire, Smoldering Still', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-31T18:54:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc004fda-e88d-5abb-b09e-7d923b86d8ad', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc004fda-e88d-5abb-b09e-7d923b86d8ad'}, {'abstract': 'Guillermo Klein, the singer Rebecca Martin, the bassist Larry Grenadier and the drummer Jeff Ballard will play at Rockwood Music Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/arts/music/back-in-town-with-tricky-tunes.html', 'snippet': 'Guillermo Klein, the singer Rebecca Martin, the bassist Larry Grenadier and the drummer Jeff Ballard will play at Rockwood Music Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A composer doesn’t necessarily mean a songwriter, especially in jazz. Guillermo Klein, originally from Argentina, who has written brilliantly for his jazz groups in New York and Barcelona since the mid-’90s, never made a distinction. He creates pieces full of tricky counterpoint and canon, but he also writes emotionally direct, harmonically complex, circular songs to be sung by himself and others, including Liliana Herrero and Luciana Souza. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 770, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1995, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Back in Town With Tricky Tunes', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Back in Town With Tricky Tunes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-01-31T18:56:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/894f45df-f588-51fc-8fe1-ea317a5ca1b9', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/894f45df-f588-51fc-8fe1-ea317a5ca1b9'}, {'abstract': 'A Brooklyn Academy of Music retrospective offers John Carpenter’s 1978 classic “Halloween” on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/movies/its-safe-to-look-back.html', 'snippet': 'A Brooklyn Academy of Music retrospective offers John Carpenter’s 1978 classic “Halloween” on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s amazing what you can do with ingenuity, a microbudget, a butcher knife and a Captain Kirk mask. Take “Halloween,” John Carpenter’s 1978 classic, which unleashed the killer Michael Myers (not to mention Jamie Lee Curtis) on the world. “John Carpenter: Master of Fear,” a Brooklyn Academy of Music retrospective Thursday through Feb. 22, offers “Halloween” on Friday; other screenings include Mr. Carpenter’s tribute to Hong Kong cinema, “Big Trouble in Little China” (1986); his remake of “The Thing” (1982); and his Reagan-era parable “They Live” (1988). ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE1/01GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/arts/01GUIDE1/01GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02